WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.208
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.208 --> 00:00:04.850
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:05.630
for everyday life.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:08.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.760
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.710
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:14.630
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.690
Today, my guest is Dr.
Robin Carhart-Harris.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.180
Dr. Carhart-Harris is a
distinguished professor

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.550
of neurology and psychiatry
at the University

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.660
of California, San Francisco.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.330
He is one of the
leading researchers

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.700
in the field of psychedelics
and how they change

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.440
neural circuitry in the brain.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.810
His laboratory is responsible
for understanding,

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.300
for instance, how
psilocybin, also sometimes

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.670
referred to as magic
mushrooms change

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.400
neural circuitry in the
brain, such that new ideas

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.680
and new forms of learning occur.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.930
His laboratory is also
responsible for carrying out

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.090
various clinical
trials, some of which

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.250
have demonstrated that
appropriate dosages

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:52.250
of psilocybin can alleviate
major depression in more

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.267
than 67% of people
that take the drug.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:57.350
Now, this is not to say
that everybody should take

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.110
psilocybin, and today's
discussion describes both

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.020
the clinical trials and why
treatments with psychedelics

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.730
in some cases work
and in some cases

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.220
do not work in order to
treat major depression.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.668
As well as discussions around
psilocybin, lysogenic acid

00:01:10.668 --> 00:01:12.210
diethylamide,
sometimes also referred

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.060
to as LSD, as well as DMT.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.540
And how these change the brain,
and how those brain changes

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.910
can relate to changes
in mental health,

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.820
as it relates to depression and
other psychiatric challenges,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.890
as well as how psychedelics
are being applied

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.770
in order to change neural
circuitry for sake of expanding

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.910
different aspects
of the human mind,

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:34.150
including creativity,
intelligence, and much more.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.180
During today's discussion
Dr. Carhart-Harris

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.600
teaches us about the history of
the study of psychedelics, as

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.870
well as how the legislature,
that is the laws surrounding

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.330
psychedelics, are evolving
in the United States

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.700
and elsewhere for the
use of psychedelics

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.325
to treat psychiatric challenges.

00:01:49.325 --> 00:01:50.700
By the end of
today's discussion,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.283
you will have a
thorough understanding

00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:53.730
of how psychedelics work.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.740
Both in the short-term during
the actual journey or trip.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.520
In fact, much of my discussion
today with Dr. Carhart-Harris

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.380
talks about the
different aspects

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.210
of the psychedelic
journey and how those

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.978
relate to therapeutic outcomes.

00:02:04.978 --> 00:02:07.020
And, of course, by the
end of today's discussion,

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.090
you will also understand
the long-term effects

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.490
of psychedelics, that
is, how they can actually

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:12.690
rewire the brain.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.430
Before we begin, I'd
like to emphasize

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.950
that this podcast is separate
from my teaching and research

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:17.940
roles at Stanford.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.100
It is, however, part
of my desire and effort

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.080
to bring zero cost to
consumer information

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.880
about science and
science-related tools

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:24.990
to the general public.

