00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.350
And now for my discussion
with Dr. Robin Carhart-Harris.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:34.860
Dr. Carhart-Harris, welcome.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.000
I've been wanting to talk
to you for a long time.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.410
I certainly have known who
you are for quite a while,

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:44.910
because I place you in
this very small but very

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.850
special and important
category of researchers

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.750
who has been pioneering
the use of psychedelics

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.210
for the treatment of
specific clinical conditions,

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.940
and really carrying the
torch for, essentially,

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:57.810
the entire field.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:01.830
So I want to start with
a voice of gratitude

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.710
and say thank you for doing
this incredibly important work.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:08.250
Could you tell us a little bit
about what psychedelics are?

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:10.740
In fact, I'm curious as to
how the name psychedelic ever

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:11.280
came to be.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.363
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Mhm.

00:07:12.363 --> 00:07:15.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And what
you think they potentially

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.040
reveal about the
workings of the brain.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.425
And then we'll talk about
the clinical applications.

00:07:19.425 --> 00:07:20.550
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Sure.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:25.620
Well, even that one
is kind of hot one,

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.660
because opinions differ on
how to define psychedelic.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.910
But perhaps, a
good starting place

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.390
is to start with the etymology,
where did the word come from?

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:42.380
And it was a Brit
excommunicated living

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:48.120
in Canada Humphry Osmond
who was due to present

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:52.620
a paper at a National
Academy of Sciences meeting

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.780
on psychotomimetics--

00:07:54.780 --> 00:08:00.480
drugs that mimic aspects of
psychosis in their action.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.000
And certain drugs
like mescaline--

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:08.310
let's see, 1956-- and
LSD were on the bill.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:12.240
And he felt
dissatisfied with them

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:15.000
being under this category
of psychotomimetics,

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.870
and felt that the signature
psychological effects

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:21.540
of these compounds
went beyond just

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:23.620
mimicking psychotic symptoms.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:27.030
And so he wanted to
find a more apt term

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:30.570
to speak to, in a sense,
the principal component

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.429
of their action.

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:36.990
And he jotted down a few
different possibilities

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:38.970
about a dozen or so, I think.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.490
And one of them was
psychodelic, actually

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:45.690
it started as and ended
up being psychedelic.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:50.790
And he had a correspondence
going on with another Brit

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.870
also living in the US,
Aldous Huxley, where

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.680
they were playing
with some terms

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.900
to refer to these compounds.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.100
And in the end, Osmond
won with psychedelic,

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:11.820
and he had this little ditty of,
to fathom hell or soar angelic,

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.990
just take a pinch
of psychedelic.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:18.510
That's where you put
the disclaimer in.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:21.360
And so what does that mean?

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.460
It's two ancient Greek words
psyche, means the human mind

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:31.650
or if we're being actually
true to the ancient Greek it

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.990
means soul.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:39.660
And then the other component
means to make clear

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:44.430
or to make visible or to
make manifest or to reveal.

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.500
So all of those work.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.900
And it's a neologism.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:50.010
It's a made up word.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:54.030
But it does have that
ancient Greek origin.

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:57.810
And it's speaking
to this principle

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:02.430
that these compounds reveal
aspects of the psyche,

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:06.930
of the human mind, the soul
that are ordinarily not

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.940
entirely visible.

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:12.820
And so that's the etymology,
and it's wonderfully poetic.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:15.850
But I happen to think
it's also very accurate.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:19.615
It's a useful term,
because it's sort of,

00:10:19.615 --> 00:10:22.330
you might say
valence nonspecific.

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:24.730
It doesn't say, you're
going to have a great time

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.220
or that you're going to go mad.

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:30.970
It's more that-- it reveals a
psyche, and it could be hellish

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:34.420
but it could be heavenly.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:39.340
So that's the etymology and
also a bit of the psychology.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:42.970
And sort of pointing
to the phenomenology,

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.010
the subjective experience.

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:48.460
But there's also a
pharmacology here.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:53.590
And quite recently, there was
put out a consensus statement

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.980
about psychedelics
that's really referring

00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:00.820
to what we call the classic
psychedelics to say that these

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:04.750
are all compounds that work
on a particular receptor

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:07.970
in the brain, the
serotonin 2A receptor.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:12.110
And that's another way that we
could define these compounds.

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:15.950
I said this one's a little
hot, because I'm of the view

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:19.640
that while the pharmacology
is really useful, how

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.800
the drugs work chemically, you
can't avoid the phenomenology.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:26.600
And if we're true
to the etymology,

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:30.620
where the term came from,
then we must recognize,

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:34.465
and we cannot neglect the
subjective experience.

00:11:34.465 --> 00:11:37.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Thank you
for that beautiful description

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:40.610
of what brought us
to today in terms

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:42.127
of using the word psychedelics.

00:11:42.127 --> 00:11:43.835
And now, it's thrown
around all the time.

00:11:43.835 --> 00:11:44.480
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:44.840
Too much.

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:45.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:45.880
Too much.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.320
And I'm guessing--
well, not guessing.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.950
I'm certain that it's also used
to describe many compounds that

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:56.790
don't touch the 5-HT2A,
the serotonin 2A receptor.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:00.960
So there is a broader
categorization by most people.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.590
And it'll be interesting to
see where all the nomenclature

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:04.850
and naming goes.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:07.850
For the time being, I'd
love for you to tell us

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:11.930
a bit more about this idea
that psychedelics, however

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:15.920
one defines them, can reveal
something about the mind that

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:17.570
can't be revealed otherwise.

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:20.330
Are you talking about
the subconscious?

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:24.500
I mean, psychologists and most
famously, Freud but also Jung

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:28.940
and also neuroscientists, I
think, think about subconscious

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:29.690
processing.

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:31.490
I think, perhaps, the
most salient example

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:35.670
for me that's outside the
realm of anything psychedelic

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:37.200
would be blindsight.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.268
This phenomenon that you take
people that are blind but still

00:12:41.268 --> 00:12:42.810
have some connectivity
in their brain

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.450
and you present
them a board with--

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:47.610
or a computer screen with
different number of dots

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:48.960
on each side and you
say how many dots

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:51.330
are on each side of the screen,
and they say, what do you mean?

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:52.110
I can't see the screen.

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:52.610
I'm blind.

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:53.880
And you say, well, just guess.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:58.020
And their guess rate
is accurate far more

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:00.047
than chance would predict.

00:13:00.047 --> 00:13:00.185
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Mhm.

00:13:00.185 --> 00:13:02.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So they
have so-called blindsight,

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.710
and people have said, well,
this is the subconscious

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:06.450
revealing itself.

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:08.400
There's no psychedelic
drug involved.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:13.530
But what you're describing is
a pharmacologic-induced state

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:17.850
that reveals something that
normally should we assume is

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:22.230
masked or that we
are oblivious to--

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:24.510
even though it's
expressing itself.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:26.190
What does it mean
for these drugs

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.850
to be revealing something about
the workings of the mind that

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:32.270
would not be obvious
to us, otherwise.

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:33.480
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:38.280
So the example of blindsight is
interesting but it's different.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:43.380
Blindsight would be referring to
non-conscious processing, maybe

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:44.820
implicit processing.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:48.090
So stuff going on in
the mind in perception

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:50.160
in a sense that is
below the threshold

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:51.580
of conscious awareness.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:53.770
But yet is influencing you.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:57.720
So it's sort-- it's kind of
related, but it's different.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:01.890
So in depth, psychology,
psychoanalysis, psychodynamic,

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:06.870
psychology, Sigmund Freud,
Carl Jung, and so on.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.690
We talk about the unconscious.

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:14.430
And there, it's more
about the kind of blood

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:19.410
and guts of the human
condition, the human nature--

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.420
both the personal unconscious.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.610
So things that
you might not want

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:27.430
to necessarily be conscious
of, because it's painful.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:30.722
So that's the repression
aspect pushing it out

00:14:30.722 --> 00:14:31.680
of conscious awareness.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:33.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Repressed
memories in particular?

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:34.605
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:14:34.605 --> 00:14:39.440
Like traumatic memories,
difficult relationships.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.900
It could be complex
trauma, not necessarily

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:47.600
just a specific index trauma,
but a series of trauma.

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:49.830
And then you have the
collective unconscious,

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:52.520
which was really Carl
Jung's contribution

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:58.340
to say that there's a
transpersonal quality

00:14:58.340 --> 00:14:59.840
to the unconscious.

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:05.870
There's aspects about humans,
not just this individual human.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:08.900
There's aspects to our
minds, our psyches that

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:13.170
are not fully available
to conscious awareness,

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:16.100
but can come up
in certain states.

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:20.300
Psychoanalysis went crazy for
dreaming as their royal road

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:22.490
to a knowledge of
the unconscious.

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:23.930
That was Freud.

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:29.480
But we now with
psychedelics, and this

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.190
was what drew me into the area.

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:34.400
Was discovering
literature that was

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:39.090
speaking to this particular
action, the psychedelic action.

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:42.750
And was saying that
when these drugs--

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:48.210
like LSD, psilocybin
found in magic mushrooms,

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:52.170
when they're used
in psychotherapy,

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:59.250
material comes up that may be
may have been repressed that

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:04.620
is of therapeutic value
and awareness and insight

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:06.660
of this material
seems to catalyze

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:10.560
the therapeutic process with
strong emotional release.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:15.720
These cathartic
experiences and insights.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:19.650
Whether they're insights
that are personal

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.800
or whether they're
transpersonal.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:27.360
But for me, this is really
where the meat of it

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:30.810
is with psychedelics and classic
psychedelics, in particular.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:35.250
The likes of compounds
like LSD and psilocybin.

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:38.580
I would say that if it
wasn't for this action

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:41.790
by classic psychedelics,
we wouldn't be

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:44.520
so interested in psychedelics.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:48.900
I think, if we only had
compounds like ketamine, MDMA

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:52.770
cannabis, that could be
said, broadly speaking,

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:54.990
to be psychedelic-like.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:56.700
I don't think it
necessarily would

00:16:56.700 --> 00:17:00.900
have captured the world's
attention as psychedelics

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:02.500
are right now.

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:06.180
I actually think it's
a major gap to fill.

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:10.749
Is this principal action of
the classic psychedelics.

00:17:10.749 --> 00:17:13.290
What does this mean
that I'm referring to?

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:14.640
Psyche-revealing.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:16.109
What is that?

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:19.380
And I suppose where
I'm going with this

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:22.930
is, what is that in terms
of the biology as well?

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:25.260
What's going on in
the brain and the body

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:28.319
when people become
aware of things

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:31.917
that previously they
weren't fully aware of.

00:17:31.917 --> 00:17:33.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'd
like to talk about some

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.780
of the clinical trials that
you've been involved with.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:40.050
In particular,
looking at psilocybin.

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:44.760
As you mentioned, the principal
hallucinatory psychedelic agent

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:47.040
in magic mushrooms.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:50.100
I'd like to start with a kind
of nuts and bolts question

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:53.580
just so that everyone's
on the same page.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.097
I've read the papers
that you've published

00:17:56.097 --> 00:17:57.930
and that others have
published in this area.

00:17:57.930 --> 00:18:01.560
And typically, the dosages
used in these trials

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:05.660
are 25 milligrams of psilocybin.

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:07.670
And we talk about
one recent trial,

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:10.760
in particular, that
compared 25 to 10 milligrams

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:13.490
to more frequent use
of very small amounts.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:16.980
1 milligram over 3
weeks, for instance.

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:21.210
However, when people talk
about magic mushrooms,

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:24.750
they often talk about gram
doses of the mushroom,

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:27.780
because I'm assuming that
they contain milligram

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:30.270
dosages of psilocybin.

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:33.247
Here, we're not encouraging
use of any kind.

00:18:33.247 --> 00:18:34.330
These are clinical trials.

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:38.760
But for clarity of
understanding, what

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:40.410
is the conversion, typically?

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:43.350
Like 1 gram of
magic mushrooms will

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:48.060
contain how many milligrams
of psilocybin on average.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:50.040
Because what I'm
trying to do here

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:53.610
is calibrate people to
this idea of microdosing

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:55.380
versus macrodosing.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:57.000
And that's fairly
straightforward

00:18:57.000 --> 00:18:59.110
to do with respect to
the clinical trials.

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:02.310
But then in a lot of the
lay discussion around this,

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:05.610
you hear about heroic
doses versus microdoses.

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:07.510
And so I think there's
a lot of confusion.

00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:12.300
So if you would,
educate us on this idea

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:13.980
of what's a microdose.

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:18.970
And perhaps, also how many
milligrams of psilocybin

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:22.495
are contained in a gram of quote
unquote, "magic mushrooms?"

00:19:22.495 --> 00:19:23.620
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Sure.

00:19:23.620 --> 00:19:26.990
Well, a microdose is
neither of these are that.

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:27.490
Simple.

00:19:27.490 --> 00:19:29.230
But they're fun.

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:30.370
It's a fun challenge.

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:34.480
But microdose, one
definition is that it's

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:39.400
a dose of typically a
classic psychedelic like LSD

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:46.660
or psilocybin that has some
perceptible psychedelic

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:47.830
effects.

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:51.280
It doesn't put you into a
noticeable altered state

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:55.480
of consciousness that
feels like you're tripping.

00:19:55.480 --> 00:20:00.010
And if that was LSD, it
looks as though the threshold

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:05.713
is around about, let's
see, 10, 11, 12 micrograms.

00:20:05.713 --> 00:20:06.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Micrograms.

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:07.495
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Micrograms.

00:20:07.495 --> 00:20:08.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Want
to be very clear here.

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:09.390
Micrograms.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:10.930
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:13.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
10 micrograms of LSD,

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:17.030
are you saying will not induce
visual hallucinations in most

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:17.530
people?

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:20.810
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: So
that's threshold level.

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:24.940
That's about the level that
some people who are sensitive

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:27.140
could feel it.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:31.090
But if you were to talk
to the microdosing gurus,

00:20:31.090 --> 00:20:36.400
they might say that that's
the ballpark for an LSD dose

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.920
that you would
consider a micro dose,

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:42.460
and then you would
take semi-regularly.

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:44.380
It's typically
something like one day

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:47.920
on, one day off or one
day on, two days off.

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:49.280
This kind of thing.

00:20:49.280 --> 00:20:51.070
There's different protocols.

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:53.440
And yeah.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:57.310
So some like Jim Fadiman--

00:20:57.310 --> 00:21:01.570
one of the popularizers
of microdosing, I think

00:21:01.570 --> 00:21:06.370
would say that a true microdose
should be sub-perceptible.

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:09.700
You shouldn't feel
it, yet the assumption

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:13.300
is it's going to change you
in some way on a kind of trait

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:18.490
level more than a state
level, and maybe behaviorally.

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:23.470
And the typical story goes,
it will improve well-being.

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:26.200
And maybe, maybe,
it could improve

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:28.450
certain aspects
of cognition, say,

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.950
related to creative thinking.

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:34.120
I emphasize the maybe
there because that's

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:35.920
another angle with microdosing.

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:40.450
We're waiting for some
compelling evidence.

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:42.400
As things stand
right now, I'd say,

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:45.170
we lack that
compelling evidence.

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:49.120
There's some suggestive stuff,
but often the study designs

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:50.530
aren't that strong.

00:21:50.530 --> 00:21:53.290
It's really hard to do a
study with microdosing,

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:56.950
because you need to have
permission to give people

00:21:56.950 --> 00:22:01.300
a microdose that for
practical reasons,

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:08.800
they would go home with,
otherwise, you're requiring

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:13.090
them to be in the lab, say
three times a week for x number

00:22:13.090 --> 00:22:18.010
of weeks to meet the criteria
of a course of microdosing,

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:22.580
which might be two or three
times a week for say, a month.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:26.140
And that's a hard thing
to do in a lab study.

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:27.880
It's expensive.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:32.110
You'd need to do that
against a suitable control,

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:33.910
so a placebo control.

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:37.000
And there is a study
that's been done

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:41.200
in New Zealand that has some
interesting preliminary data

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:42.130
that did--

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:46.180
I think did the design right.

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:49.560
But it hasn't been
published yet.

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:53.170
I've seen some positive
findings presented

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:55.270
around improvements in mood.

00:22:55.270 --> 00:22:59.840
But it's a bit early to
get too excited about that.

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:03.430
It needs to go through
peer review and all that.

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:08.170
But as things stand, the
evidence is pretty thin.

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:10.720
And we have to be
honest about that.

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:16.450
We did quite a creative study
with my colleagues at Imperial.

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:21.230
The guy leading that, Balazs
Szigeti, a Hungarian chap,

00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:23.270
did a really creative design.

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:24.890
Very much his brainchild.

00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:28.910
He instructed people to
do their own blinding,

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:31.580
their own
placebo-controlled blinding

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:33.590
of their own microdosing.

00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:36.290
So this was a classic
citizen science study

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:39.800
like do it yourself science
where they would get their LSD

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:43.250
tabs and chop them up, put
them into gel capsules,

00:23:43.250 --> 00:23:47.570
opaque, and have other capsules
that are the placebos that they

00:23:47.570 --> 00:23:49.940
just close empty capsule.

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:53.570
And then there was a whole
barcode scan technique

00:23:53.570 --> 00:23:56.900
so that you kind
of shuffle them up.

00:23:56.900 --> 00:24:00.080
But they've got the
barcode in, the QR code.

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:02.510
So you can break
the code later on,

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:03.890
but once you've
shuffled them up,

00:24:03.890 --> 00:24:07.933
you no longer know which
ones had the microdosing

00:24:07.933 --> 00:24:08.975
and which ones are empty.

00:24:08.975 --> 00:24:10.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Was this LSD?

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:11.330
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
This was LSD.

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:13.280
Also tried it with
mushrooms, but the issues

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:16.340
with the mushrooms was
people would burp sometimes.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:17.030
They'd belch.

00:24:17.030 --> 00:24:19.740
And then they'd have
this mushroom taste.

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:22.080
So then he instructed
people to get

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:26.620
some non-psychoactive
mushroom material to put in.

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:27.300
So it's really--

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:28.365
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Not an easy study.

00:24:28.365 --> 00:24:30.115
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
--not an easy study.

00:24:30.115 --> 00:24:33.690
And it was-- I love
that kind of science.

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:37.440
Real creative first
mover kind of science.

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:39.120
And the results
were fascinating,

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:44.550
because the short story is
that the microdosing didn't

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:46.278
compellingly beat the placebo.

00:24:46.278 --> 00:24:47.445
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It did not.

00:24:47.445 --> 00:24:48.778
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: It didn't.

00:24:48.778 --> 00:24:51.870
And he controlled,
because he asked.

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:54.970
He controlled for expectancy.

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:57.960
So people's positive expectancy,
which is, in a sense,

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:00.690
the vehicle that carries
the placebo response.

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:02.400
It's why you have a placebo.

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:06.390
Is that positive expectancy
can drive a therapeutic effect

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:09.090
to a large extent.

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:12.300
So you measured that
pre-trial and then used

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:14.910
it to correct for the response.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:16.500
And how did it work?

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:20.510
Those who got a
placebo but thought

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:24.020
they got a microdose
did as well as those

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:27.380
who thought they got a microdose
and did get a microdose.

00:25:27.380 --> 00:25:30.210
So it was the bigger effect--

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:33.560
the majority of the
effect was in thinking

00:25:33.560 --> 00:25:35.180
that you got a microdose.

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:38.810
So in a sense, it was a victory
for the power of the placebo

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:39.680
response.

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:42.530
And it's created all
sorts of controversy.

00:25:42.530 --> 00:25:44.880
People don't want to believe
it, that kind of thing.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:46.672
But that's the beauty
of science, isn't it?

00:25:46.672 --> 00:25:50.510
That science is not about
what you want to believe.

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:54.092
That right there is
the beauty of science.

00:25:54.092 --> 00:25:56.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
love that experiment.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:58.720
Kudos to them.

00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:01.770
I'm not going to attempt to
say his last name correctly.

00:26:01.770 --> 00:26:03.040
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: I tried.

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:03.400
Yeah.

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:04.300
Probably you made a mess of it--

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:04.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:05.230
No.

00:26:05.230 --> 00:26:08.280
I think you got it.

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:10.740
You were involved in
a clinical trial that

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:12.930
was published last
year comparing

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:19.380
25 milligrams of psilocybin
to 10 milligrams of psilocybin

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:23.295
to a drug called escitalopram.

00:26:23.295 --> 00:26:24.420
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:25.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:25.830
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Lexapro.

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:25.920
Yeah.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:26.325
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Lexapro?

00:26:26.325 --> 00:26:27.408
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Mhm.

00:26:27.408 --> 00:26:33.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And this 1
milligram over a 3-week dosage.

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:36.150
I'm wanting to discuss the
results of that study a bit

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:37.890
and some of the other
trials that you've

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:40.350
done involving psilocybin
for depression,

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:42.300
the treatment of depression.

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:43.650
Could we calibrate ourselves?

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:47.820
25 milligrams of
psilocybin, is that what--

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:49.800
it's going to be a
perceptible dose presumably.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:51.270
Hallucinations and all that.

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:53.970
And is that what
one would find in--

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:55.890
I'm guessing here,
if I'm accurate,

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:58.170
this does not mean that
I have experience here.

00:26:58.170 --> 00:27:00.335
But 2 grams of mushrooms?

00:27:00.335 --> 00:27:02.460
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: It's
more than that we think.

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:02.850
Yeah.

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:03.360
Sorry.

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:04.155
I missed that--

00:27:04.155 --> 00:27:04.785
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No, no.

00:27:04.785 --> 00:27:05.910
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
I missed that one.

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:06.990
Went off on a tangent.

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:08.410
But yeah.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:11.680
25 milligrams of
psilocybin would be--

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:13.783
we don't know,
and it's important

00:27:13.783 --> 00:27:15.700
that I say that because
I wouldn't want people

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:18.760
to hear my answer
here, and then use

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:21.070
it to calibrate their
own dosing of mushrooms

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:22.510
and get it way off.

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:24.100
So it's guesswork.

00:27:24.100 --> 00:27:27.340
And I would love to see someone
do a proper study on it.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:32.830
And look at the
psilocybin content

00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:38.410
in a given mass of psilocybin
mushrooms, magic mushrooms.

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:42.760
But to my knowledge, that
hasn't really been done.

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:47.150
Someone like Paul Stamets would
give a better answer here.

00:27:47.150 --> 00:27:51.790
But I think the percentage
within the mushroom mass is--

00:27:51.790 --> 00:27:55.150
of psilocybin in the
mushroom mass and psilocin

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:58.000
which is the metabolite
of psilocybin

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:03.947
is something in the 1%, a
little bit higher, maybe, range.

00:28:03.947 --> 00:28:04.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:09.730
So 1 gram, 1000 milligrams
of magic mushroom

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:14.052
would contain about 10
milligrams of psilocybin.

00:28:14.052 --> 00:28:14.635
Is that right?

00:28:14.635 --> 00:28:15.970
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Broadly speaking, yeah.

00:28:15.970 --> 00:28:16.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:16.710
Great.

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:18.340
That helps calibrate.

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:21.850
And I think, again, just
allows the layperson

00:28:21.850 --> 00:28:23.620
to understand a
bit more of where

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:25.660
we're headed with
these psilocybin

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:27.310
trials and the results.

00:28:27.310 --> 00:28:29.710
So we don't have to
restrict our discussion

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:31.220
to just that I clinical trial.

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:33.925
But if we include that
one and compare it

00:28:33.925 --> 00:28:35.800
to some of the other
trials that you've done,

00:28:35.800 --> 00:28:39.640
I mean, your laboratory
is seeing phenomenal--

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:41.800
in my opinion,
phenomenal results

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:46.030
in the treatment of, otherwise,
intractable depression,

00:28:46.030 --> 00:28:49.615
major depression, which so
many people suffer from.

00:28:49.615 --> 00:28:55.690
From two-- I suppose
there two sessions

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:59.552
of using psilocybin in these
ranges of 10 to 25 milligrams.

00:28:59.552 --> 00:29:00.510
Do I have that correct?

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:01.170
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yes.

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:02.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:29:02.320 --> 00:29:05.410
Could we talk a little bit
about what people typically

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:08.880
experience during
those sessions that

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:12.300
allows this phenomenal
transformation of mood

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:15.570
and state and trait as well?

00:29:15.570 --> 00:29:19.740
And I'm especially
interested in whether or not

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:23.370
it is the experience
during those sessions that

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:27.780
is the trigger that's necessary
for the transformation

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.150
from a depressed to a
non-depressed state.

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:33.090
Because the impulse
is to think it is.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:36.540
That what one thinks and
sees and hallucinates is--

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:37.980
and hears is so vital.

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:42.030
But of course, these drugs can
create neuroplasticity changes

00:29:42.030 --> 00:29:45.340
in our neural wiring, presumably
for long periods of time.

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:48.840
So what are your thoughts
on the experience itself?

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:51.638
And maybe for those who have
not done these compounds before,

00:29:51.638 --> 00:29:53.430
you could explain a
little bit about what's

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.770
typical for people,
and what you think

00:29:55.770 --> 00:30:00.030
is leading to that incredible
positive and pervasive change

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:02.440
in mood, state, and trait.

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:05.010
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: I would
say that it's more than impulse

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:09.070
that is leading us to think that
the experience is important.

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:13.960
It's really data and
converging evidence now.

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:18.220
So independent teams,
independent studies

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:24.130
are converging on the magnitude
of certain kinds of experience

00:30:24.130 --> 00:30:27.250
rated, yes, with
subjective rating scales

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:30.410
is predicting
therapeutic outcomes.

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:34.990
Pretty strongly
and very reliably.

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:36.850
And so that's guiding us.

00:30:36.850 --> 00:30:39.250
Now, could you say, well,
maybe those experiences

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:44.050
are some kind of epiphenomenon
of say, a central brain action?

00:30:44.050 --> 00:30:45.130
Well, absolutely.

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:47.740
But then all experience
are epiphenomenon

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:48.850
by that principle.

00:30:48.850 --> 00:30:50.950
And yet we care
about it, you know?

00:30:50.950 --> 00:30:55.180
And it matters to us and
in our human relations

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:56.450
with each other.

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:59.080
So I think it does
matter to a human being

00:30:59.080 --> 00:31:02.530
when they're in say, a
psilocybin therapy session,

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:05.680
and as the drug effects
begin to come on

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:10.680
and the body starts to feel
a little strange and tingly

00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:16.260
and there's some
initial anxiety.

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:18.960
And then in their
mind's eye, they

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:22.920
start to notice patterns
and maybe colors

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:26.670
and then, maybe, those patterns
deepen and they're dynamic

00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:29.370
and they have this
fascinating organic quality.

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:30.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Are they--

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:34.302
patients in your studies
typically using an eye mask?

00:31:34.302 --> 00:31:35.385
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yes.

00:31:35.385 --> 00:31:36.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
they're in the eye mask?

00:31:36.510 --> 00:31:37.110
So eyes closed?

00:31:37.110 --> 00:31:37.425
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:31:37.425 --> 00:31:39.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's why
you said mind's eye as opposed

00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:41.460
to looking out into
the clinical setting.

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:41.737
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yes.

00:31:41.737 --> 00:31:43.500
And that's one of
the major differences

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:46.500
to psychedelic therapy
versus taking a psychedelic.

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:48.570
Is you shut your eyes.

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:52.830
And it's a world away
from taking a psychedelic.

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:53.505
Yeah.

00:31:53.505 --> 00:31:55.470
A rave or something.

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:58.180
In a sense, good luck with that.

00:31:58.180 --> 00:32:01.350
But in psychedelic
therapy, yeah.

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:05.040
It's settled conditions.

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:06.390
There's music playing.

00:32:06.390 --> 00:32:10.435
And what I'm describing here
is very much the default.

00:32:10.435 --> 00:32:14.110
There's actually very
little variability

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:15.970
between the different
sites that have done

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:19.120
this work on these conditions.

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:22.840
Typically, it's two people.

00:32:22.840 --> 00:32:25.960
Ideally, mental health
professionals, at least one

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:28.780
who's a psychiatrist or
a clinical psychologist

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:31.990
or some other kind of
psychotherapist or psychiatric

00:32:31.990 --> 00:32:33.850
nurse.

00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:37.090
But ideally, two who
meet those criteria

00:32:37.090 --> 00:32:43.090
with a individual who's ingested
the drug and music playing

00:32:43.090 --> 00:32:44.350
throughout.

00:32:44.350 --> 00:32:47.410
A kind of runway into taking
the drug and then throughout,

00:32:47.410 --> 00:32:48.570
so there's continuity.

00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:51.505
ANDREW HUBERMAN: They're music
with lyrics or without lyrics?

00:32:51.505 --> 00:32:53.672
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Without
lyrics, to begin with,

00:32:53.672 --> 00:32:59.620
and the music typically
is spacious to begin with.

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:05.470
And then builds and
becomes atmospheric.

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:07.990
There might be, I don't
know, some tribal drums

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:12.310
in the distance or
something as it develops

00:33:12.310 --> 00:33:15.580
or like the sound of a
bird in the distance.

00:33:15.580 --> 00:33:17.470
A call.

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:23.470
And then as it gets into
more stronger drug effects,

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:29.740
the music starts
to coax emotion,

00:33:29.740 --> 00:33:34.657
and very intentionally, strings,
for example, would come in

00:33:34.657 --> 00:33:40.660
and it would be an
interesting experiment and one

00:33:40.660 --> 00:33:44.260
that we'd love to do actually
to see whether if you were

00:33:44.260 --> 00:33:48.160
to pull that out, whether the
psychedelic experience would

00:33:48.160 --> 00:33:53.770
be as emotionally intense as
it is in psychedelic therapy

00:33:53.770 --> 00:33:55.960
when you have music
there as a default.

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:58.870
And across the board, people
should find this remarkable,

00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:00.280
because it kind of is.

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:02.110
All of the published
studies that

00:34:02.110 --> 00:34:08.230
are now having such an impact
on psychiatry and beyond

00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:12.250
have music there as
a staple component.

00:34:12.250 --> 00:34:15.880
And we just take it as
assumption that it needs to be.

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:18.290
I tend to share that assumption.

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:21.400
But it's remarkable that it
hasn't been tested properly.

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:22.270
But it's there.

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:24.440
And if you were
to run with that,

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:29.270
and if you had a critical
agenda, you would say,

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:32.560
well, this is music therapy.

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:35.290
Why are you making all this
fuss about psychedelics

00:34:35.290 --> 00:34:38.770
when it's music that's there
in all of these trials with all

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:40.130
these fantastic findings.

00:34:40.130 --> 00:34:42.289
So there is something to that.

00:34:42.289 --> 00:34:45.190
And this will tee me
up probably to talk

00:34:45.190 --> 00:34:50.440
about psychedelic-therapy
being a combination treatment.

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:52.270
We have a hyphen
between the two,

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:57.430
because I share the hypothesis.

00:34:57.430 --> 00:35:00.130
The assumption that
should be tested better.

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:02.470
There is a positive
interaction between the two.

00:35:02.470 --> 00:35:04.915
That there's a synergy
between the two.

00:35:04.915 --> 00:35:07.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's why
it's psychedelic-therapy

00:35:07.570 --> 00:35:10.195
with a hyphen, just
like Carhart-Harris.

00:35:10.195 --> 00:35:11.550
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: OK.

