00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:52.590
we were having about
the unconscious

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:54.840
or about psychedelics
revealing something that's

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:59.400
there all the time but that
we don't have access to.

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.130
And again, I'm struggling
to find the right language

00:38:02.130 --> 00:38:03.780
for this, because
we don't really

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:07.140
have a neural mechanism
like top-down inhibition

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:08.910
or something like
that to explain how

00:38:08.910 --> 00:38:12.270
this unconscious
might be uncorked

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:13.650
in the psychedelic experience.

00:38:13.650 --> 00:38:17.070
But to make it quite simple and
direct, how important do you

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:20.410
think it really
is for the patient

00:38:20.410 --> 00:38:23.650
to feel like they are,
quote unquote, "letting go"

00:38:23.650 --> 00:38:26.230
and what in the
world is "letting go"

00:38:26.230 --> 00:38:27.250
in biological terms?

00:38:27.250 --> 00:38:27.790
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:28.670
Yeah.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:30.520
Well, I think we'll
get there in terms

00:38:30.520 --> 00:38:35.440
of having the neural
correlates of the mind

00:38:35.440 --> 00:38:37.580
revealing itself to itself.

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:43.630
The emergence of the
unconscious into consciousness

00:38:43.630 --> 00:38:48.050
or unconscious material
into conscious awareness.

00:38:48.050 --> 00:38:49.700
It's a wonderful challenge.

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:53.860
It's a huge challenge, but
it's a challenge to embrace.

00:38:53.860 --> 00:38:57.580
And letting go very
much is, again,

00:38:57.580 --> 00:39:01.930
a staple component of
how the different teams

00:39:01.930 --> 00:39:06.490
do this work in terms of
encouraging a willingness

00:39:06.490 --> 00:39:07.960
to let go.

00:39:07.960 --> 00:39:11.260
And when we started out
doing our depression work

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:14.490
and did that first trial,
it was the first trial

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:21.580
of psychedelic in a formally
diagnosed depression, you know?

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:23.620
Where that was the
target population.

00:39:23.620 --> 00:39:25.300
A depressed population.

00:39:25.300 --> 00:39:27.250
It was the first modern
study to do that.

00:39:27.250 --> 00:39:33.520
And we visited
Hopkins, friends there

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:40.630
and were mentored on how to do
the guiding Bill Richards, Mary

00:39:40.630 --> 00:39:42.010
Cusumano.

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:52.270
They were just so brilliant and
wise in their guidance to us

00:39:52.270 --> 00:39:55.610
as how to do the
guiding in our trial.

00:39:55.610 --> 00:40:01.570
And so this phrase of trust, let
go, be open, you'll hear a lot.

00:40:01.570 --> 00:40:06.040
I don't know who fairly it
should be attributed to,

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:10.820
but I would attribute
it to Bill Richards.

00:40:10.820 --> 00:40:11.320
Yeah.

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:12.430
Everything's borrowed.

00:40:12.430 --> 00:40:14.350
You probably got it
from someone else.

00:40:14.350 --> 00:40:16.120
But it's such a key principle.

00:40:16.120 --> 00:40:19.210
And it's almost like
a mantra that you're

00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:20.750
trying to instill in people.

00:40:20.750 --> 00:40:24.260
Trust, let go, be open.

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:27.260
And those different
components where the trust

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:30.830
is about the therapeutic
rapport that, again, this

00:40:30.830 --> 00:40:33.590
goes beyond just intuition now.

00:40:33.590 --> 00:40:36.440
We formerly measured
therapeutic rapport.

00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:41.690
We do it even with just a single
item, a visual analog scale

00:40:41.690 --> 00:40:46.940
item, a subjective rating scale
item on the morning of dosing,

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:51.080
and we find that it's
a significant predictor

00:40:51.080 --> 00:40:53.330
of the quality of the
experience that you

00:40:53.330 --> 00:40:56.780
have under the drug in
the psychedelic therapy,

00:40:56.780 --> 00:41:00.770
and then the therapeutic
outcomes x weeks or months

00:41:00.770 --> 00:41:02.330
later.

00:41:02.330 --> 00:41:07.310
So very powerful chain
of predictive components

00:41:07.310 --> 00:41:11.240
there, but trust,
essentially, important.

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:14.960
And again, not just to intuition
but the data pointing to that.

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:21.020
Let go, there's a readiness
to surrender, to let go,

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:22.400
to not resist.

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:25.100
And we do measure that
too and see that it's

00:41:25.100 --> 00:41:27.800
predictive of response.

00:41:27.800 --> 00:41:34.550
And then the being open is
about a willingness to go there,

00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:38.600
to confront, to be inquisitive.

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:41.100
Something that's
easier said than done.

00:41:41.100 --> 00:41:43.952
Can be terrifying.

00:41:43.952 --> 00:41:48.470
When you're dealing with a
very vulnerable population,

00:41:48.470 --> 00:41:50.690
it's probably more--

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:53.270
the rule then, the
exception that they're

00:41:53.270 --> 00:41:56.720
carrying some significant
adversity, life adversity

00:41:56.720 --> 00:41:59.990
or frank trauma that
they've suffered.

00:41:59.990 --> 00:42:04.760
And so that message of be
open, be willing to confront

00:42:04.760 --> 00:42:09.530
and to go there is
really powerful.

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:12.380
And that's how it plays out.

00:42:12.380 --> 00:42:14.360
And often, there is struggle.

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:16.520
There's something
going on that is I

00:42:16.520 --> 00:42:18.470
don't want to be feeling this.

00:42:18.470 --> 00:42:19.910
Make it stop.

00:42:19.910 --> 00:42:22.010
That can be
nightmarish at times,

00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:23.990
but it's very, very strong.

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:29.010
And with these big doses that
we give, it's very strong.

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:33.420
And actually, a
student that I've

00:42:33.420 --> 00:42:39.960
worked with, I think now
doing a PhD, at Harry Brouwer

00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:43.620
is working on a fantastic
project characterizing

00:42:43.620 --> 00:42:46.080
the different phases of
the psychedelic experience

00:42:46.080 --> 00:42:48.810
where the early
phase is dominated

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:56.040
by negative emotions and
negatively-valenced feelings

00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:57.540
of anxiety and struggle.

00:42:57.540 --> 00:43:00.293
And then it's a different
story in the latter half.

00:43:00.293 --> 00:43:01.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Could
I ask about that?

00:43:01.960 --> 00:43:04.210
First of all, I think that's
fascinating and important

00:43:04.210 --> 00:43:07.020
to analyze the different phases
and, again, I'm delighted

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:08.880
here because people
typically hear

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:11.100
about a psychedelic
journey but we never

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:14.087
really hear about the
stereotypic components

00:43:14.087 --> 00:43:16.170
of the beginning, middle,
and end of that journey.

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:18.900
We know that there's a peak
and that there's a parachuting

00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:21.640
down and et cetera.

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:24.960
But when you say that typically
there's an anxiety, maybe

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:27.340
some negative valence
in the early stage,

00:43:27.340 --> 00:43:30.880
do you mean about the sensations
people are experiencing

00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:34.390
or about some prior event
that's being called to mind

00:43:34.390 --> 00:43:36.250
that they're remembering?

00:43:36.250 --> 00:43:39.310
Likewise, for the positive
phase of the psychedelic

00:43:39.310 --> 00:43:41.267
journey or trip are people--

00:43:41.267 --> 00:43:42.475
do they still call it a trip?

00:43:42.475 --> 00:43:42.865
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:43:42.865 --> 00:43:43.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: All right.

00:43:43.990 --> 00:43:46.960
For the-- I guess,
we'll use trip.

00:43:46.960 --> 00:43:48.250
For the psychedelic trip.

00:43:48.250 --> 00:43:52.008
Are people feeling positive
about the experience like, uh.

00:43:52.008 --> 00:43:53.800
Like there's been some
sort of breakthrough

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:56.980
or they're in a
calmer state, or is it

00:43:56.980 --> 00:44:00.010
that they tend to be
focusing on prior events

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:01.400
that were positive.

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:04.360
So in other words,
is there a threading

00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:09.130
through of some concept that
comes to mind for people?

00:44:09.130 --> 00:44:12.220
Maybe about an earlier trauma
or maybe about a sense of self

00:44:12.220 --> 00:44:14.607
or a sense of other forgiveness.

00:44:14.607 --> 00:44:15.940
It could be any of these things.

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:18.937
But what do we know about the
finer details of all that?

00:44:18.937 --> 00:44:20.020
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Mhm.

00:44:20.020 --> 00:44:23.350
I would say, the
initial struggle

00:44:23.350 --> 00:44:25.990
is more against the
general drug effects

00:44:25.990 --> 00:44:29.300
than pinning it on
something specific.

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:32.440
It's more that normal
waking consciousness.

00:44:32.440 --> 00:44:35.240
We have a sense,
generally speaking,

00:44:35.240 --> 00:44:40.270
if we're well or well enough, a
sense of assuredness about what

00:44:40.270 --> 00:44:44.080
is a table here and so on.

00:44:44.080 --> 00:44:48.470
And we have that assuredness
to an extent about ourselves

00:44:48.470 --> 00:44:48.970
as well.

00:44:48.970 --> 00:44:51.050
It might be illusory,
but we have it.

00:44:51.050 --> 00:44:54.700
And what the drug's doing is
it's breaking down all of that.

00:44:54.700 --> 00:44:57.040
And it's scary as hell.

00:44:57.040 --> 00:45:01.420
And if it's a big dose,
it's just like human nature

00:45:01.420 --> 00:45:06.560
to rage against that a
bit and a bit like dying.

00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:07.640
I don't want this.

00:45:07.640 --> 00:45:09.882
It feels like I could be dying.

00:45:09.882 --> 00:45:11.465
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
might lose my mind.

00:45:11.465 --> 00:45:11.960
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:45:11.960 --> 00:45:12.350
That too.

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:12.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I'd never come back.

00:45:12.890 --> 00:45:14.932
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Those two are the classics.

00:45:14.932 --> 00:45:20.480
Is oh, but I might know that
I've taken a psychedelic

00:45:20.480 --> 00:45:23.120
and I might even know a
bit about psychedelics,

00:45:23.120 --> 00:45:26.090
but I still fear that
I'm going to go mad

00:45:26.090 --> 00:45:30.440
or I know that
generally speaking,

00:45:30.440 --> 00:45:41.540
these drugs don't have
a high fertility risk.

00:45:41.540 --> 00:45:44.300
I still think I'm going to die.

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:47.930
And it's very palpable
and that comes up.

00:45:47.930 --> 00:45:49.910
So yeah.

00:45:49.910 --> 00:45:51.560
I mean, those are
the core fears.

00:45:51.560 --> 00:45:55.010
That those two and very
reliably, that comes up.

00:45:55.010 --> 00:45:57.740
And it's really like
a basic drug action.

00:45:57.740 --> 00:46:00.740
It's dose-dependent but
it's a basic drug action

00:46:00.740 --> 00:46:04.520
that is forcing something
about the nature

00:46:04.520 --> 00:46:07.430
of the mind and the
way it's made up

00:46:07.430 --> 00:46:11.090
that makes it feel that way,
but it feels like I'm losing

00:46:11.090 --> 00:46:14.060
my mind or it feels like
I could lose my mind

00:46:14.060 --> 00:46:18.980
or that I could go insane
or that maybe I'm dying here

00:46:18.980 --> 00:46:20.270
and this is bad.

00:46:20.270 --> 00:46:21.155
Yeah.

00:46:21.155 --> 00:46:23.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You've
talked many times before

00:46:23.810 --> 00:46:26.270
and have done really
wonderful work looking

00:46:26.270 --> 00:46:31.130
at the changes in communication
between different brain areas

00:46:31.130 --> 00:46:33.500
while people are under the
influence of psychedelics.

00:46:33.500 --> 00:46:36.320
And I think the gestalt of those
data, correct me if I'm wrong,

00:46:36.320 --> 00:46:39.350
is that compared to the
non-psychedelic state, that

00:46:39.350 --> 00:46:41.720
under psychedelic influence.

00:46:41.720 --> 00:46:43.400
There is far more--

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:44.960
let's just call it
interconnectivity

00:46:44.960 --> 00:46:48.770
or communication between brain
areas that typically aren't

00:46:48.770 --> 00:46:50.480
communicating, which
probably is not

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:53.990
surprising to people given
the subjective effects

00:46:53.990 --> 00:46:55.730
of these drugs.

00:46:55.730 --> 00:46:59.450
What is the evidence that
after the psychedelic journey

00:46:59.450 --> 00:47:02.570
is over, that some
or perhaps all

00:47:02.570 --> 00:47:05.480
of that enhanced communication
across brain areas

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:06.720
is maintained?

00:47:06.720 --> 00:47:09.020
And if so, what role
do you feel that could

00:47:09.020 --> 00:47:13.100
play in these incredible
positive therapeutic outcomes?

00:47:13.100 --> 00:47:14.390
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:47:14.390 --> 00:47:19.640
So we've had some recent
findings in that direction

00:47:19.640 --> 00:47:21.440
where, yes, it's true.

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:26.796
And picture says
a thousand words

00:47:26.796 --> 00:47:29.450
that some people might be
familiar with are these two

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:33.650
circles project that
we did in collaboration

00:47:33.650 --> 00:47:38.090
with some researchers where
ordinarily, the communication

00:47:38.090 --> 00:47:44.120
is going on within
systems like other regions

00:47:44.120 --> 00:47:47.240
of the visual system
will be speaking mostly

00:47:47.240 --> 00:47:49.100
within the visual system.

00:47:49.100 --> 00:47:52.670
There'll be a kind of
cliquishness or a modularity

00:47:52.670 --> 00:47:55.730
to the quality of the
communication in the brain.

00:47:55.730 --> 00:48:02.690
And then the cool finding with
psilocybin was the first paper,

00:48:02.690 --> 00:48:04.520
is that the communication, yes.

00:48:04.520 --> 00:48:10.220
It transcends these modules and
becomes much more inter-modular

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:14.450
crossing different modalities
and that effect correlated

00:48:14.450 --> 00:48:16.550
with the magnitude of
the subjective effects

00:48:16.550 --> 00:48:20.120
and then we replicated it with
LSD using different methods.

00:48:20.120 --> 00:48:22.760
And a new paper
will come out soon

00:48:22.760 --> 00:48:26.210
with DMT showing
a similar effect.

00:48:26.210 --> 00:48:28.610
It's a bit of a debate
about what regions are most

00:48:28.610 --> 00:48:29.210
implicated.

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:32.930
But the general effect of an
increase in global functional

00:48:32.930 --> 00:48:36.650
connectivity is what we call
it or global communication

00:48:36.650 --> 00:48:37.265
in the brain.

00:48:37.265 --> 00:48:39.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And this
is while under the influence

00:48:39.260 --> 00:48:39.560
of these drugs.

00:48:39.560 --> 00:48:40.370
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
This is under the--

00:48:40.370 --> 00:48:42.662
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So putting
people into a brain scanner

00:48:42.662 --> 00:48:45.787
while they are under the
influence of the drug.

00:48:45.787 --> 00:48:46.370
Is that right?

00:48:46.370 --> 00:48:46.580
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yes.

00:48:46.580 --> 00:48:48.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That itself
must be quite an experience,

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:50.690
given that these
scanners are small tubes.

00:48:50.690 --> 00:48:53.540
You're in a bite bar, you've
got a bite bar in your mouth.

00:48:53.540 --> 00:48:55.737
That's quite a study.

00:48:55.737 --> 00:48:57.320
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
You don't always

00:48:57.320 --> 00:48:59.300
have a bite bar, at least
with the psychedelics.

00:48:59.300 --> 00:48:59.800
But yeah.

00:48:59.800 --> 00:49:02.210
You've got to keep
your head still.

00:49:02.210 --> 00:49:06.570
And you have the loud
MR scanner noise going.

00:49:06.570 --> 00:49:11.610
But because it's regular, there
aren't too many surprises.

00:49:11.610 --> 00:49:14.593
So it's actually
surprisingly tolerable.

00:49:14.593 --> 00:49:16.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You're
in a hospital setting.

00:49:16.510 --> 00:49:18.450
So you're not worried about
what would happen if you had

00:49:18.450 --> 00:49:19.500
a cardiac event or something.

00:49:19.500 --> 00:49:20.625
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:49:20.625 --> 00:49:22.950
You got professionals around,
and most people generally

00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:26.580
tolerate that setting quite
well, surprisingly well.

00:49:26.580 --> 00:49:27.600
But yeah.

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:28.380
We do all that.

00:49:28.380 --> 00:49:31.710
And yes, we do
see the opening up

00:49:31.710 --> 00:49:35.580
of the communication across
systems in the brain.

00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:38.910
And it does speak
to intuition about

00:49:38.910 --> 00:49:40.620
the subjective experience.

00:49:40.620 --> 00:49:45.963
The different modalities might
be blending with each other.

00:49:45.963 --> 00:49:47.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sorry
for interrupting,

00:49:47.630 --> 00:49:50.440
but I have to ask, is it
thought that the activation

00:49:50.440 --> 00:49:52.900
of the serotonin
2A receptors what's

00:49:52.900 --> 00:49:56.410
responsible for the increased
communication between brain

00:49:56.410 --> 00:49:58.687
areas that under
normal circumstances

00:49:58.687 --> 00:49:59.770
would not be communicated?

00:49:59.770 --> 00:50:00.853
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yes.

00:50:00.853 --> 00:50:03.940
So there's a few reasons why
some modeling work that--

00:50:03.940 --> 00:50:07.360
computational modeling work that
first identifies where the 2A

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:10.030
receptor is and then looks at--

00:50:10.030 --> 00:50:14.290
models its basic effect
on neural activity.

00:50:14.290 --> 00:50:21.370
Will recapitulate the--
or recreate the effect

00:50:21.370 --> 00:50:24.850
that we see actually in
the data with the scanning.

00:50:24.850 --> 00:50:27.340
So doing the
computational modeling.

00:50:27.340 --> 00:50:33.370
You can see the same effect by
knowing where the key receptors

00:50:33.370 --> 00:50:36.340
are and then making them
do a certain thing that we

00:50:36.340 --> 00:50:37.510
know psychedelics do.

00:50:37.510 --> 00:50:39.640
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I can
imagine two possibilities.

00:50:39.640 --> 00:50:42.780
And I think it's important to
distinguish between these two.

00:50:42.780 --> 00:50:47.780
One possibility is that the
activation of this serotonin 2A

00:50:47.780 --> 00:50:49.790
receptor leads to
increased connectivity

00:50:49.790 --> 00:50:54.680
and thereby auditory and
visual hallucinations emerge,

00:50:54.680 --> 00:50:57.380
changed patterns of
thinking emerge, et cetera.

00:50:57.380 --> 00:50:59.330
That's the obvious
interpretation.

00:50:59.330 --> 00:51:02.940
But the scientist
in me has to ask,

00:51:02.940 --> 00:51:07.840
is it possible that all of
that increased connectivity

00:51:07.840 --> 00:51:11.500
is occurring, and yet that is
a distinct phenomenon layered

00:51:11.500 --> 00:51:14.260
on top of some other effect
of the psychedelic drugs

00:51:14.260 --> 00:51:19.360
impacting access to the
unconscious, hallucinations?

00:51:19.360 --> 00:51:22.090
In other words, is it the
increased connectivity

00:51:22.090 --> 00:51:23.950
that's leading to the
subjective experience,

00:51:23.950 --> 00:51:25.675
or are those two things
happening in parallel?

00:51:25.675 --> 00:51:27.820
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Well,
they happen in parallel

00:51:27.820 --> 00:51:29.560
and they map to each other.

00:51:29.560 --> 00:51:33.760
But the question of
causality, what causes what,

00:51:33.760 --> 00:51:39.100
is the tricky thing
where I would suggest

00:51:39.100 --> 00:51:42.490
that the causality is circular.

00:51:42.490 --> 00:51:44.440
That they influence each other.

00:51:44.440 --> 00:51:47.350
And this gets a
bit philosophical.

00:51:47.350 --> 00:51:51.790
But it matters, because
otherwise, there's

00:51:51.790 --> 00:51:57.160
a trap that it's easy to fall
into where you're thinking

00:51:57.160 --> 00:51:59.050
that it's all about
the brain action

00:51:59.050 --> 00:52:00.610
causing the
subjective experience.

00:52:00.610 --> 00:52:04.360
And that's typically what we
do in cognitive neuroscience.

00:52:04.360 --> 00:52:11.030
It's like the first port of
call materialist approach.

00:52:11.030 --> 00:52:14.060
But one can be a materialist,
essentially, but still

00:52:14.060 --> 00:52:17.540
appreciate that circular
causality that mind also

00:52:17.540 --> 00:52:20.150
interacts with brain.

00:52:20.150 --> 00:52:22.760
And it's so hard to
pick the two apart.

00:52:22.760 --> 00:52:24.890
And there is a kind
of essential dualism

00:52:24.890 --> 00:52:28.550
where subjective experience
is a thing in and of itself.

00:52:28.550 --> 00:52:30.890
But that's not to
divorce it from what's

00:52:30.890 --> 00:52:32.995
going on the biological level.

00:52:32.995 --> 00:52:34.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
The reason I ask

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:38.120
is because as I understand
it, nowadays, there's

00:52:38.120 --> 00:52:42.560
a bit of a movement within
the scientific community

00:52:42.560 --> 00:52:47.480
that studies psychedelics
to develop drugs that can,

00:52:47.480 --> 00:52:49.610
essentially, cure
or alleviate many

00:52:49.610 --> 00:52:52.100
of the symptoms of
depression or trauma

00:52:52.100 --> 00:52:54.830
that are built off our
understanding of how

00:52:54.830 --> 00:52:58.610
psychedelics like psilocybin and
here I'll throw MDMA in there,

00:52:58.610 --> 00:53:01.310
although classically not a
psychedelic, it gets lumped in.

00:53:01.310 --> 00:53:03.090
We can get back to that later.

00:53:03.090 --> 00:53:07.950
But that do not
produce hallucinations

00:53:07.950 --> 00:53:10.912
or massive changes in
subjective experience.

00:53:10.912 --> 00:53:12.870
Actually, I think this
is what initially got us

00:53:12.870 --> 00:53:14.730
into conversation
on Twitter as I

00:53:14.730 --> 00:53:16.830
had learned about this
paper published out

00:53:16.830 --> 00:53:18.150
of a group at UC Davis.

00:53:18.150 --> 00:53:20.190
That essentially
modifying psychedelics

00:53:20.190 --> 00:53:23.580
so that they have potential
therapeutic application

00:53:23.580 --> 00:53:27.260
for the treatment of
depression but zero

00:53:27.260 --> 00:53:29.630
hallucinogenic properties.

00:53:29.630 --> 00:53:30.650
And I thought, wow.

00:53:30.650 --> 00:53:33.387
This is going to be a
very controversial thing

00:53:33.387 --> 00:53:34.220
in the world, right?

00:53:34.220 --> 00:53:35.900
Because the history
of psychedelics,

00:53:35.900 --> 00:53:38.180
as you pointed out,
has been one of people

00:53:38.180 --> 00:53:40.820
accessing different modes
of thinking, feeling.

00:53:40.820 --> 00:53:43.070
Seeing things and letting go.

00:53:43.070 --> 00:53:44.000
Trust, et cetera.

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:45.600
A therapeutic relationship.

00:53:45.600 --> 00:53:47.853
And here we have--

00:53:47.853 --> 00:53:50.270
I don't want to say pharma
because it's not really pharma,

00:53:50.270 --> 00:53:53.540
but we have laboratories who
are trying to tease apart

00:53:53.540 --> 00:53:56.960
the activation of receptors
independent of all

00:53:56.960 --> 00:53:58.580
that subjective
experience in order

00:53:58.580 --> 00:54:00.800
to, essentially, treat
the same conditions.

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:02.630
I'd love for you to
comment on this, where

00:54:02.630 --> 00:54:04.850
you think it might be going.

00:54:04.850 --> 00:54:07.430
And whether or not
you think that's

00:54:07.430 --> 00:54:11.450
the right or the wrong approach,
if it has any validity at all.

00:54:11.450 --> 00:54:13.850
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: It is
pharmas, just smaller pharma,

00:54:13.850 --> 00:54:14.960
sort of startup pharma.

00:54:14.960 --> 00:54:15.140
But--

00:54:15.140 --> 00:54:15.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:54:15.320 --> 00:54:16.670
So because pharma
would like to have

00:54:16.670 --> 00:54:18.770
drugs that can cure depression
but don't make people

00:54:18.770 --> 00:54:19.280
hallucinate.

00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:20.030
Is that correct?

00:54:20.030 --> 00:54:21.572
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Oh, they would.

00:54:21.572 --> 00:54:23.870
And patients might
and the system

00:54:23.870 --> 00:54:26.540
would love it, because
the system is used to it.

00:54:26.540 --> 00:54:27.315
It's medicine.

00:54:27.315 --> 00:54:28.273
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

00:54:28.273 --> 00:54:33.870
And it doesn't give this mental
imagery of the Summer of Love

00:54:33.870 --> 00:54:38.730
in San Francisco or of
kaleidoscope eyes, right?

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:39.600
It's more of--

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:40.725
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:54:40.725 --> 00:54:42.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You
can imagine, the more--

00:54:42.660 --> 00:54:45.060
I have to be careful
with my wording here.

00:54:45.060 --> 00:54:48.950
Those who would not be
inclined toward that

00:54:48.950 --> 00:54:51.877
might embrace a therapeutic
that is strictly

00:54:51.877 --> 00:54:54.210
effective at treating depression
with no hallucinations.

00:54:54.210 --> 00:54:55.335
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:54:55.335 --> 00:55:00.000
And it doesn't look like an
individual lying on a sofa

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:08.160
crying their eyes out about
the life that they've lived.

00:55:08.160 --> 00:55:12.150
And that deep catharsis
being life-transforming.

00:55:12.150 --> 00:55:15.650
Is very different
from that model.

00:55:15.650 --> 00:55:21.490
I'm skeptical of it
for a few reasons.

00:55:21.490 --> 00:55:27.560
And one is that I
can't see the logic--

00:55:27.560 --> 00:55:32.000
I can't see the pieces fit
in a way that's compelling.

00:55:32.000 --> 00:55:35.810
And I'm also skeptical,
because I think it could easily

00:55:35.810 --> 00:55:40.130
be wishful thinking, because
of that point that patients

00:55:40.130 --> 00:55:43.130
would like it and the
system would like it.

00:55:43.130 --> 00:55:49.100
And you got to bear
that in mind as well.

00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:53.180
So wouldn't it be
convenient if it were true

00:55:53.180 --> 00:55:55.160
and you could get the
therapeutic action

00:55:55.160 --> 00:55:56.915
without the psychedelic effects?

00:55:56.915 --> 00:55:58.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, in a way, that's

00:55:58.540 --> 00:56:01.400
a little bit of what microdosing
seems to be designed to do.

00:56:01.400 --> 00:56:05.330
Like you said, take dosages
that are below that perceptual

00:56:05.330 --> 00:56:08.540
or awareness of some
effect threshold

00:56:08.540 --> 00:56:12.260
over a longer period of time in
an attempt to ping the circuits

00:56:12.260 --> 00:56:16.070
or alter the circuits
but not hallucinate.

00:56:16.070 --> 00:56:17.075
Not have a catharsis.

00:56:17.075 --> 00:56:20.930
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: So
if microdosing can do that,

00:56:20.930 --> 00:56:23.330
and it's sub-perceptible,
then microdosing

00:56:23.330 --> 00:56:26.750
isn't a psychedelic
action, because whereas

00:56:26.750 --> 00:56:28.070
the psychedelic action.

00:56:28.070 --> 00:56:31.740
When psychedelic, when defined,
means psyche revealing.

00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:33.960
You're not getting that effect.

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:35.970
You might be getting
the pharmacology,

00:56:35.970 --> 00:56:39.210
you might be getting some
direct serotonin to a receptor

00:56:39.210 --> 00:56:42.970
agonism that could be driving
a therapeutic response,

00:56:42.970 --> 00:56:46.380
but you can get that
with SSRIs as well.

00:56:46.380 --> 00:56:49.330
And so my point is, what's new?

00:56:49.330 --> 00:56:49.830
OK.

00:56:49.830 --> 00:56:52.740
Maybe it's a bit new
and people are now

00:56:52.740 --> 00:56:56.970
developing direct 2A
agonists rather than indirect

00:56:56.970 --> 00:57:00.420
through a serotonin releaser
like the selective serotonin

00:57:00.420 --> 00:57:03.405
reuptake inhibitors,
the SSRIs like Lexapro.

00:57:03.405 --> 00:57:05.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Are there any SSRIs

00:57:05.460 --> 00:57:10.890
that selectively agonize,
which folks by the way means,

00:57:10.890 --> 00:57:12.630
activate, in a good way.

00:57:12.630 --> 00:57:14.310
Agony sounds terrible.

00:57:14.310 --> 00:57:16.860
Those not informed might
think that mean that disrupt.

00:57:16.860 --> 00:57:20.400
But that can activate the
serotonin 2A receptor.

00:57:20.400 --> 00:57:24.733
Are there any drugs that will
do that are not psychedelic?

00:57:24.733 --> 00:57:26.400
I'm not aware of any,
but then again I'm

00:57:26.400 --> 00:57:27.195
not a psychopharmacologist.

00:57:27.195 --> 00:57:28.778
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Well, there are.

00:57:28.778 --> 00:57:31.560
I mean, are there any
that are licensed and used

00:57:31.560 --> 00:57:33.820
as medicines in psychiatry?

00:57:33.820 --> 00:57:38.430
I actually had this debate
recently on social media,

00:57:38.430 --> 00:57:42.540
and I couldn't see a
compelling example.

00:57:42.540 --> 00:57:45.570
I saw 2A agonists that
were used for other things.

00:57:45.570 --> 00:57:48.150
You have a compound
like Lesuride

00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:51.540
used in treating Parkinson's,
but actually it's

00:57:51.540 --> 00:57:52.940
more of a dopamine agonist.

00:57:52.940 --> 00:57:53.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

00:57:53.230 --> 00:57:55.100
So they're always hitting
other things, right?

00:57:55.100 --> 00:57:55.340
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:57:55.340 --> 00:57:55.580
Yeah.

00:57:55.580 --> 00:57:56.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
They're always tapping

00:57:56.550 --> 00:57:57.590
other neurotransmitters--

00:57:57.590 --> 00:58:00.048
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: All that
being used for other things.

00:58:00.048 --> 00:58:01.650
So is there a
selective serotonin

00:58:01.650 --> 00:58:05.250
2A receptor
stimulator, an agonist,

00:58:05.250 --> 00:58:09.270
that isn't psychedelic, that
is therapeutic in psychiatry

00:58:09.270 --> 00:58:10.920
and the answer firmly is, no.

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:12.180
I haven't seen it yet.

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:13.590
Will they develop one?

00:58:13.590 --> 00:58:16.350
Well, for patients
sake, I hope so.

00:58:16.350 --> 00:58:18.975
Because it would be great.

00:58:18.975 --> 00:58:20.130
Let's wait and see.

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:22.770
If they do, I doubt
it'll be psychedelic

00:58:22.770 --> 00:58:28.210
and I doubt it would have much
to do with psychedelic therapy.

00:58:28.210 --> 00:58:32.110
And it would be much more
like the system we're used to

00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:34.660
of chronic pharmacotherapy.

00:58:34.660 --> 00:58:37.450
Take your drug every day.

00:58:37.450 --> 00:58:41.230
Let's hope they find it and
it works for patients sake.

00:58:41.230 --> 00:58:45.910
But as things stand right
now, I'm a little skeptical.

00:58:45.910 --> 00:58:47.470
Now, some of the
findings that are

00:58:47.470 --> 00:58:50.800
being seen that are
really exciting, fantastic

00:58:50.800 --> 00:58:55.510
work being done, showing
things like increases

00:58:55.510 --> 00:58:59.680
in the communication
components of neurons,

00:58:59.680 --> 00:59:04.722
dendritic growth, spine
growth, synaptic spine growth.

00:59:04.722 --> 00:59:06.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
by the way, folks,

00:59:06.430 --> 00:59:10.930
just I'll interrupt for
not necessarily spine--

00:59:10.930 --> 00:59:13.960
not the cerebral
column but spines

00:59:13.960 --> 00:59:16.750
are these little
tiny twigs with bulbs

00:59:16.750 --> 00:59:20.230
on the end of neurons that
allow for communication

00:59:20.230 --> 00:59:21.440
points between neurons.

00:59:21.440 --> 00:59:23.110
So neuroplasticity
is often associated

00:59:23.110 --> 00:59:24.820
with growth of
dendrites and spines

00:59:24.820 --> 00:59:27.505
and so forth, which is
what Robin's referring to.

00:59:27.505 --> 00:59:29.380
That reminds me, and I
just want to make sure

00:59:29.380 --> 00:59:31.630
that we close the hatch
on the earlier answer,

00:59:31.630 --> 00:59:33.130
because I interrupted you.

00:59:33.130 --> 00:59:35.890
Is the increased
connectivity between--

00:59:35.890 --> 00:59:38.680
or communication
between brain areas

00:59:38.680 --> 00:59:41.170
that's observed while people
are under the influence

00:59:41.170 --> 00:59:44.080
of the psychedelic
also observed later

00:59:44.080 --> 00:59:45.875
after the effects of
the drug wear off?

00:59:45.875 --> 00:59:46.205
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

00:59:46.205 --> 00:59:47.663
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And then I'll just

00:59:47.663 --> 00:59:50.370
throw in another question there,
because we're on to this topic

00:59:50.370 --> 00:59:50.870
now.

00:59:50.870 --> 00:59:55.340
To what extent do we think that
neuroplasticity, structural

00:59:55.340 --> 00:59:57.890
changes in neurons,
functional changes in neurons

00:59:57.890 --> 00:59:59.810
are responsible for that?

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:02.270
And how long does that last?

01:00:02.270 --> 01:00:04.520
Let's say I come
into your clinic,

01:00:04.520 --> 01:00:07.040
I'm a subject in your
experiment, I take--

01:00:07.040 --> 01:00:09.290
come in the morning, I
do my psychedelic journey

01:00:09.290 --> 01:00:10.790
five or six hours later.

01:00:10.790 --> 01:00:14.450
I'm parachuting back to
reality, as we call it,

01:00:14.450 --> 01:00:16.310
and then I go home.

01:00:16.310 --> 01:00:19.190
Increase connectivity
lasts for how long,

01:00:19.190 --> 01:00:22.273
and how long are the structural
brain changes occurring?

01:00:22.273 --> 01:00:23.690
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Well, you're

01:00:23.690 --> 01:00:26.930
asking fantastic questions,
and partly because we

01:00:26.930 --> 01:00:28.340
don't have the answer yet.

01:00:28.340 --> 01:00:32.100
But we do have some data.

01:00:32.100 --> 01:00:34.670
And so we have looked
at, first of all,

01:00:34.670 --> 01:00:37.700
in a sense, the functional
plasticity of what we assume

01:00:37.700 --> 01:00:39.170
it to be or at
least the functional

01:00:39.170 --> 01:00:43.790
changes, the increase in
communication across systems

01:00:43.790 --> 01:00:46.220
that increase in
global connectivity,

01:00:46.220 --> 01:00:47.660
functional connectivity.

01:00:47.660 --> 01:00:49.800
Do we see it after the trip?

01:00:49.800 --> 01:00:53.210
We know we see it during the
trip pretty well replicated

01:00:53.210 --> 01:00:55.160
correlating with
intense drug effects.

01:00:55.160 --> 01:00:56.930
Do we see it after the trip?

01:00:56.930 --> 01:00:58.790
Well the answer
is, we've seen it

01:00:58.790 --> 01:01:03.200
in two different depression
cohorts, psilocybin therapy

01:01:03.200 --> 01:01:04.160
for depression.

01:01:04.160 --> 01:01:07.790
In one study where we looked
the next day, we saw it.

01:01:07.790 --> 01:01:15.260
A kind of residual effect
similar to what you see acutely

01:01:15.260 --> 01:01:17.090
being seen the next day.

01:01:17.090 --> 01:01:19.970
And then in a
subsequent study, we

01:01:19.970 --> 01:01:23.010
saw it also three weeks later.

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:26.270
So we've seen it in two
independent data sets.

01:01:26.270 --> 01:01:29.810
This decrease in modularity
is how we measure it.

01:01:29.810 --> 01:01:31.670
It's the same
thing, essentially.

01:01:31.670 --> 01:01:33.750
Broadly speaking,
it's the same thing.

01:01:33.750 --> 01:01:36.680
An increase in
global connectivity,

01:01:36.680 --> 01:01:38.150
functional connectivity.

01:01:38.150 --> 01:01:40.400
And actually,
unpublished we've seen it

01:01:40.400 --> 01:01:44.000
in healthy volunteers on
a correlational level.

01:01:44.000 --> 01:01:46.640
Not on an absolute
change level, but if you

01:01:46.640 --> 01:01:50.960
look at its relationship
to a mental health outcome,

01:01:50.960 --> 01:01:53.510
and this is an important thing
to stress with the depression

01:01:53.510 --> 01:01:56.990
work, we saw a relationship
between the magnitude

01:01:56.990 --> 01:02:00.710
of that change, the decrease
in modularity or increase

01:02:00.710 --> 01:02:03.590
in global connectivity,
and the improvement

01:02:03.590 --> 01:02:04.565
in symptom severity.

01:02:04.565 --> 01:02:05.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So interesting.

01:02:05.660 --> 01:02:06.830
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:02:06.830 --> 01:02:09.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And just
to state it a different way,

01:02:09.080 --> 01:02:12.410
so what Robin is referring to
is when you say modularity,

01:02:12.410 --> 01:02:15.470
as neuroscientists, we think of
the different modular networks

01:02:15.470 --> 01:02:16.460
of the brain.

01:02:16.460 --> 01:02:19.100
The eye talks to a region
of the thalamus involved

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:23.000
in vision, which talks to the
visual cortex, which eventually

01:02:23.000 --> 01:02:26.460
converges with auditory
information, of course.

01:02:26.460 --> 01:02:29.780
But there's a separation
or modularity of function.

01:02:29.780 --> 01:02:32.870
This increased connectivity
is cross-modular

01:02:32.870 --> 01:02:35.120
in during the trip,
but afterwards as well.

01:02:35.120 --> 01:02:38.120
And you're saying that that
correlates very strongly

01:02:38.120 --> 01:02:40.370
with the strength of
the therapeutic outcome

01:02:40.370 --> 01:02:41.180
for depression.

01:02:41.180 --> 01:02:44.180
I mean, the logical
extension of that

01:02:44.180 --> 01:02:47.630
is that extreme modularity
of brain function

01:02:47.630 --> 01:02:50.185
is depressive in some way.

01:02:50.185 --> 01:02:51.560
Now, we don't want
to go too far,

01:02:51.560 --> 01:02:54.380
but what does that
mean that increasing

01:02:54.380 --> 01:02:58.610
crosstalk between different
modules of the brain

01:02:58.610 --> 01:03:02.750
is so strongly correlated with
a positive therapeutic outcome?

01:03:02.750 --> 01:03:04.310
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
We don't other

01:03:04.310 --> 01:03:06.240
than there's a relationship.

01:03:06.240 --> 01:03:07.970
I mean, this is the thing.

01:03:07.970 --> 01:03:12.140
We need to be a little careful
not to run with it too far.

01:03:12.140 --> 01:03:14.810
I mean, there's some
things that it suggests.

01:03:14.810 --> 01:03:22.090
I think it suggests a more
flexible mode of brain

01:03:22.090 --> 01:03:25.870
functioning, if you're not
getting stuck in modules

01:03:25.870 --> 01:03:29.110
or the modules
aren't excessively

01:03:29.110 --> 01:03:31.340
cut off from each other.

01:03:31.340 --> 01:03:34.450
But you see different things
with different presentations

01:03:34.450 --> 01:03:36.280
if you were to
look at cognition.

01:03:36.280 --> 01:03:39.520
Sharper cognition is
actually associated

01:03:39.520 --> 01:03:41.140
with more modularity.

01:03:41.140 --> 01:03:44.110
So it's a rule that's
a little slippery.

01:03:44.110 --> 01:03:46.483
And we need to be
careful with it.

01:03:46.483 --> 01:03:48.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Again,
forgive me for interrupting,

01:03:48.650 --> 01:03:51.500
but I think I have
friends who are--

01:03:51.500 --> 01:03:53.140
I would say, are
on the spectrum.

01:03:53.140 --> 01:03:56.680
Who are very linear
in their thinking

01:03:56.680 --> 01:04:00.310
and extremely
intelligent in the kind

01:04:00.310 --> 01:04:03.010
of classic sense of
being able to ratchet

01:04:03.010 --> 01:04:05.800
through hard problems
to arrive at a solution.

01:04:05.800 --> 01:04:07.510
And then I have
friends who are-- let's

01:04:07.510 --> 01:04:08.718
just call them what they are.

01:04:08.718 --> 01:04:11.080
From the creative communities
outside of science

01:04:11.080 --> 01:04:15.550
that are very expansive,
they see connections

01:04:15.550 --> 01:04:17.623
between many different
things, but sometimes, you

01:04:17.623 --> 01:04:19.040
have to-- not all
of them, but you

01:04:19.040 --> 01:04:21.050
have to catch their ideas
with a butterfly net.

01:04:21.050 --> 01:04:23.458
And oftentimes, what
they're saying sometimes

01:04:23.458 --> 01:04:24.750
it just doesn't make any sense.

01:04:24.750 --> 01:04:28.100
Now, they also produce
incredible creative works.

01:04:28.100 --> 01:04:30.020
But to have a
conversation with them

01:04:30.020 --> 01:04:32.210
is anything but a
linear experience.

01:04:32.210 --> 01:04:35.270
They are not random
thought generators,

01:04:35.270 --> 01:04:39.680
but there's a non-linearity or
randomness to their processing

01:04:39.680 --> 01:04:41.900
that's distinct from
these other folks

01:04:41.900 --> 01:04:43.430
that I'm describing
on the spectrum.

01:04:43.430 --> 01:04:44.722
And of course, it's a spectrum.

01:04:44.722 --> 01:04:46.910
There's a whole
range in between.

01:04:46.910 --> 01:04:50.630
It sounds to me like there
is some therapeutic value

01:04:50.630 --> 01:04:54.530
to being able to move along
this continuum from the more

01:04:54.530 --> 01:04:56.083
linear to the nonlinear.

01:04:56.083 --> 01:04:56.750
Is that correct?

01:04:56.750 --> 01:04:57.680
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Well, I think so.

01:04:57.680 --> 01:04:58.562
Yeah.

01:04:58.562 --> 01:05:00.020
It's resonating
what you're saying.

01:05:00.020 --> 01:05:01.580
It's speaking to my intuition.

01:05:01.580 --> 01:05:08.090
That you could be very
passy, passing things up,

01:05:08.090 --> 01:05:11.220
chopping things up like an
analytical scientist like I'm

01:05:11.220 --> 01:05:11.720
doing a bit.

01:05:11.720 --> 01:05:13.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A splitter
as we say in science.

01:05:13.130 --> 01:05:15.050
You're either a
lumper or a splitter.

01:05:15.050 --> 01:05:15.595
Yeah.

01:05:15.595 --> 01:05:16.970
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
The way I'm

01:05:16.970 --> 01:05:22.250
being very particular about when
to call something psychedelic.

01:05:22.250 --> 01:05:25.520
That kind of passy analytical
way of thinking you

01:05:25.520 --> 01:05:29.630
might associate with
a more modular system.

01:05:29.630 --> 01:05:33.080
Whereas the system that's
more globally, interconnected,

01:05:33.080 --> 01:05:34.610
and open, yeah.

01:05:34.610 --> 01:05:37.970
Might be more flexible
and creative and divergent

01:05:37.970 --> 01:05:39.900
in the associations
and so on so.

01:05:39.900 --> 01:05:40.400
Yes.

01:05:40.400 --> 01:05:44.330
That's speaking to my intuition
to how you're describing it.

01:05:44.330 --> 01:05:48.350
And I imagine, if you take
severe psychopathology, severe

01:05:48.350 --> 01:05:51.140
mental illness
like a depression,

01:05:51.140 --> 01:05:53.480
I've always thought that
there's something intuitive

01:05:53.480 --> 01:05:55.010
about the term itself.

01:05:55.010 --> 01:05:57.938
Like a depression in a
landscape, which is a halt.

01:05:57.938 --> 01:05:59.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Physical depression.

01:05:59.480 --> 01:06:01.272
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
A physical depression

01:06:01.272 --> 01:06:02.610
that is easy to fall into.

01:06:02.610 --> 01:06:04.660
And if you do, it's
hard to get out of.

01:06:04.660 --> 01:06:05.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So almost--

01:06:05.990 --> 01:06:08.040
if I understand what
you're saying correctly,

01:06:08.040 --> 01:06:11.990
almost like getting stuck at
one location on this continuum.

01:06:11.990 --> 01:06:15.710
Because most people don't
reside at one extreme

01:06:15.710 --> 01:06:17.145
or the other full time.

01:06:17.145 --> 01:06:18.770
They can kind of
migrate back and forth

01:06:18.770 --> 01:06:21.815
between expansive states
and more linear states.

01:06:21.815 --> 01:06:23.960
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Like you do with low mood.

01:06:23.960 --> 01:06:25.910
If you're "healthy"
in inverted commas,

01:06:25.910 --> 01:06:28.310
you can feel your low
mood, your disappointment.

01:06:28.310 --> 01:06:29.750
But you can spring back.

01:06:29.750 --> 01:06:30.800
But someone with--

01:06:30.800 --> 01:06:31.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And you
know you can spring back.

01:06:31.970 --> 01:06:32.300
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:06:32.300 --> 01:06:33.258
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:06:33.258 --> 01:06:37.040
Whereas the suicidal
depressive person or suicidally

01:06:37.040 --> 01:06:42.880
depressed person, somehow,
at least in my understanding,

01:06:42.880 --> 01:06:46.250
there's something about that
extreme depressive states

01:06:46.250 --> 01:06:48.890
and extreme anxiety states,
something my laboratory is

01:06:48.890 --> 01:06:51.650
a bit more familiar
with anxiety, which

01:06:51.650 --> 01:06:54.950
alters the perception of
time such that people feel

01:06:54.950 --> 01:07:00.650
like that negative state
is going to go on forever

01:07:00.650 --> 01:07:05.360
or that if it goes away, that
it's going to return at random.

01:07:05.360 --> 01:07:06.485
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:07:06.485 --> 01:07:08.152
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Kind
of a vulnerability

01:07:08.152 --> 01:07:09.095
to the time domain.

01:07:09.095 --> 01:07:10.220
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:07:10.220 --> 01:07:10.910
Yeah.

01:07:10.910 --> 01:07:15.380
And it's so tragic, that
cognitive bias in depression

01:07:15.380 --> 01:07:18.320
that everything's
hopeless and that there

01:07:18.320 --> 01:07:20.100
is no light at the
end of the tunnel.

01:07:20.100 --> 01:07:20.600
Yeah.

01:07:20.600 --> 01:07:21.100
Yes.

01:07:21.100 --> 01:07:25.370
So if you were to get stuck in
that rut and have that bias,

01:07:25.370 --> 01:07:29.810
then you're cut off
from other things,

01:07:29.810 --> 01:07:34.175
other sensory
modalities or modules,

01:07:34.175 --> 01:07:36.830
cut off from the world,
cut off from other people.

01:07:36.830 --> 01:07:39.620
Stuck in your inner rut.

01:07:39.620 --> 01:07:40.640
So yes.

01:07:40.640 --> 01:07:42.440
I think we're sharing
this intuition

01:07:42.440 --> 01:07:45.410
that a decrease in
modularity or an opening

01:07:45.410 --> 01:07:50.030
up of the system, the brain
could relate to an opening

01:07:50.030 --> 01:07:56.060
up of the mind that is enduring
after the psychedelic dosing

01:07:56.060 --> 01:07:56.840
session.

01:07:56.840 --> 01:07:57.410
And yeah.

01:07:57.410 --> 01:08:00.080
And the third replication
was to see in healthies

01:08:00.080 --> 01:08:01.820
an improvement in well-being.

01:08:01.820 --> 01:08:04.055
Because they're healthy, we
don't look at depression.

01:08:04.055 --> 01:08:05.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So these are people

01:08:05.030 --> 01:08:06.800
that are healthy
walking into the trial?

01:08:06.800 --> 01:08:07.130
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:08:07.130 --> 01:08:09.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Take
psilocybin twice?

01:08:09.110 --> 01:08:11.690
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Well, actually they do.

01:08:11.690 --> 01:08:14.540
But the first dose is
1 milligram, which they

01:08:14.540 --> 01:08:16.545
don't feel it's a placebo dose.

01:08:16.545 --> 01:08:17.576
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Quote
unquote, "micro dose."

01:08:17.576 --> 01:08:17.674
Yeah.

01:08:17.674 --> 01:08:18.799
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:08:18.799 --> 01:08:22.819
We stick EGG on their heads
to measure their brainwaves

01:08:22.819 --> 01:08:24.529
during each dose.

01:08:24.529 --> 01:08:27.630
And 1 milligram,
you see no change.

01:08:27.630 --> 01:08:30.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think
that, you microdosers.

01:08:30.020 --> 01:08:30.960
No, I'm just kidding.

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:32.450
I mean, nothing against
the micro doses.

01:08:32.450 --> 01:08:34.283
I've always just been
a little bit skeptical

01:08:34.283 --> 01:08:36.560
based on my conversations
with the scientists

01:08:36.560 --> 01:08:41.899
actually doing the
work with psychedelics.

01:08:41.899 --> 01:08:44.239
It seems like the answer
keeps coming back.

01:08:48.060 --> 01:08:53.163
1 or 2, maybe 3 macro doses
in a controlled safe setting.

01:08:53.163 --> 01:08:55.080
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Well, that's compelling.

01:08:55.080 --> 01:08:56.910
The evidence for
that is compelling.

01:08:56.910 --> 01:09:00.810
And that's what's making all
the difference right now.

01:09:00.810 --> 01:09:02.850
Microdosing is just
appealing, but again,

01:09:02.850 --> 01:09:06.899
science isn't about
what we want to believe,

01:09:06.899 --> 01:09:09.930
it's about what's
actually coming through

01:09:09.930 --> 01:09:13.463
and what seems to
hold up to testing.

01:09:13.463 --> 01:09:15.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'd like
to just take a brief break

01:09:15.630 --> 01:09:18.300
and thank one of our
sponsors, which is LMNT.

01:09:18.300 --> 01:09:21.060
LMNT is an electrolyte drink
that has everything you need

01:09:21.060 --> 01:09:22.109
and nothing you don't.

01:09:22.109 --> 01:09:23.880
That means, plenty of salt--

01:09:23.880 --> 01:09:25.770
sodium, magnesium,
and potassium,

01:09:25.770 --> 01:09:28.500
the so-called
electrolytes and no sugar.

01:09:28.500 --> 01:09:30.750
Now, salt, magnesium,
and potassium

01:09:30.750 --> 01:09:33.270
are critical to the function
of all the cells in your body,

01:09:33.270 --> 01:09:36.180
in particular, to the function
of your nerve cells, also

01:09:36.180 --> 01:09:37.050
called neurons.

01:09:37.050 --> 01:09:39.720
And we now know that
even slight reductions

01:09:39.720 --> 01:09:42.810
in electrolyte concentrations
or dehydration of the body

01:09:42.810 --> 01:09:45.689
can lead to deficits in
cognitive and physical

01:09:45.689 --> 01:09:46.470
performance.

01:09:46.470 --> 01:09:49.149
LMNT contains a science-backed
electrolyte ratio

01:09:49.149 --> 01:09:53.020
of 1,000 milligrams, that's 1
gram of sodium, 200 milligrams

01:09:53.020 --> 01:09:55.720
of potassium, and 60
milligrams of magnesium.

01:09:55.720 --> 01:09:57.850
I typically drink LMNT
first thing in the morning

01:09:57.850 --> 01:09:59.832
when I wake up in order
to hydrate my body

01:09:59.832 --> 01:10:01.540
and make sure I have
enough electrolytes.

01:10:01.540 --> 01:10:04.030
And while I do any kind
of physical training

01:10:04.030 --> 01:10:06.220
and after physical training
as well, especially,

01:10:06.220 --> 01:10:08.230
if I've been sweating
a lot and certainly,

01:10:08.230 --> 01:10:11.190
I drink LMNT in my water
when I'm in the sauna

01:10:11.190 --> 01:10:12.940
and after going in the
sauna, because that

01:10:12.940 --> 01:10:14.232
causes quite a lot of sweating.

01:10:14.232 --> 01:10:17.170
If you'd like to try LMNT
you can go to drinklmnt--

01:10:17.170 --> 01:10:21.358
that's L-M-N-T.com/huberman to
claim a free LMNT sample pack

01:10:21.358 --> 01:10:22.150
with your purchase.

01:10:22.150 --> 01:10:26.185
Again, that's drinklmnt--
L-M-N-T.com/huberman.

01:10:26.185 --> 01:10:27.310
Would you say that's right?

01:10:27.310 --> 01:10:30.340
That one or two or three
sessions, and how far

01:10:30.340 --> 01:10:32.575
apart are those
typically spaced in time?

01:10:32.575 --> 01:10:33.700
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:10:33.700 --> 01:10:38.290
Typically, one to three
weeks across the sites

01:10:38.290 --> 01:10:40.900
is the way people are doing
the psychedelic therapy

01:10:40.900 --> 01:10:42.160
dosing sessions.

01:10:42.160 --> 01:10:48.210
Two sessions, you know,
Hopkins, Imperial, NYU.

01:10:48.210 --> 01:10:51.870
That's been a kind
of default to--

01:10:51.870 --> 01:10:54.360
we actually use three
in a current anorexia

01:10:54.360 --> 01:10:57.450
trial, psilocybin
therapy for anorexia.

01:10:57.450 --> 01:11:02.610
Two patients left
to see after 19

01:11:02.610 --> 01:11:04.380
who've gone through the trial.

01:11:04.380 --> 01:11:06.615
Very exciting results there.

01:11:06.615 --> 01:11:08.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You're
seeing an alleviation

01:11:08.490 --> 01:11:12.300
of the obsessive thought
about food and a willingness

01:11:12.300 --> 01:11:14.445
to consume healthier
amounts of food.

01:11:14.445 --> 01:11:15.570
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:11:15.570 --> 01:11:18.510
Even improved weight
at the long follow-up.

01:11:18.510 --> 01:11:19.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So critical.

01:11:19.830 --> 01:11:22.050
When we did an episode
on eating disorders,

01:11:22.050 --> 01:11:26.310
I learned that anorexia
nervosa, which by the way folks,

01:11:26.310 --> 01:11:28.750
the rates are not increasing.

01:11:28.750 --> 01:11:30.630
It's been pretty
stable through time

01:11:30.630 --> 01:11:34.150
despite what's said about
social media, and et cetera.

01:11:34.150 --> 01:11:37.860
But anorexia nervosa
being the most

01:11:37.860 --> 01:11:39.930
deadly of all
psychiatric illnesses,

01:11:39.930 --> 01:11:44.550
which is a big statement,
because manic depression,

01:11:44.550 --> 01:11:48.445
so-called bipolar depression,
has a 20 to 30 times

01:11:48.445 --> 01:11:50.530
the typical suicide rate.

01:11:50.530 --> 01:11:55.780
Basically, many people with
anorexia, I think is how it's--

01:11:55.780 --> 01:11:57.880
is what one says, not anorexics.

01:11:57.880 --> 01:12:00.910
But people with
anorexia often die.

01:12:00.910 --> 01:12:02.165
Many of them die.

01:12:02.165 --> 01:12:03.290
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:12:03.290 --> 01:12:04.630
So tragic.

01:12:04.630 --> 01:12:06.590
So often young people as well.

01:12:06.590 --> 01:12:10.670
And similarly, with suicide
in terms of premature death.

01:12:10.670 --> 01:12:16.660
So the tragedy with
psychiatry is so strong.

01:12:16.660 --> 01:12:18.880
It's so rewarding to
be doing that trial

01:12:18.880 --> 01:12:21.010
and to be seeing good results.

01:12:21.010 --> 01:12:22.540
I have to check
myself a little bit

01:12:22.540 --> 01:12:27.340
that I'm reporting on it in
this really promisory way

01:12:27.340 --> 01:12:32.260
and the trial isn't yet
publicly released and published.

01:12:32.260 --> 01:12:34.420
So it's still ongoing as well.

01:12:34.420 --> 01:12:36.295
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
that was three sessions.

01:12:36.295 --> 01:12:38.087
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
It is three sessions.

01:12:38.087 --> 01:12:41.170
And I can't say what the dosage
is, because we still have--

01:12:41.170 --> 01:12:43.810
there is a blinding component.

01:12:43.810 --> 01:12:46.600
But there are three
dosing sessions in there.

01:12:46.600 --> 01:12:47.320
Let's see now.

01:12:47.320 --> 01:12:54.610
I think they're two weeks
apart and we do the follow-up.

01:12:54.610 --> 01:12:55.780
Yes.

01:12:55.780 --> 01:12:59.200
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'd
like to close out

01:12:59.200 --> 01:13:02.200
this description of the
journey and the trip

01:13:02.200 --> 01:13:05.170
by extending past the
day when people actually

01:13:05.170 --> 01:13:08.650
take the drug into this--
what I've described

01:13:08.650 --> 01:13:10.540
as the integration phase.

01:13:10.540 --> 01:13:12.670
You have to re-integrate.

01:13:12.670 --> 01:13:15.040
All this increased connectivity
during the session.

01:13:15.040 --> 01:13:18.640
Hallucinations, insights,
anxiety, letting go.

01:13:18.640 --> 01:13:21.170
Maybe revelation,
maybe epiphany.

01:13:21.170 --> 01:13:21.670
OK.

01:13:21.670 --> 01:13:22.420
Great.

01:13:22.420 --> 01:13:25.780
At what point is
that consolidated?

01:13:25.780 --> 01:13:30.250
Meaning, are these
patients or subjects

01:13:30.250 --> 01:13:32.945
in studies having
daily conversation

01:13:32.945 --> 01:13:33.820
with their therapist?

01:13:33.820 --> 01:13:35.920
Are they journaling every day?

01:13:35.920 --> 01:13:39.428
And I want to keep in mind
that most people are not going

01:13:39.428 --> 01:13:40.720
to be part of a clinical trial.

01:13:40.720 --> 01:13:42.520
And of course, here
we're not suggesting

01:13:42.520 --> 01:13:44.140
what people do or not do.

01:13:44.140 --> 01:13:46.620
But let's just put it this way.

01:13:46.620 --> 01:13:53.400
Were people to use psychedelics,
what is the way that people can

01:13:53.400 --> 01:13:56.820
maximize on the neuroplasticity
and the brain changes

01:13:56.820 --> 01:14:00.240
in a positive way in the
days and weeks afterwards?

01:14:00.240 --> 01:14:02.880
In other words, how long does
this so-called integration

01:14:02.880 --> 01:14:03.900
last?

01:14:03.900 --> 01:14:07.720
And how far can we take this?

01:14:07.720 --> 01:14:11.700
I mean, I could imagine
that how often one

01:14:11.700 --> 01:14:14.050
chooses to think
about the insights

01:14:14.050 --> 01:14:15.270
could also have an impact.

01:14:15.270 --> 01:14:15.540
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:14:15.540 --> 01:14:15.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right?

01:14:15.960 --> 01:14:18.090
Because clearly people
went to raves, clearly

01:14:18.090 --> 01:14:20.470
people did psychedelics
in the '60s.

01:14:20.470 --> 01:14:23.875
We don't know if clearly
people do psychedelics now.

01:14:23.875 --> 01:14:25.500
But we don't have
data on those people.

01:14:25.500 --> 01:14:27.930
You have access to the
understanding of how they're

01:14:27.930 --> 01:14:30.900
spending their time and
the therapeutic outcomes,

01:14:30.900 --> 01:14:33.780
which we haven't gotten to
the numbers yet, but again,

01:14:33.780 --> 01:14:35.700
are incredibly impressive.

01:14:35.700 --> 01:14:37.290
In upwards of, as
I understand it,

01:14:37.290 --> 01:14:40.545
60% or more people getting
relief from depression.

01:14:40.545 --> 01:14:41.670
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:14:41.670 --> 01:14:42.170
70%, yeah.

01:14:42.170 --> 01:14:43.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: 70%.

01:14:43.350 --> 01:14:44.910
Incredible, especially
when compared

01:14:44.910 --> 01:14:49.900
to the typical antidepressant
treatments and so on.

01:14:49.900 --> 01:14:53.430
So what is this
business of integration?

01:14:53.430 --> 01:14:54.855
How is it done properly?

01:14:54.855 --> 01:14:55.980
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:14:55.980 --> 01:14:56.560
Yeah.

01:14:56.560 --> 01:14:57.060
Gosh.

01:14:57.060 --> 01:15:00.015
Well, how long does
it last as well?

01:15:00.015 --> 01:15:01.620
A lifetime?

01:15:01.620 --> 01:15:02.580
You know?

01:15:02.580 --> 01:15:06.780
Life is a journey like
a trip is a journey.

01:15:06.780 --> 01:15:09.810
And there's always work to do.

01:15:09.810 --> 01:15:12.990
As Jack Kornfield says, after
the, ecstasy the laundry.

01:15:12.990 --> 01:15:14.190
And you know?

01:15:14.190 --> 01:15:14.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love that.

01:15:14.620 --> 01:15:15.640
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:15:15.640 --> 01:15:16.140
Yeah.

01:15:16.140 --> 01:15:19.350
There'll be other good ones
as well, but forget them.

01:15:19.350 --> 01:15:21.000
But yeah.

01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:27.000
So the work is
ongoing, and yeah.

01:15:27.000 --> 01:15:29.130
But this gives you a foot up.

01:15:29.130 --> 01:15:34.860
It enables people to do the work
more easily, and that's true.

01:15:34.860 --> 01:15:36.870
The classic psychedelics
is also true.

01:15:36.870 --> 01:15:41.970
Very true of MDMA therapy for
post-traumatic stress disorder.

01:15:41.970 --> 01:15:44.790
It's really giving you a
leg up, making it easier

01:15:44.790 --> 01:15:48.460
to do very, very difficult
work going back to a trauma.

01:15:48.460 --> 01:15:52.990
Trying to digest it,
process it, integrate it.

01:15:52.990 --> 01:15:56.800
So it's such an
essential component

01:15:56.800 --> 01:15:59.915
of the treatment model.

01:15:59.915 --> 01:16:03.700
But one has to be realistic
as well by saying, oh,

01:16:03.700 --> 01:16:05.530
integration lasts a lifetime.

01:16:05.530 --> 01:16:08.230
Well, people
delivering a service

01:16:08.230 --> 01:16:10.940
can't be there for a lifetime.

01:16:10.940 --> 01:16:13.990
So what's the answer there?

01:16:13.990 --> 01:16:19.830
And people are wrestling
with that issue right now.

01:16:19.830 --> 01:16:23.800
And I think one of the solutions
might be that it's, in a sense,

01:16:23.800 --> 01:16:27.370
on you to appoint.

01:16:27.370 --> 01:16:31.450
The therapeutic team
can treat you to a point

01:16:31.450 --> 01:16:34.780
and then it becomes what
you might call practice.

01:16:34.780 --> 01:16:38.510
In a similar way, that
meditation is a practice.

01:16:38.510 --> 01:16:40.640
It's something that
you have to keep up.

01:16:40.640 --> 01:16:44.325
And if it slips, then
things could slip.

01:16:44.325 --> 01:16:46.750
And that's the way it is.

01:16:46.750 --> 01:16:52.690
Or you have another
psychedelic treatment.

01:16:52.690 --> 01:16:54.970
So people have even used
this term of practice

01:16:54.970 --> 01:16:56.590
in relation to
psychedelics, where

01:16:56.590 --> 01:17:00.790
there's a psychedelic practice
like there's a meditation

01:17:00.790 --> 01:17:02.080
practice.

01:17:02.080 --> 01:17:07.540
But I'm using meditation
intentionally here because they

01:17:07.540 --> 01:17:15.100
actually think that
meditative practice, elements

01:17:15.100 --> 01:17:19.660
of spiritual practice could
be a very important complement

01:17:19.660 --> 01:17:23.480
to psychedelic therapy.

01:17:23.480 --> 01:17:26.320
And I think it's probably
doing something similar

01:17:26.320 --> 01:17:30.205
in terms of promoting
an ability to sit with--

01:17:32.740 --> 01:17:35.350
a former colleague of
mine said it quite well.

01:17:35.350 --> 01:17:36.940
In relation to
psychedelic therapy

01:17:36.940 --> 01:17:40.390
versus chronic
pharmacotherapy or like SSRIs

01:17:40.390 --> 01:17:41.590
being on them all the time.

01:17:41.590 --> 01:17:43.180
Says psychedelic
therapy allows you

01:17:43.180 --> 01:17:46.480
to sit with rather than sit on.

01:17:46.480 --> 01:17:48.190
And so that's quite good.

01:17:48.190 --> 01:17:49.120
Yeah.

01:17:49.120 --> 01:17:56.020
So the meditation, the
mindfulness, the ability to,

01:17:56.020 --> 01:17:58.840
yes, be present-centered
but also present-centered

01:17:58.840 --> 01:18:02.020
and accepting so
if things come up,

01:18:02.020 --> 01:18:06.040
you can watch and
process and then let go.

01:18:06.040 --> 01:18:09.376
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That
Holy Grail of mindfulness.

01:18:09.376 --> 01:18:12.151
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:18:12.151 --> 01:18:15.592
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Awareness
without reactivity, respond.

01:18:15.592 --> 01:18:17.050
I grew up in the
Bay Area and you'd

01:18:17.050 --> 01:18:18.520
hear this language, right?

01:18:18.520 --> 01:18:19.990
And I'm not being
disparaging this.

01:18:19.990 --> 01:18:23.080
I have friends that are on
the board of Esalen and work

01:18:23.080 --> 01:18:23.710
down there.

01:18:23.710 --> 01:18:25.890
And I've gone there.

01:18:25.890 --> 01:18:28.570
And yet you hear
these terms, right?

01:18:28.570 --> 01:18:31.720
Be responsive, not reactive,
which to a neuroscientist

01:18:31.720 --> 01:18:35.610
is like grates on me,
which probably just means

01:18:35.610 --> 01:18:37.170
I have issues.

01:18:37.170 --> 01:18:38.130
And surely, I do.

01:18:38.130 --> 01:18:40.590
But what does that mean?

01:18:40.590 --> 01:18:42.570
It's saying like, oh,
to be the observer

01:18:42.570 --> 01:18:44.880
but not be drawn
into the experience.

01:18:44.880 --> 01:18:47.500
And again, I don't want
to be overly reductionist,

01:18:47.500 --> 01:18:52.830
but what I find so compelling
about the emerging data,

01:18:52.830 --> 01:18:57.000
because it really is data,
on psychedelics as treatments

01:18:57.000 --> 01:19:00.510
for depression and trauma,
namely psilocybin and MDMA

01:19:00.510 --> 01:19:05.430
is that it really seems to
allow people this space that

01:19:05.430 --> 01:19:07.035
is so commonly thrown around.

01:19:07.035 --> 01:19:09.047
Giving space between
stimulus and reaction.

01:19:09.047 --> 01:19:10.380
Viktor Frankl talked about this.

01:19:10.380 --> 01:19:12.300
But I've been reading
a wonderful book

01:19:12.300 --> 01:19:13.657
called The Prince of Medicine.

01:19:13.657 --> 01:19:15.240
Dates back to the
origins of medicine.

01:19:15.240 --> 01:19:16.470
Very dense book.

01:19:16.470 --> 01:19:18.178
People have been
talking about this stuff

01:19:18.178 --> 01:19:21.570
and thinking about this
stuff for thousands of years.

01:19:21.570 --> 01:19:26.460
Psychedelics seem to give people
access to that better version

01:19:26.460 --> 01:19:28.500
of self, which is remarkable.

01:19:28.500 --> 01:19:31.177
What's also remarkable, it's,
perhaps, worth pointing out

01:19:31.177 --> 01:19:32.760
is that, five years
ago, I never would

01:19:32.760 --> 01:19:34.410
have been comfortable
having this conversation.

01:19:34.410 --> 01:19:36.077
I would have been
afraid to lose my job.

01:19:36.077 --> 01:19:39.660
Stanford Magazine, this week,
just published an entire issue

01:19:39.660 --> 01:19:42.690
about psychedelics with
how ketamine works, MDMA,

01:19:42.690 --> 01:19:45.510
psilocybin with the appropriate
cautionary notes in there.

01:19:45.510 --> 01:19:49.700
But clearly, times are changing.

01:19:49.700 --> 01:19:55.970
Speaking of which, I know you're
doing a trial on first-time use

01:19:55.970 --> 01:19:57.470
of psychedelics.

01:19:57.470 --> 01:19:59.240
What inspired that?

01:19:59.240 --> 01:20:01.370
And what are you observing?

01:20:01.370 --> 01:20:04.190
And as you tell us
that, please give us

01:20:04.190 --> 01:20:06.230
a few of the key contours.

01:20:06.230 --> 01:20:10.902
What's the dose, how
old are these subjects?

01:20:10.902 --> 01:20:12.110
I'm assuming it's men, women.

01:20:12.110 --> 01:20:14.525
Are they suffering
from depression or not?

01:20:14.525 --> 01:20:16.020
What's the landscape
of that study?

01:20:16.020 --> 01:20:19.033
And I realize this is still
early days of the study

01:20:19.033 --> 01:20:20.450
or maybe it's close
to completion.

01:20:20.450 --> 01:20:21.980
It's not yet published,
however, correct?

01:20:21.980 --> 01:20:24.522
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: It's not
published, it's not submitted,

01:20:24.522 --> 01:20:25.290
it is completed.

01:20:25.290 --> 01:20:29.120
So this was another one of
our COVID studies, in a sense,

01:20:29.120 --> 01:20:32.240
meaning COVID hit and we
had to finish the study.

01:20:32.240 --> 01:20:34.580
And it was hard to finish
the study because of COVID.

01:20:34.580 --> 01:20:38.360
That was true of our psilocybin
therapy versus escitalopram.

01:20:38.360 --> 01:20:43.010
Lexapro trial, which is
published in The New England

01:20:43.010 --> 01:20:44.120
Journal of Medicine.

01:20:44.120 --> 01:20:44.652
But the--

01:20:44.652 --> 01:20:46.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This was '20--
that paper, by the way, folks,

01:20:46.760 --> 01:20:48.950
we'll provide a link to
in the show note captions

01:20:48.950 --> 01:20:52.170
as well as some of
Robin's other papers.

01:20:52.170 --> 01:20:55.710
I think the 2022 New England
Journal paper is really

01:20:55.710 --> 01:20:59.460
fabulous given the different
dosages and the comparison

01:20:59.460 --> 01:21:00.960
to, essentially,
what is microdosing

01:21:00.960 --> 01:21:02.477
and the comparison
to citalopram.

01:21:02.477 --> 01:21:03.560
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Mhm.

01:21:03.560 --> 01:21:04.060
Yeah.

01:21:04.060 --> 01:21:06.090
That's interesting
that you link the way

01:21:06.090 --> 01:21:08.850
we gave small doses of
psilocybin to microdosing.

01:21:08.850 --> 01:21:10.500
We didn't think of it that way.

01:21:10.500 --> 01:21:14.970
We thought it was just
a necessary placebo

01:21:14.970 --> 01:21:18.900
for the big dose,
the 25 milligrams.

01:21:18.900 --> 01:21:19.920
Yeah.

01:21:19.920 --> 01:21:22.650
So that we could say to everyone
we're giving you psilocybin

01:21:22.650 --> 01:21:23.950
and not be lying.

01:21:23.950 --> 01:21:24.450
Yeah.

01:21:24.450 --> 01:21:28.500
For those who got escitalopram,
Lexapro for six weeks,

01:21:28.500 --> 01:21:30.810
they got a very, very
low dose of psilocybin.

01:21:30.810 --> 01:21:33.450
But it allowed us to standardize
all the psychotherapy and so

01:21:33.450 --> 01:21:33.970
on.

01:21:33.970 --> 01:21:37.380
But the other study
that you're referring to

01:21:37.380 --> 01:21:40.170
was in healthy volunteers.

01:21:40.170 --> 01:21:41.670
Middle-aged.

01:21:41.670 --> 01:21:43.590
Average age, I think, was 40.

01:21:43.590 --> 01:21:46.140
So not your typical
student study

01:21:46.140 --> 01:21:50.145
that is so often the case
in psychology research.

01:21:50.145 --> 01:21:54.100
All the undergrads end up
volunteering for your study.

01:21:54.100 --> 01:21:59.010
So this is more of an
age range and also--

01:21:59.010 --> 01:22:03.000
I think it was an equal
proportion of male and female.

01:22:03.000 --> 01:22:04.800
All the staff
actually were female,

01:22:04.800 --> 01:22:06.517
which the staff were
very proud of them.

01:22:06.517 --> 01:22:08.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Although,
it produces its own potential

01:22:08.850 --> 01:22:09.930
confound, right?

01:22:09.930 --> 01:22:13.318
To become all one sex of staff.

01:22:13.318 --> 01:22:14.610
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Possibly.

01:22:14.610 --> 01:22:15.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:22:15.090 --> 01:22:17.340
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: They
did a good job in the sense

01:22:17.340 --> 01:22:22.050
that we saw significant
improvements in well-being

01:22:22.050 --> 01:22:23.340
at the end of the trial.

01:22:23.340 --> 01:22:25.360
So let me describe the design.

01:22:25.360 --> 01:22:29.730
It was a repeated measures
design, meaning people come in,

01:22:29.730 --> 01:22:33.690
you collect your baseline
data and do a brain scan,

01:22:33.690 --> 01:22:36.540
and you give people a placebo.

01:22:36.540 --> 01:22:37.740
We gave people a placebo.

01:22:37.740 --> 01:22:39.700
And actually let me
rewind a little bit.

01:22:39.700 --> 01:22:42.690
Everyone's healthy volunteers,
middle-aged never taken

01:22:42.690 --> 01:22:44.160
a psychedelic in their life.

01:22:44.160 --> 01:22:45.390
None of them.

01:22:45.390 --> 01:22:50.354
Entirely fresh, virgin
people coming in.

01:22:50.354 --> 01:22:53.790
And the plan is to give them
their first ever psychedelic

01:22:53.790 --> 01:22:54.400
experience.

01:22:54.400 --> 01:22:55.980
So that's what we
did in this study.

01:22:55.980 --> 01:22:59.070
But to do it, we have this
repeated measures design where

01:22:59.070 --> 01:23:01.350
they'll first get a placebo.

01:23:01.350 --> 01:23:03.480
And we have the
placebo so that we

01:23:03.480 --> 01:23:07.200
can do all the procedures,
all the therapy, all the music

01:23:07.200 --> 01:23:11.040
listening but not give a
whopping dose of psilocybin.

01:23:11.040 --> 01:23:13.740
Again, we gave them a
placebo dose of psilocybin.

01:23:13.740 --> 01:23:14.880
1 milligram.

01:23:14.880 --> 01:23:18.960
We stick EEG headsets
on during the experience

01:23:18.960 --> 01:23:24.510
to record the brain activity
from the scalp, the oscillating

01:23:24.510 --> 01:23:25.680
electrical activity.

01:23:25.680 --> 01:23:31.110
And we do the MRI scanning
before and after to see deeper

01:23:31.110 --> 01:23:34.530
into the brain, and we can look
at the functional connectivity

01:23:34.530 --> 01:23:37.500
that we were referring to
earlier, and also properties

01:23:37.500 --> 01:23:40.540
of brain anatomy, which
we did in this study.

01:23:40.540 --> 01:23:45.090
So the short story is that
all of the changes that we

01:23:45.090 --> 01:23:50.310
saw both psychologically
and neurobiologically

01:23:50.310 --> 01:23:52.710
were seen with
the 25 milligrams.

01:23:52.710 --> 01:23:55.440
It all happened with
that big whopping dose.

01:23:55.440 --> 01:23:56.980
And what did we see?

01:23:56.980 --> 01:23:59.430
Well, we did see
significant improvements

01:23:59.430 --> 01:24:01.710
in psychological well-being.

01:24:01.710 --> 01:24:06.060
We saw what I call the entropic
brain effect, which is actually

01:24:06.060 --> 01:24:08.040
formerly quite accurate.

01:24:08.040 --> 01:24:11.850
We see an increase in the
informational complexity

01:24:11.850 --> 01:24:16.800
of ongoing brain activity
recorded with the EEG

01:24:16.800 --> 01:24:19.780
on the dose of psilocybin.

01:24:19.780 --> 01:24:21.930
The activity becomes
more complex.

01:24:21.930 --> 01:24:23.770
It's harder to
predict across time.

01:24:23.770 --> 01:24:25.980
It's more informationally rich.

01:24:25.980 --> 01:24:28.350
And that effect
correlates as it does

01:24:28.350 --> 01:24:31.720
very reliably with the magnitude
of the subjective effect.

01:24:31.720 --> 01:24:35.040
So the bigger the trip, the
bigger this entropic brain

01:24:35.040 --> 01:24:36.240
effect.

01:24:36.240 --> 01:24:40.260
Now, pretty well
replicated finding.

01:24:40.260 --> 01:24:44.790
But then the MRI, seeing
deep into the brain

01:24:44.790 --> 01:24:48.390
was probably our
most exciting result

01:24:48.390 --> 01:24:52.610
where we didn't just see some
functional brain changes,

01:24:52.610 --> 01:24:56.190
but we've seen some anatomical
brain changes as well.

01:24:56.190 --> 01:24:59.870
And we used a technique called
diffusion tensor imaging that

01:24:59.870 --> 01:25:03.920
looks at the cabling of the
brain, the white matter tracts.

01:25:03.920 --> 01:25:06.905
And we saw a change
in major tracts.

01:25:06.905 --> 01:25:12.290
So we limited our search
space to really thick tracts.

01:25:12.290 --> 01:25:13.940
Really thick fibers.

01:25:13.940 --> 01:25:17.030
And the fibers that
came through as changing

01:25:17.030 --> 01:25:21.320
were ones that traveled
between the prefrontal cortex

01:25:21.320 --> 01:25:25.280
and the thalamus
and the striatum.

01:25:25.280 --> 01:25:29.480
There were two tracks, two
prefrontal tracks that changed.

01:25:29.480 --> 01:25:31.670
And they changed in the
direction of a decrease

01:25:31.670 --> 01:25:37.670
in axial diffusivity,
which could be interpreted

01:25:37.670 --> 01:25:41.090
as tract integrity where
a decrease would be

01:25:41.090 --> 01:25:43.850
an increase in tract integrity.

01:25:43.850 --> 01:25:46.880
It is something that you
see in the developing brain.

01:25:46.880 --> 01:25:50.930
The axial diffusivity
decreases as the brain goes

01:25:50.930 --> 01:25:54.170
from being a baby
to being an adult.

01:25:54.170 --> 01:25:56.570
Axial diffusivity goes down.

01:25:56.570 --> 01:25:59.750
And then in aging and
pathologies of aging,

01:25:59.750 --> 01:26:03.090
axial diffusivity goes up.

01:26:03.090 --> 01:26:05.525
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This is
in the opposite direction

01:26:05.525 --> 01:26:07.790
of the results you talked
about earlier in terms

01:26:07.790 --> 01:26:10.490
of brain connectivity
of increased

01:26:10.490 --> 01:26:11.870
communication across areas.

01:26:11.870 --> 01:26:13.850
If I understand
correctly, and I'm

01:26:13.850 --> 01:26:16.820
perfectly happy to be wrong,
by the way, that this decrease

01:26:16.820 --> 01:26:21.950
in axial diffusivity
translates to a higher

01:26:21.950 --> 01:26:25.130
fidelity of communication
between the prefrontal cortex

01:26:25.130 --> 01:26:29.000
and the thalamus and
striatum as opposed to less.

01:26:29.000 --> 01:26:30.920
And your description
of this as somewhat

01:26:30.920 --> 01:26:35.510
like the transition from
babyhood and childhood

01:26:35.510 --> 01:26:37.280
to adulthood.

01:26:37.280 --> 01:26:39.350
Speaks to the same where
we know that there's

01:26:39.350 --> 01:26:42.110
a massive culling of
connections as opposed

01:26:42.110 --> 01:26:43.332
to growth of connections.

01:26:43.332 --> 01:26:44.790
So in other words,
as we get older,

01:26:44.790 --> 01:26:46.332
we get better at
doing certain things

01:26:46.332 --> 01:26:49.430
and less good at doing
potentially most everything

01:26:49.430 --> 01:26:50.090
else.

01:26:50.090 --> 01:26:51.647
Is that right?

01:26:51.647 --> 01:26:52.730
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Ish.

01:26:52.730 --> 01:26:57.500
Because the change was
anatomical and not functional.

01:26:57.500 --> 01:27:03.170
So the other stuff was is
really measuring communication

01:27:03.170 --> 01:27:07.490
in the brain by looking at
how the activity fluctuates

01:27:07.490 --> 01:27:11.990
across time and whether those
fluctuations in activity

01:27:11.990 --> 01:27:13.770
are synchronous between regions.

01:27:13.770 --> 01:27:16.740
And if they are, we say
they're functionally connected.

01:27:16.740 --> 01:27:18.740
And we infer that they're
talking to each other,

01:27:18.740 --> 01:27:21.470
because they go up
and down in synchrony.

01:27:21.470 --> 01:27:23.150
But when it comes
to the anatomy,

01:27:23.150 --> 01:27:28.160
we're talking about the
just static material stuff.

01:27:28.160 --> 01:27:35.300
And so we're seeing the fibers
and a property of the fibers

01:27:35.300 --> 01:27:37.370
change.

01:27:37.370 --> 01:27:39.320
At least that's what we think.

01:27:39.320 --> 01:27:42.290
And recently, we had
an independent person

01:27:42.290 --> 01:27:45.650
come in and reanalyze the data,
because one of those things,

01:27:45.650 --> 01:27:52.020
incredible finding
requires credible evidence,

01:27:52.020 --> 01:27:53.160
really strong evidence.

01:27:53.160 --> 01:27:56.580
And I would say, the evidence
at the moment is one study.

01:27:56.580 --> 01:27:58.590
So we need to be
cautious on that.

01:27:58.590 --> 01:28:03.210
But we did re-analyze it
and use this correction

01:28:03.210 --> 01:28:08.070
procedure-free water correction
to be more sure that it

01:28:08.070 --> 01:28:11.190
was a change in the
actual microstructure

01:28:11.190 --> 01:28:14.740
rather than something to do
with the extracellular space.

01:28:14.740 --> 01:28:17.370
The water surrounding
the fibers.

01:28:17.370 --> 01:28:18.900
And it came through.

01:28:18.900 --> 01:28:21.690
In fact, the change
was strengthened

01:28:21.690 --> 01:28:23.490
by doing this correction step.

01:28:23.490 --> 01:28:26.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
this is neuroplasticity

01:28:26.460 --> 01:28:30.430
as the consequence of one
first time session with 25

01:28:30.430 --> 01:28:31.545
milligrams of psilocybin.

01:28:31.545 --> 01:28:32.670
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:28:32.670 --> 01:28:33.390
Yeah.

01:28:33.390 --> 01:28:35.550
So we're excited and the two--

01:28:35.550 --> 01:28:36.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Understandably so.

01:28:36.030 --> 01:28:38.613
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: --the two
different-- the second analyst

01:28:38.613 --> 01:28:41.220
coming in wasn't
sure she believed it

01:28:41.220 --> 01:28:44.550
and then she thought this
correction technique might

01:28:44.550 --> 01:28:47.760
kill the result and then it came
through, and she's like, OK.

01:28:47.760 --> 01:28:49.320
Now, I'm excited too.

01:28:49.320 --> 01:28:50.740
So we'll see.

01:28:50.740 --> 01:28:51.930
We don't know what it means.

01:28:51.930 --> 01:28:53.460
What does it mean functionally?

01:28:53.460 --> 01:28:54.390
We don't know.

01:28:54.390 --> 01:28:55.980
How did the people change?

01:28:55.980 --> 01:29:00.330
Well, psychologically, as I
said, well-being improved.

01:29:00.330 --> 01:29:03.090
We did look at their
cognition, and we

01:29:03.090 --> 01:29:06.240
used a cognitive
flexibility paradigm that

01:29:06.240 --> 01:29:10.740
looks at people's ability
to notice a rule change

01:29:10.740 --> 01:29:15.240
and then flexibly adapt their
behavior based on noticing

01:29:15.240 --> 01:29:18.607
this rule change and people
improved after the 25

01:29:18.607 --> 01:29:21.780
milligrams and didn't
significantly improve

01:29:21.780 --> 01:29:23.790
after the placebo dose.

01:29:23.790 --> 01:29:29.070
There weren't correlations with
the DTI change, the cabling

01:29:29.070 --> 01:29:31.440
change and these
psychological outcomes.

01:29:31.440 --> 01:29:34.800
But with these studies
and smaller sample sizes,

01:29:34.800 --> 01:29:38.980
you don't always see those
correlations come through.

01:29:38.980 --> 01:29:39.840
So it's something.

01:29:39.840 --> 01:29:41.007
We don't know what it means.

01:29:41.007 --> 01:29:45.120
But it's a change
in brain anatomy

01:29:45.120 --> 01:29:47.640
that's in the opposite
direction to what

01:29:47.640 --> 01:29:51.990
you see in an aging brain
or with pathology of aging.

01:29:51.990 --> 01:29:54.240
And it's what you see
in a healthy brain

01:29:54.240 --> 01:29:57.660
as it goes from normal
neurodevelopment

01:29:57.660 --> 01:29:59.010
into adulthood.

01:29:59.010 --> 01:30:01.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Very, very
exciting and intriguing.

01:30:01.380 --> 01:30:03.030
And I appreciate
that you highlighted

01:30:03.030 --> 01:30:06.030
that it's just one study,
although from everything you've

01:30:06.030 --> 01:30:08.460
said, it sounds like it's
been done with immense rigor.

01:30:08.460 --> 01:30:12.330
So we will eagerly await the
publication of that study

01:30:12.330 --> 01:30:16.140
so we can peruse all the data
and the subsequent studies.

01:30:16.140 --> 01:30:19.260
I want to hear a
bit about the study

01:30:19.260 --> 01:30:22.770
that you have been carrying
out on the use of psilocybin

01:30:22.770 --> 01:30:25.300
for the treatment
of fibromyalgia.

01:30:25.300 --> 01:30:28.410
I'm intrigued by
fibromyalgia, because I

01:30:28.410 --> 01:30:30.360
have a good friend who also--

01:30:30.360 --> 01:30:31.660
I won't reveal who it is.

01:30:31.660 --> 01:30:32.160
No.

01:30:32.160 --> 01:30:32.760
It's not me.

01:30:32.760 --> 01:30:34.385
This isn't the "I
have a friend thing."

01:30:34.385 --> 01:30:37.890
Who also is a scientist who
sits at a fairly high position

01:30:37.890 --> 01:30:41.760
in the National Institutes
of Health who quietly

01:30:41.760 --> 01:30:45.030
has expressed to me
that they are incredibly

01:30:45.030 --> 01:30:49.410
frustrated with the fact that
the standard medical community

01:30:49.410 --> 01:30:53.340
has largely ignored
fibromyalgia.

01:30:53.340 --> 01:30:56.610
And that for many years, it was
lumped with things like chronic

01:30:56.610 --> 01:30:59.550
fatigue syndrome and
other so-called--

01:30:59.550 --> 01:31:00.300
again, so-called.

01:31:00.300 --> 01:31:01.080
I'm not saying this.

01:31:01.080 --> 01:31:02.705
But people often
refer to these as, oh.

01:31:02.705 --> 01:31:03.570
It's psychosomatic.

01:31:03.570 --> 01:31:04.260
That's all in your head.

01:31:04.260 --> 01:31:07.050
Which, as a neuroscientist, is
a ridiculous statement to hear,

01:31:07.050 --> 01:31:08.370
because it's all in your head.

01:31:08.370 --> 01:31:10.020
Your brain is in
your head after all.

01:31:10.020 --> 01:31:12.780
Your physiology
and your psychology

01:31:12.780 --> 01:31:14.220
are influencing each other.

01:31:14.220 --> 01:31:14.880
Of course.

01:31:14.880 --> 01:31:17.520
And the world is starting
to appreciate that more.

01:31:17.520 --> 01:31:19.710
But first of all,
maybe you could

01:31:19.710 --> 01:31:22.290
tell people what
fibromyalgia is,

01:31:22.290 --> 01:31:25.560
what inspired you to do
a study on fibromyalgia

01:31:25.560 --> 01:31:28.980
using psilocybin of all
things because that's

01:31:28.980 --> 01:31:30.000
surprising to me.

01:31:30.000 --> 01:31:34.020
And if you are allowed
to or if you have access

01:31:34.020 --> 01:31:37.600
to the data in
mind, share with us

01:31:37.600 --> 01:31:39.975
a little bit about what you're
discovering in that study.

01:31:39.975 --> 01:31:40.800
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Sure.

01:31:40.800 --> 01:31:41.040
Yeah.

01:31:41.040 --> 01:31:41.640
Happy to.

01:31:41.640 --> 01:31:45.510
So again, it's psilocybin
therapy and the population

01:31:45.510 --> 01:31:47.110
is fibromyalgia syndrome.

01:31:47.110 --> 01:31:49.110
So this is people
presenting with

01:31:49.110 --> 01:31:51.720
a generalized chronic pain.

01:31:51.720 --> 01:31:53.690
So unlike some
other pain disorders

01:31:53.690 --> 01:31:55.790
where the pain is focused.

01:31:55.790 --> 01:32:00.230
You can say, it's my lower
back, which is very common.

01:32:00.230 --> 01:32:01.940
Chronic lower back pain.

01:32:01.940 --> 01:32:04.220
This is more generalized.

01:32:04.220 --> 01:32:09.530
And for that reason, it's
hard to know what it is.

01:32:09.530 --> 01:32:13.640
And that's why it's been a
controversial space in medicine

01:32:13.640 --> 01:32:15.110
and it's been-- yeah.

01:32:15.110 --> 01:32:17.300
It's had that charge thrown
at it that maybe it's

01:32:17.300 --> 01:32:19.970
psychosomatic, and
just to your point,

01:32:19.970 --> 01:32:25.760
is anything ever independent
of the mind, anyway.

01:32:25.760 --> 01:32:28.130
But this is actually
a fascinating space

01:32:28.130 --> 01:32:32.810
for how subjective experience,
the lived experience,

01:32:32.810 --> 01:32:35.210
and the mind can
influence the body.

01:32:35.210 --> 01:32:38.210
Because there's some really
interesting literature

01:32:38.210 --> 01:32:42.770
around the etiology.

01:32:42.770 --> 01:32:46.430
How the pain has come about.

01:32:46.430 --> 01:32:49.010
In a sense like what
caused the pain.

01:32:49.010 --> 01:32:50.780
What's the story there?

01:32:50.780 --> 01:32:53.740
And the head of the trial, I
would say to my colleagues,

01:32:53.740 --> 01:32:54.840
let's just be careful.

01:32:54.840 --> 01:32:57.690
Because there is some
fascinating literature

01:32:57.690 --> 01:33:01.440
around things like a
background of trauma

01:33:01.440 --> 01:33:10.590
and how that can relate to
issues related to inflammation

01:33:10.590 --> 01:33:12.510
and how that can
express into things

01:33:12.510 --> 01:33:15.690
like fibromyalgia syndrome.

01:33:15.690 --> 01:33:17.670
I just said, be
very careful there,

01:33:17.670 --> 01:33:19.500
because if you go in
with an assumption

01:33:19.500 --> 01:33:21.720
that there's some buried
trauma, for example,

01:33:21.720 --> 01:33:23.700
then there's that
whole other side

01:33:23.700 --> 01:33:26.880
of psychoanalysis that
massively tripped it up

01:33:26.880 --> 01:33:28.560
around false memory and so on.

01:33:28.560 --> 01:33:32.010
And so please don't
hold prior assumptions

01:33:32.010 --> 01:33:36.210
that you're going to uncover
buried trauma in every case.

01:33:36.210 --> 01:33:38.700
Now, the team of
treated, I think,

01:33:38.700 --> 01:33:44.700
eight people and it
is going very well.

01:33:44.700 --> 01:33:48.390
Again, I just want to
be careful with how

01:33:48.390 --> 01:33:52.470
I describe it to manage
expectations and not

01:33:52.470 --> 01:33:54.000
get too carried away.

01:33:54.000 --> 01:33:56.550
But I check in with
the team every week,

01:33:56.550 --> 01:34:00.750
and they're still based
in London doing the work.

01:34:00.750 --> 01:34:09.870
And it's remarkable what I hear
about the profound experiences

01:34:09.870 --> 01:34:11.490
that people have under the drug.

01:34:11.490 --> 01:34:13.950
In this study, we
only give one dose.

01:34:13.950 --> 01:34:15.660
It's a very mechanistic study.

01:34:15.660 --> 01:34:19.680
We actually have the EEG
cap on in the sessions

01:34:19.680 --> 01:34:21.210
like in the healthy
volunteer study,

01:34:21.210 --> 01:34:25.810
but this time now taking it
into a clinical population.

01:34:25.810 --> 01:34:26.550
And--

01:34:26.550 --> 01:34:31.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So they
are wearing an eye mask

01:34:31.500 --> 01:34:34.950
under the influence of 25
milligrams of psilocybin.

01:34:34.950 --> 01:34:38.280
Most of them probably have
not done psilocybin before.

01:34:38.280 --> 01:34:41.890
So it's a little bit like the
first time study in some sense.

01:34:41.890 --> 01:34:49.340
They have fibromyalgia that's
debilitating in some way.

01:34:49.340 --> 01:34:51.230
They don't want it, obviously.

01:34:51.230 --> 01:34:55.250
And during the session, are
they thinking about their pain?

01:34:55.250 --> 01:34:57.215
Are they being told to
think about their pain?

01:34:57.215 --> 01:34:57.770
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
They're not

01:34:57.770 --> 01:34:59.790
being told to think
about the pain.

01:34:59.790 --> 01:35:05.240
In fact, as I understand it,
while there is a therapeutic

01:35:05.240 --> 01:35:10.130
model around acceptance
of the pain, it isn't--

01:35:10.130 --> 01:35:11.930
unlike some of
the PTSD work, you

01:35:11.930 --> 01:35:16.910
aren't encouraging them to
focus on the index trauma

01:35:16.910 --> 01:35:20.270
and then work through it
and try and digest it.

01:35:20.270 --> 01:35:22.280
We don't do that with the pain.

01:35:22.280 --> 01:35:26.720
So the pain is there, but
there isn't an invitation

01:35:26.720 --> 01:35:27.660
to focus on it.

01:35:27.660 --> 01:35:29.410
And that's probably
one of the differences

01:35:29.410 --> 01:35:33.440
with classic psychedelic
therapy versus MDMA therapy.

01:35:33.440 --> 01:35:38.300
Arguably, MDMA therapy is
more like it's a bit closer

01:35:38.300 --> 01:35:42.110
to traditional talk therapy
where there is more dialogue.

01:35:42.110 --> 01:35:45.000
People are able to talk on MDMA.

01:35:45.000 --> 01:35:47.974
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In
the MDMA trials, do you

01:35:47.974 --> 01:35:53.330
know whether or not
they used eye masks or--

01:35:53.330 --> 01:35:55.820
because this seems to be
an important distinction

01:35:55.820 --> 01:35:58.640
between, as you described,
the therapeutic trip

01:35:58.640 --> 01:36:01.760
versus the trip that one
does going into the woods

01:36:01.760 --> 01:36:05.300
and taking psilocybin in
the woods or at a party

01:36:05.300 --> 01:36:12.110
or while staring at
a poster or a leaf.

01:36:12.110 --> 01:36:14.360
Again, I'm not trying to
trivialize those experiences.

01:36:14.360 --> 01:36:16.970
I mean, obviously,
they can be profound.

01:36:16.970 --> 01:36:18.590
So I'm told.

01:36:18.590 --> 01:36:23.720
But the MDMA trials
seem to involve,

01:36:23.720 --> 01:36:28.250
as you said, more
directed dialogue.

01:36:28.250 --> 01:36:30.673
And sometimes, even
empathic connection

01:36:30.673 --> 01:36:32.090
between people by
they're actually

01:36:32.090 --> 01:36:35.630
looking at one another,
the eyes and eye contact

01:36:35.630 --> 01:36:39.770
being such a key part of
the human social cognitive

01:36:39.770 --> 01:36:42.170
connective networks.

01:36:42.170 --> 01:36:46.188
So do you know if they
put eye masks on people

01:36:46.188 --> 01:36:47.480
during the therapeutic session?

01:36:47.480 --> 01:36:49.438
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
I'm pretty sure that they

01:36:49.438 --> 01:36:50.610
have the eye masks there.

01:36:50.610 --> 01:36:51.568
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:36:51.568 --> 01:36:53.760
Because a lot of
the MDMA work, and I

01:36:53.760 --> 01:36:56.610
was part of an MDMA trial.

01:36:56.610 --> 01:37:00.240
Was, as I understand
geared toward developing,

01:37:00.240 --> 01:37:02.865
because it's an empathogen
empathy toward the self.

01:37:02.865 --> 01:37:03.990
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:37:03.990 --> 01:37:06.840
I'm pretty sure they
have the eye masks there,

01:37:06.840 --> 01:37:09.670
but they probably-- and
it's a great question,

01:37:09.670 --> 01:37:11.460
because you could
formally test this.

01:37:11.460 --> 01:37:13.185
It probably don't
use them as much.

01:37:13.185 --> 01:37:13.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Mhm.

01:37:13.740 --> 01:37:15.865
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: The
thing is with the classic

01:37:15.865 --> 01:37:17.820
psychedelics, if you're
looking at your guides

01:37:17.820 --> 01:37:21.150
or your facilitators and
their faces are melting or--

01:37:21.150 --> 01:37:22.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
On MDMA, you just

01:37:22.890 --> 01:37:25.410
might really start to feel
more connected to you.

01:37:25.410 --> 01:37:25.590
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:37:25.590 --> 01:37:27.885
They might look especially
beautiful and you know--

01:37:27.885 --> 01:37:28.802
ANDREW HUBERMAN: sure.

01:37:28.802 --> 01:37:29.355
Yeah.

01:37:29.355 --> 01:37:30.647
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: And yeah.

01:37:30.647 --> 01:37:35.850
There's that fascinating
effect of loving the people

01:37:35.850 --> 01:37:37.740
that you're with.

01:37:37.740 --> 01:37:38.970
And so yeah.

01:37:38.970 --> 01:37:42.480
I imagine they talk more
and use the eye shades less.

01:37:42.480 --> 01:37:46.620
And it is more interpersonal
rather than like intrapersonal

01:37:46.620 --> 01:37:48.180
or going inside.

01:37:48.180 --> 01:37:50.400
They do use a
fascinating terminology

01:37:50.400 --> 01:37:54.480
that some people have critiqued,
but it is a very interesting

01:37:54.480 --> 01:37:55.260
phenomenon.

01:37:55.260 --> 01:37:57.630
And it's this notion
of the inner healer.

01:37:57.630 --> 01:37:59.910
They use that language a lot.

01:37:59.910 --> 01:38:03.450
It's been critiqued because
it sounds very suggestive.

01:38:03.450 --> 01:38:04.050
You know?

01:38:04.050 --> 01:38:06.090
And that's probably one
of the vehicles here.

01:38:06.090 --> 01:38:08.820
Driving the therapeutic
process is suggestion.

01:38:08.820 --> 01:38:10.590
I think we have to
be honest about that.

01:38:14.130 --> 01:38:16.890
So when they go inside,
that's another term

01:38:16.890 --> 01:38:19.860
that we use very much in the
classic psychedelic therapy

01:38:19.860 --> 01:38:20.610
work.

01:38:20.610 --> 01:38:21.780
You go inside.

01:38:21.780 --> 01:38:23.910
You put the eyeshades
on, and people

01:38:23.910 --> 01:38:27.240
are encouraged to go inside.

01:38:27.240 --> 01:38:29.760
But when they do that
in the MDMA work,

01:38:29.760 --> 01:38:33.060
especially, they might
be told explicitly

01:38:33.060 --> 01:38:36.790
and listen to the inner
healer and that language.

01:38:36.790 --> 01:38:39.990
So you could see how
a cynic or a skeptic

01:38:39.990 --> 01:38:43.650
could come in and see that as
some kind of like suggestive

01:38:43.650 --> 01:38:45.810
priming or biasing.

01:38:45.810 --> 01:38:48.990
I think they have a point.

01:38:48.990 --> 01:38:54.510
Skeptics often do, but I don't
think it's all of the story.

01:38:54.510 --> 01:38:58.650
And just briefly, because
it's an interesting point,

01:38:58.650 --> 01:39:02.700
speaking to that point
a bit in our psilocybin

01:39:02.700 --> 01:39:05.250
therapy versus
escitalopram trial,

01:39:05.250 --> 01:39:07.950
we measured
pre-trial expectancy.

01:39:07.950 --> 01:39:10.810
And we did it for
both conditions.

01:39:10.810 --> 01:39:13.680
So what kind of
improvement do you

01:39:13.680 --> 01:39:16.290
expect with the Lexapro,
the escitalopram

01:39:16.290 --> 01:39:17.400
at the end of the trial?

01:39:17.400 --> 01:39:20.910
And what kind of improvement if
you go into the psilocybin arm

01:39:20.910 --> 01:39:23.580
and get two big
doses of psilocybin.

01:39:23.580 --> 01:39:28.240
What kind of improvement do you
think you'll see in that arm?

01:39:28.240 --> 01:39:30.720
And of course, it
was a coin flip

01:39:30.720 --> 01:39:36.030
as to what arm people went into
and there was no crossover.

01:39:36.030 --> 01:39:41.820
What we found was that it was
true that we had a sample bias,

01:39:41.820 --> 01:39:44.550
so most people had higher
expectation stations.

01:39:44.550 --> 01:39:47.520
On average, there were higher
expectations for psilocybin

01:39:47.520 --> 01:39:50.790
and its efficacy
or effectiveness

01:39:50.790 --> 01:39:54.330
versus the SSRI, the Lexapro.

01:39:54.330 --> 01:39:57.570
However, when we looked
at the correlation

01:39:57.570 --> 01:40:01.290
or the predictive relationship
between pre-trial expectancy

01:40:01.290 --> 01:40:04.890
and response, we saw
that pre-trial expectancy

01:40:04.890 --> 01:40:07.770
for the escitalopram
predicted response

01:40:07.770 --> 01:40:12.930
to escitalopram across virtually
every single measure, all

01:40:12.930 --> 01:40:15.960
these different measures
of depression and anxiety

01:40:15.960 --> 01:40:20.400
and well-being, and I
think none of the scales.

01:40:20.400 --> 01:40:25.080
I'm pretty sure it was none of
about 12 or so mental health

01:40:25.080 --> 01:40:26.550
rating scales.

01:40:26.550 --> 01:40:29.970
Was there a relationship
between pre-trial expectancy?

01:40:29.970 --> 01:40:33.330
Even though it was high,
it didn't predict--

01:40:33.330 --> 01:40:36.450
pre-trial expectancy
didn't predict response

01:40:36.450 --> 01:40:38.230
to the psilocybin therapy.

01:40:38.230 --> 01:40:44.200
So that was a bit of a smash
on the head for the idea

01:40:44.200 --> 01:40:47.710
that the classic
psychedelic therapy is

01:40:47.710 --> 01:40:50.030
some kind of placebo response.

01:40:50.030 --> 01:40:53.560
And I think it's so important
to address that question.

01:40:53.560 --> 01:40:57.550
Because if it
doesn't come through

01:40:57.550 --> 01:41:00.700
as it didn't come
through, then it

01:41:00.700 --> 01:41:04.870
opens up even more intrigue
about, well, what is it then?

01:41:04.870 --> 01:41:08.260
If it's not just a placebo
response or a super placebo

01:41:08.260 --> 01:41:12.710
response like an amplification
of the placebo response,

01:41:12.710 --> 01:41:14.170
then it must be something else.

01:41:14.170 --> 01:41:15.220
And how intriguing?

01:41:15.220 --> 01:41:18.040
It has a direct
therapeutic action.

01:41:18.040 --> 01:41:19.870
It must be something.

01:41:19.870 --> 01:41:22.090
And we don't yet
know what it is.

01:41:22.090 --> 01:41:25.720
I talked about the
residual increase

01:41:25.720 --> 01:41:27.010
in global connectivity.

01:41:27.010 --> 01:41:28.720
That's one possibility.

01:41:28.720 --> 01:41:32.875
But the truth is, we're
just scratching the surface.

01:41:32.875 --> 01:41:35.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And yet
the therapeutic outcomes

01:41:35.110 --> 01:41:39.850
are, again, just so
marvelously impressive.

01:41:39.850 --> 01:41:42.550
I'm curious as to why as--

01:41:42.550 --> 01:41:44.110
well, there are that many labs.

01:41:44.110 --> 01:41:47.650
But the laboratories that are
focused on classic psychedelics

01:41:47.650 --> 01:41:49.930
for the treatment of
depression and now as you

01:41:49.930 --> 01:41:52.910
mentioned, promising results
for anorexia and fibromyalgia

01:41:52.910 --> 01:41:53.410
as well.

01:41:53.410 --> 01:41:56.230
Although preliminary
very promising.

01:41:56.230 --> 01:42:01.570
Why the lack of
attention toward LSD?

01:42:01.570 --> 01:42:05.470
Is it that the LSD
trips are just too long?

01:42:05.470 --> 01:42:09.790
Is it that they are
qualitatively different?

01:42:09.790 --> 01:42:14.122
Are there any data on
non-microdoses of LSD?

01:42:14.122 --> 01:42:15.580
And here I want to
be very careful,

01:42:15.580 --> 01:42:18.880
because I learned through my
interactions on social media

01:42:18.880 --> 01:42:22.040
that this term microdose
is very misleading.

01:42:22.040 --> 01:42:24.370
And in some cases, can be
dangerously misleading,

01:42:24.370 --> 01:42:25.810
because, as you
mentioned earlier,

01:42:25.810 --> 01:42:31.270
the effective
psychedelic dose or the--

01:42:31.270 --> 01:42:32.740
effective meaning
that can induce

01:42:32.740 --> 01:42:37.330
a real trip with hallucinations,
et cetera, of LSD

01:42:37.330 --> 01:42:41.840
is actually in the
microgram range.

01:42:41.840 --> 01:42:45.220
So some people hear microdose,
and they think microgram of LSD

01:42:45.220 --> 01:42:49.090
is a-- micrograms is a
microdose when, in fact,

01:42:49.090 --> 01:42:53.080
a macrodose of LSD can be
measured in micrograms, right?

01:42:53.080 --> 01:42:56.650
So this is where in the
absence of scientific training,

01:42:56.650 --> 01:42:59.500
people can really go astray.

01:42:59.500 --> 01:43:01.450
Or even just in lack
of understanding

01:43:01.450 --> 01:43:02.500
of the metric system.

01:43:02.500 --> 01:43:07.690
And since now you're a
recent arrival to the US,

01:43:07.690 --> 01:43:08.660
fortunate for us.

01:43:08.660 --> 01:43:09.160
Sorry.

01:43:09.160 --> 01:43:12.850
England's loss is
the US's gain by--

01:43:12.850 --> 01:43:16.900
Robbins Lab moved from England
to the United States recently.

01:43:16.900 --> 01:43:19.330
So score 1 for us.

01:43:19.330 --> 01:43:24.192
But why isn't there more
use of LSD in these trials?

01:43:24.192 --> 01:43:25.900
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
I think it probably

01:43:25.900 --> 01:43:27.680
is the duration of the trip.

01:43:27.680 --> 01:43:31.850
It used to be stigma
and it was easier

01:43:31.850 --> 01:43:34.460
to get your psilocybin
study through,

01:43:34.460 --> 01:43:37.860
because others were, they
were getting that through.

01:43:37.860 --> 01:43:40.490
So there was the likes of
Franz Vollenweider in Zurich

01:43:40.490 --> 01:43:43.220
in Switzerland and
then Roland Griffiths

01:43:43.220 --> 01:43:46.530
coming along and doing the
psilocybin work at Hopkins.

01:43:46.530 --> 01:43:49.760
So you could appeal
to that precedence

01:43:49.760 --> 01:43:51.620
and say, well, they're
doing it over there.

01:43:51.620 --> 01:43:55.955
Can we not do it
in little England?

01:43:55.955 --> 01:43:57.620
So that's how it worked for us.

01:43:57.620 --> 01:44:00.800
We did actually go on
and do an LSD study

01:44:00.800 --> 01:44:06.770
once we'd laid the foundations
for doing this kind of work.

01:44:06.770 --> 01:44:08.270
And it was a brain
imaging study.

01:44:08.270 --> 01:44:10.010
It was a really
extensive one actually

01:44:10.010 --> 01:44:12.890
where we used both MRI
and another modality

01:44:12.890 --> 01:44:17.540
called MEG, sort of
super EEG in a sense.

01:44:17.540 --> 01:44:23.090
But you know, why didn't
that turn our heads

01:44:23.090 --> 01:44:28.430
to think, oh, should we not
be doing our trials with LSD?

01:44:28.430 --> 01:44:31.130
It does have something
to do with the pragmatics

01:44:31.130 --> 01:44:34.820
like a study day with
psilocybin is long enough.

01:44:34.820 --> 01:44:37.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
four to six-hour trip?

01:44:37.190 --> 01:44:38.315
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:44:38.315 --> 01:44:44.540
And the FDA asked us to
have the people in the lab

01:44:44.540 --> 01:44:48.530
until eight hours post-dose,
which personally I think

01:44:48.530 --> 01:44:51.740
could be quite excessive,
especially if it's a low dose.

01:44:51.740 --> 01:44:54.890
And if you have that in the
placebo condition as well,

01:44:54.890 --> 01:44:56.793
it becomes impractical.

01:44:56.793 --> 01:44:57.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:44:57.710 --> 01:44:59.630
Scientists are not
paid nearly enough

01:44:59.630 --> 01:45:02.745
to warrant the-- there's
no such thing as overtime

01:45:02.745 --> 01:45:04.370
for the graduate
students and postdocs.

01:45:04.370 --> 01:45:05.495
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:45:05.495 --> 01:45:07.370
It's often those
more junior members

01:45:07.370 --> 01:45:09.168
that are doing that
really hard work.

01:45:09.168 --> 01:45:11.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It was
described very well to me

01:45:11.210 --> 01:45:15.170
by a student when I was-- a
graduate student said to me,

01:45:15.170 --> 01:45:18.470
they really can't afford
to pay us by the hour.

01:45:18.470 --> 01:45:19.683
Because we used to work.

01:45:19.683 --> 01:45:22.100
He was a electrophysiologist,
so he would run experiments.

01:45:22.100 --> 01:45:23.330
No joke, folks.

01:45:23.330 --> 01:45:25.580
Three to five-day experiments.

01:45:25.580 --> 01:45:28.700
Sleeping in bouts of
two hours here or there

01:45:28.700 --> 01:45:33.230
in a dark room with a bunch
of equipment and recordings.

01:45:33.230 --> 01:45:37.290
So these are long, long
acute electrophysiological

01:45:37.290 --> 01:45:37.790
recordings.

01:45:37.790 --> 01:45:39.230
So yeah.

01:45:39.230 --> 01:45:43.515
No scientist does it for
the money, I promise you.

01:45:43.515 --> 01:45:46.070
There is money in
pharma, there is not

01:45:46.070 --> 01:45:49.130
money in personal income.

01:45:49.130 --> 01:45:52.340
It's not lucrative for
the basic scientist.

01:45:52.340 --> 01:45:53.900
So yes.

01:45:53.900 --> 01:45:55.430
LSD is what?

01:45:55.430 --> 01:45:58.310
Anywhere from 8 to 15
hours, something like that.

01:45:58.310 --> 01:45:58.370
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:45:58.370 --> 01:45:59.540
15 would be a little long.

01:45:59.540 --> 01:46:01.100
You'd be a bit worried
if you were still

01:46:01.100 --> 01:46:03.200
tripping at that time, maybe
with a really big dose.

01:46:03.200 --> 01:46:03.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oops.

01:46:03.710 --> 01:46:04.210
No.

01:46:04.210 --> 01:46:05.915
Just kidding.

01:46:05.915 --> 01:46:07.070
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:46:07.070 --> 01:46:10.400
Eight hours plus-- and
dose dependent, yeah.

01:46:10.400 --> 01:46:12.530
If it's a bigger dose,
it's a longer experience.

01:46:12.530 --> 01:46:17.090
But if you're going
to dose say, 10:00 AM

01:46:17.090 --> 01:46:20.780
in the morning, which is more
or less how it often goes, then

01:46:20.780 --> 01:46:24.140
that's 6:00 PM still
feeling the effects.

01:46:24.140 --> 01:46:28.730
And then how long do you
wait now to close things out

01:46:28.730 --> 01:46:30.200
before they can go home.

01:46:30.200 --> 01:46:34.340
Even with psilocybin, you
have people still at work

01:46:34.340 --> 01:46:35.330
into the evening.

01:46:35.330 --> 01:46:37.190
And the staff are
always there later,

01:46:37.190 --> 01:46:40.250
of course, because they've
got to pack up and yeah.

01:46:40.250 --> 01:46:41.660
So these are long days.

01:46:41.660 --> 01:46:45.043
And it's too much, you know?

01:46:45.043 --> 01:46:46.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That makes sense.

01:46:46.460 --> 01:46:48.080
Practical constraints.

01:46:48.080 --> 01:46:51.770
I learned from a recent
guest on this podcast

01:46:51.770 --> 01:46:54.110
that we recorded with,
Dr. Satchin Panda who

01:46:54.110 --> 01:46:57.025
is a colleague of mine when I
was down at the Salk Institute.

01:46:57.025 --> 01:46:58.400
Pioneered a lot
of the studies on

01:46:58.400 --> 01:47:00.050
so-called intermittent fasting.

01:47:00.050 --> 01:47:04.250
That the reason that the eating
period in these studies--

01:47:04.250 --> 01:47:06.620
in animals and now
on humans is 8 hours,

01:47:06.620 --> 01:47:08.360
the feeding window
in these studies

01:47:08.360 --> 01:47:12.050
is because the graduate student
was going to, otherwise,

01:47:12.050 --> 01:47:13.865
lose their relationship.

01:47:13.865 --> 01:47:15.740
Because their significant
other says, listen,

01:47:15.740 --> 01:47:17.900
you can be in the lab
for 12 hours, that

01:47:17.900 --> 01:47:20.608
meant some hours before the
experiment, then 8 hours,

01:47:20.608 --> 01:47:21.900
and then some hours afterwards.

01:47:21.900 --> 01:47:23.358
But you can't stay
in there longer.

01:47:23.358 --> 01:47:25.760
And many people use the
eight-hour feeding window

01:47:25.760 --> 01:47:26.880
as a consequence.

01:47:26.880 --> 01:47:29.420
So the scientist has to
exist and be carried out

01:47:29.420 --> 01:47:30.635
in real-world frame.

01:47:30.635 --> 01:47:31.330
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: It does.

01:47:31.330 --> 01:47:31.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:47:31.680 --> 01:47:32.930
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: It does.

01:47:32.930 --> 01:47:36.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: MDMA is a
little bit shorter, right?

01:47:36.740 --> 01:47:37.700
It's about a four to--

01:47:37.700 --> 01:47:39.140
it's also about four
to six hours, correct?

01:47:39.140 --> 01:47:39.320
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:47:39.320 --> 01:47:40.950
It's kind of similar
to psilocybin.

01:47:40.950 --> 01:47:41.450
Yeah.

01:47:41.450 --> 01:47:41.780
It is.

01:47:41.780 --> 01:47:43.280
And actually, in
the maps work, they

01:47:43.280 --> 01:47:45.017
redose after a certain point.

01:47:45.017 --> 01:47:46.225
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The booster.

01:47:46.225 --> 01:47:48.808
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: They have
a booster or optional booster,

01:47:48.808 --> 01:47:49.490
yeah.

01:47:49.490 --> 01:47:51.470
So there is that.

01:47:51.470 --> 01:47:53.840
And now, people
are thinking, well,

01:47:53.840 --> 01:47:58.620
even the psilocybin sessions
are long and expensive.

01:47:58.620 --> 01:48:01.190
And if you have to have
two staff members there

01:48:01.190 --> 01:48:03.050
all the time, that's expensive.

01:48:03.050 --> 01:48:04.730
That's where most
of the expense is.

01:48:04.730 --> 01:48:06.510
Is in the staffing.

01:48:06.510 --> 01:48:10.130
So can we abridge the
experience, make it shorter,

01:48:10.130 --> 01:48:15.060
and get away with it, and get
similar therapeutic outcomes?

01:48:15.060 --> 01:48:17.970
So there's a lot of
interest in that direction.

01:48:17.970 --> 01:48:19.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
May I ask about--

01:48:19.390 --> 01:48:21.182
sorry to interrupt,
but I want to make sure

01:48:21.182 --> 01:48:25.510
I don't forget to ask about
combination psilocybin MDMA

01:48:25.510 --> 01:48:26.380
therapies.

01:48:26.380 --> 01:48:28.480
The reason I ask about this is--

01:48:28.480 --> 01:48:30.670
and here, truly
not me, but I know

01:48:30.670 --> 01:48:36.220
people who do
self-administered combination

01:48:36.220 --> 01:48:37.885
psilocybin and MDMA.

01:48:37.885 --> 01:48:39.010
I think I heard this right.

01:48:39.010 --> 01:48:42.170
I think it's called
a hippy flip.

01:48:42.170 --> 01:48:44.110
There's another one
that involves LSD too.

01:48:44.110 --> 01:48:46.960
Again, I'm not suggesting people
do these drug combinations.

01:48:46.960 --> 01:48:49.450
But the way it was
described to me

01:48:49.450 --> 01:48:53.560
was that the psilocybin,
because it's so serotonergic,

01:48:53.560 --> 01:48:57.870
sometimes can be not a downer
but can have a bit of kind

01:48:57.870 --> 01:48:59.920
of a murky feel to it.

01:48:59.920 --> 01:49:02.110
Some real deep
introspection, sometimes

01:49:02.110 --> 01:49:05.960
in the darker realms of one's
psyche depressive thoughts,

01:49:05.960 --> 01:49:06.460
et cetera.

01:49:06.460 --> 01:49:09.130
Not that it necessarily stays
that way throughout the trip,

01:49:09.130 --> 01:49:11.740
but that the MDMA,
because it has

01:49:11.740 --> 01:49:15.520
a very strongly serotonergic
but also dopaminergic--

01:49:15.520 --> 01:49:18.290
I mean, it has an
amphetamine component.

01:49:18.290 --> 01:49:20.280
Cocaine-like, in fact.

01:49:20.280 --> 01:49:21.860
If you've ever seen
someone on MDMA,

01:49:21.860 --> 01:49:25.190
their pupils are about
the size of a quarters.

01:49:25.190 --> 01:49:29.960
For a reason, they're
extreme autonomic arousal

01:49:29.960 --> 01:49:33.170
compared to a sedative,
which by the way,

01:49:33.170 --> 01:49:35.250
would constrict the pupils.

01:49:35.250 --> 01:49:38.960
So they describe the use
of MDMA to balance out

01:49:38.960 --> 01:49:42.800
the affect component of it.

01:49:42.800 --> 01:49:47.060
What are your thoughts on
combination psilocybin MDMA?

01:49:47.060 --> 01:49:49.070
Does this hold any
therapeutic potential?

01:49:49.070 --> 01:49:52.880
This is, obviously, backyard
chemistry in the sense

01:49:52.880 --> 01:49:56.730
that people are cowboying
this stuff on their own,

01:49:56.730 --> 01:49:58.658
which again, I don't
really recommend.

01:49:58.658 --> 01:50:00.200
I'd like to see the
science go first,

01:50:00.200 --> 01:50:02.940
but I understand this is how
it works in the real world.

01:50:02.940 --> 01:50:03.440
Yeah.

01:50:03.440 --> 01:50:05.630
What are your thoughts
on combining compounds?

01:50:05.630 --> 01:50:06.755
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:50:06.755 --> 01:50:10.610
Well, I guess, they're cowboying
it in recreational context,

01:50:10.610 --> 01:50:13.533
but also underground therapists
do work with this combo.

01:50:13.533 --> 01:50:15.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's
what I'm referring to.

01:50:15.450 --> 01:50:17.825
So I'm not talking about people
partying with this stuff.

01:50:17.825 --> 01:50:22.040
I'm talking about there are
thousands now of therapists

01:50:22.040 --> 01:50:24.680
that offer psychedelic
therapies illegally,

01:50:24.680 --> 01:50:26.990
really, because it's not
legal at least, not in the US,

01:50:26.990 --> 01:50:28.370
to possess or sell.

01:50:28.370 --> 01:50:30.035
But that are doing this.

01:50:30.035 --> 01:50:30.400
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:50:30.400 --> 01:50:31.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So that's
really why I'm asking.

01:50:31.740 --> 01:50:32.865
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:50:32.865 --> 01:50:34.910
And I think there's
something to be said for--

01:50:34.910 --> 01:50:37.130
one has to be careful
with this as a scientist.

01:50:37.130 --> 01:50:40.610
But if they're doing
it, are they using

01:50:40.610 --> 01:50:41.930
some kind of trial and error?

01:50:41.930 --> 01:50:45.740
The same is true, of
course, with longer history

01:50:45.740 --> 01:50:49.580
of psychedelic plant
medicine use if by plants, we

01:50:49.580 --> 01:50:51.990
include the fungi as well.

01:50:51.990 --> 01:50:55.180
So in the extended sense plants.

01:50:55.180 --> 01:50:57.120
There will have been
trial and error there.

01:50:57.120 --> 01:51:00.230
It might not be as systematic
as the science we do today,

01:51:00.230 --> 01:51:02.570
but maybe there's been
a learning process.

01:51:02.570 --> 01:51:05.690
And maybe what they
do, they've come

01:51:05.690 --> 01:51:07.610
to because they found it works.

01:51:07.610 --> 01:51:11.480
So by that principle, I'm
interested in that combination

01:51:11.480 --> 01:51:15.260
and whether it does offer
some advantages, maybe,

01:51:15.260 --> 01:51:17.570
in certain patients.

01:51:17.570 --> 01:51:19.620
One of the buzz
terms in medicine

01:51:19.620 --> 01:51:23.040
these days is
precision medicine.

01:51:23.040 --> 01:51:25.540
A precision medicine and
personalized medicine.

01:51:25.540 --> 01:51:29.100
So maybe there are
certain cases where

01:51:29.100 --> 01:51:35.490
introducing say, psilocybin
after the MDMA or the other way

01:51:35.490 --> 01:51:38.520
around could offer
some advantages.

01:51:38.520 --> 01:51:41.220
And the differences
are interesting.

01:51:41.220 --> 01:51:46.260
Psilocybin can get
you to deep places.

01:51:46.260 --> 01:51:53.340
Maybe the kernel of your
suffering and major life

01:51:53.340 --> 01:51:57.660
experiences and complexes
that are causally

01:51:57.660 --> 01:52:02.640
linked to whatever the pathology
that you're presenting with.

01:52:02.640 --> 01:52:06.270
But it can do it, sometimes,
quite aggressively.

01:52:06.270 --> 01:52:10.020
And if it say, post-traumatic
stress disorder,

01:52:10.020 --> 01:52:11.790
it can be overwhelming.

01:52:11.790 --> 01:52:13.260
And you can fight it.

01:52:13.260 --> 01:52:15.555
And really, it's that.

01:52:15.555 --> 01:52:18.300
The resistance is
really challenged,

01:52:18.300 --> 01:52:20.670
and they fight back.

01:52:20.670 --> 01:52:23.760
And the therapeutic
breakthrough and the progress

01:52:23.760 --> 01:52:27.210
isn't happening because
you've agitated the defense

01:52:27.210 --> 01:52:28.650
mechanisms.

01:52:28.650 --> 01:52:33.360
Whereas what MDMA offers
is something arguably

01:52:33.360 --> 01:52:39.000
more directionally reliable
in terms of the valence.

01:52:39.000 --> 01:52:42.913
It's more directionally positive
generally, an MDMA experience.

01:52:42.913 --> 01:52:44.955
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Hard to
have a bad time on MDMA.

01:52:44.955 --> 01:52:45.540
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:52:45.540 --> 01:52:47.332
ANDREW HUBERMAN: To be
quite blunt, I mean.

01:52:47.332 --> 01:52:49.943
But one of the concerns
I had with MDMA,

01:52:49.943 --> 01:52:51.360
I've never done
it recreationally.

01:52:51.360 --> 01:52:54.870
I have had not and have not
ever done it recreationally.

01:52:54.870 --> 01:52:57.900
But when it was done in
this therapeutic setting,

01:52:57.900 --> 01:53:00.990
I realized, because there was
a music on at the beginning,

01:53:00.990 --> 01:53:02.850
I actually asked
them to turn it off,

01:53:02.850 --> 01:53:06.300
because I realized that
the music was becoming

01:53:06.300 --> 01:53:08.218
such an attractor
to my attention

01:53:08.218 --> 01:53:10.260
that I suddenly was starting
to think about music

01:53:10.260 --> 01:53:11.790
and my love of
music, which was not

01:53:11.790 --> 01:53:16.353
the focus of the session
that was there for.

01:53:16.353 --> 01:53:18.270
And I'm glad that they
did turn the music off,

01:53:18.270 --> 01:53:19.687
because the moment
they did, I was

01:53:19.687 --> 01:53:22.410
able to drop in within the eye
mask to this sort of go inward

01:53:22.410 --> 01:53:24.960
and address some certain
issues that at least to me

01:53:24.960 --> 01:53:27.190
felt key and productive.

01:53:27.190 --> 01:53:30.180
So that seems to be the
kind of hazard with MDMA.

01:53:30.180 --> 01:53:35.940
Is that it's such an empathogen
that one could start to-- you

01:53:35.940 --> 01:53:39.735
could go down any number
of different rabbit holes.

01:53:39.735 --> 01:53:40.860
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:53:40.860 --> 01:53:44.790
But it's also-- it's a
strength, because you well,

01:53:44.790 --> 01:53:48.630
the classics like psilocybin
can take you there very reliably

01:53:48.630 --> 01:53:50.940
but maybe a bit aggressively.

01:53:50.940 --> 01:53:54.630
MDMA makes it
easier to go there.

01:53:54.630 --> 01:53:56.260
And that's its strength.

01:53:56.260 --> 01:53:59.640
And that's why that marriage
of MDMA therapy for PTSD,

01:53:59.640 --> 01:54:03.480
in particular, is a good combo.

01:54:03.480 --> 01:54:06.000
It works, because you
are going to go there.

01:54:06.000 --> 01:54:09.690
In a sense, you have to really
make the therapeutic progress.

01:54:09.690 --> 01:54:12.150
You're going to have
to go back there.

01:54:12.150 --> 01:54:16.320
But we're going to set it up
so that you can go back there

01:54:16.320 --> 01:54:20.550
and feel safer and
more trusting and be

01:54:20.550 --> 01:54:24.570
able to go back there whereas
you've never, otherwise,

01:54:24.570 --> 01:54:28.290
been able to go back there
without dissociating or having

01:54:28.290 --> 01:54:30.910
horrible flashbacks and so on.

01:54:30.910 --> 01:54:34.020
So that's the strength
that it offers.

01:54:34.020 --> 01:54:36.690
I guess the limitation
would be that, maybe, it

01:54:36.690 --> 01:54:41.460
doesn't take you as deep as
the classic psychedelics.

01:54:41.460 --> 01:54:43.650
And I tend to think
I'm biased on this one,

01:54:43.650 --> 01:54:46.320
that there's a kind of
honesty to the classics

01:54:46.320 --> 01:54:50.430
in that it is hell
as well as heaven.

01:54:50.430 --> 01:54:51.970
And that's the psyche.

01:54:51.970 --> 01:54:55.140
It isn't all roses.

01:54:55.140 --> 01:54:56.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
really appreciate

01:54:56.700 --> 01:54:57.658
that you bring that up.

01:54:57.658 --> 01:54:59.490
Because I think that
there's such a fear

01:54:59.490 --> 01:55:02.490
of so-called bad trips.

01:55:02.490 --> 01:55:07.980
There's such a fear in
non-psychedelic states

01:55:07.980 --> 01:55:14.130
to avoid the painful and
everything we know from trauma

01:55:14.130 --> 01:55:15.648
and the treatment of trauma.

01:55:15.648 --> 01:55:17.190
And we've had several
guests on here.

01:55:17.190 --> 01:55:19.648
And my close colleague, close,
close colleague at Stanford,

01:55:19.648 --> 01:55:22.240
Dr. David Spiegel, our
Associate Chair of Psychiatries,

01:55:22.240 --> 01:55:23.920
Clinical Hypnotist.

01:55:23.920 --> 01:55:26.770
Amazing human being and
scientist and clinician,

01:55:26.770 --> 01:55:31.300
has really just embedded
this in my mind.

01:55:31.300 --> 01:55:33.010
That the only way
to deal with trauma

01:55:33.010 --> 01:55:36.580
is to get right up next to
that trauma to the point

01:55:36.580 --> 01:55:38.260
where some relief
is experienced.

01:55:38.260 --> 01:55:41.165
There is no other real way.

01:55:41.165 --> 01:55:42.790
And so, I really
appreciate that you're

01:55:42.790 --> 01:55:46.375
saying that the classic
psychedelics may offer

01:55:46.375 --> 01:55:48.790
with a very strong
nudge, perhaps,

01:55:48.790 --> 01:55:52.450
the opportunity to get into
the uncomfortable in a way

01:55:52.450 --> 01:55:58.210
that MDMA or some non-classical
psychedelics, perhaps, do not.

01:55:58.210 --> 01:56:01.660
We were talking about time
frames or duration of trips

01:56:01.660 --> 01:56:03.910
and these different
compounds and how they differ

01:56:03.910 --> 01:56:06.040
and how they're similar.

01:56:06.040 --> 01:56:09.700
I'd love for you to educate
me on DMT and some of the work

01:56:09.700 --> 01:56:11.200
that you're doing with DMT.

01:56:11.200 --> 01:56:16.390
My understanding is that it's
a very brief trip minutes.

01:56:16.390 --> 01:56:17.830
People I know who
have done this.

01:56:17.830 --> 01:56:20.590
Again, therapeutically,
actually, I'll

01:56:20.590 --> 01:56:23.770
just point to one very
exciting, I think,

01:56:23.770 --> 01:56:26.740
group and initiative which
is the Veterans Solutions

01:56:26.740 --> 01:56:28.660
Initiative, which is a group--

01:56:28.660 --> 01:56:30.945
this is carried out in
Mexico, but in conjunction

01:56:30.945 --> 01:56:33.070
with laboratories at Stanford
and elsewhere who are

01:56:33.070 --> 01:56:35.290
evaluating the neural changes.

01:56:35.290 --> 01:56:37.090
And this involves
ibogaine, which

01:56:37.090 --> 01:56:40.600
is iboga, which is a very
long duration psychedelic.

01:56:40.600 --> 01:56:47.200
22 hours or more, followed by I
think one or two doses of DMT.

01:56:47.200 --> 01:56:50.832
This is for veterans to deal
with any number of issues.

01:56:50.832 --> 01:56:52.540
Appears to be working
with great success.

01:56:52.540 --> 01:56:53.830
And I've spoken
to several people

01:56:53.830 --> 01:56:54.872
who've gone through this.

01:56:54.872 --> 01:56:59.170
And the way that they described
DMT, almost across the board,

01:56:59.170 --> 01:57:02.290
was quote, here I'm just
pulling quotes, right?

01:57:02.290 --> 01:57:04.730
Anecdata.

01:57:04.730 --> 01:57:07.620
The most profound experience
of my entire life,

01:57:07.620 --> 01:57:10.370
even greater than the
birth of my children,

01:57:10.370 --> 01:57:16.280
quote, "like being attached to
the shockwave of an atom bomb."

01:57:16.280 --> 01:57:17.120
Quote.

01:57:17.120 --> 01:57:19.310
"There's no way I
would do another dose,

01:57:19.310 --> 01:57:21.830
because the first one
was so unbelievable."

01:57:21.830 --> 01:57:23.495
Interesting, by the way.

01:57:23.495 --> 01:57:24.870
I think most of
us, including me,

01:57:24.870 --> 01:57:26.360
would think, why wouldn't
you want to do it again then?

01:57:26.360 --> 01:57:28.980
But this idea that that
was just beyond anything.

01:57:28.980 --> 01:57:30.920
So these are
significant-- excuse

01:57:30.920 --> 01:57:33.500
me these are significant
statements coming

01:57:33.500 --> 01:57:36.320
from individuals
who have existed

01:57:36.320 --> 01:57:39.380
at the extremes of human
experience to begin with.

01:57:39.380 --> 01:57:41.840
These are so-called
tier 1 operators

01:57:41.840 --> 01:57:45.140
within special operations
who exit and may or may not

01:57:45.140 --> 01:57:45.680
have trauma.

01:57:45.680 --> 01:57:49.975
But DMT sounds like a big deal.

01:57:49.975 --> 01:57:51.100
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:57:51.100 --> 01:57:53.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Short
duration, really big deal.

01:57:53.730 --> 01:57:55.460
What do we know
about its chemistry?

01:57:55.460 --> 01:57:58.100
What do we know about how
it's impacting brain networks?

01:57:58.100 --> 01:58:00.560
And what in the
world is going on

01:58:00.560 --> 01:58:03.860
that people are
describing it as the ways

01:58:03.860 --> 01:58:05.730
I just mentioned
a few moments ago?

01:58:05.730 --> 01:58:07.470
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

01:58:07.470 --> 01:58:10.251
It's a rocket ship.

01:58:10.251 --> 01:58:15.300
If the psilocybin is like a
ship leaving port, then yeah.

01:58:15.300 --> 01:58:20.460
This is a rocket
ship into craziness.

01:58:20.460 --> 01:58:21.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Is it serotonin 2A?

01:58:21.990 --> 01:58:23.280
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: It is.

01:58:23.280 --> 01:58:23.780
Yeah.

01:58:23.780 --> 01:58:25.450
So it is a classic psychedelic.

01:58:25.450 --> 01:58:29.910
It's a direct agonist, a direct
stimulator of the serotonin 2A

01:58:29.910 --> 01:58:30.990
receptor.

01:58:30.990 --> 01:58:37.680
It's an order of magnitude
less potent than psilocybin.

01:58:37.680 --> 01:58:41.398
But potency is a funny thing,
because it's dose-dependent.

01:58:41.398 --> 01:58:43.440
So that doesn't mean that
the experience with DMT

01:58:43.440 --> 01:58:45.180
is less than that of psilocybin.

01:58:45.180 --> 01:58:46.890
It's just that you
give more of the drug.

01:58:49.500 --> 01:58:53.550
But that's matched
by it's stickiness

01:58:53.550 --> 01:58:55.290
for the serotonin
2A receptor, which

01:58:55.290 --> 01:58:58.620
is this kind of golden rule
in psychedelic sciences

01:58:58.620 --> 01:59:01.020
that it was discovered
in the mid 1980s.

01:59:01.020 --> 01:59:04.830
This tight relationship between
the affinity or the stickiness

01:59:04.830 --> 01:59:08.700
or the binding potential of a
psychedelic for 2A receptor,

01:59:08.700 --> 01:59:09.390
in particular.

01:59:09.390 --> 01:59:12.360
Serotonin 2A and its potency.

01:59:12.360 --> 01:59:14.500
And the stickier the
drug, the more potent.

01:59:14.500 --> 01:59:15.810
So LSD really sticky.

01:59:15.810 --> 01:59:16.800
Very, very potent.

01:59:16.800 --> 01:59:20.790
You only need those
tiny microgram doses.

01:59:20.790 --> 01:59:26.130
So DMT, by its affinity,
is a little less potent,

01:59:26.130 --> 01:59:31.635
but by its effects when
you give a standard dose,

01:59:31.635 --> 01:59:33.180
it's just wild.

01:59:33.180 --> 01:59:38.800
And DMT, because there's another
compound called 5-methoxy DMT,

01:59:38.800 --> 01:59:42.090
which is a bit different
pharmacologically

01:59:42.090 --> 01:59:46.050
and subjectively, it's similar
in terms of its kinetics.

01:59:46.050 --> 01:59:48.510
It's another rocket ship.

01:59:48.510 --> 01:59:56.490
Both compounds in the wild,
so to speak, are smoked often.

01:59:56.490 --> 01:59:58.440
DMT and 5-MeO.

01:59:58.440 --> 02:00:02.110
People are vaping
both actually now.

02:00:02.110 --> 02:00:07.040
There are vape pens that are
being developed for people

02:00:07.040 --> 02:00:10.430
to administer this,
but more traditionally,

02:00:10.430 --> 02:00:11.810
it's been a smoking thing.

02:00:11.810 --> 02:00:15.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This is
clinically not recreationally

02:00:15.020 --> 02:00:15.570
or both.

02:00:15.570 --> 02:00:16.820
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Both no.

02:00:16.820 --> 02:00:19.280
I mean, underground
practitioners

02:00:19.280 --> 02:00:21.050
are using the vape pens.

02:00:21.050 --> 02:00:24.080
They like them because
people titrate the dosage.

02:00:24.080 --> 02:00:27.650
They get a feel for what it
is to be going into this state

02:00:27.650 --> 02:00:32.540
so that they feel they
can let go and go into it.

02:00:32.540 --> 02:00:34.700
And actually, I think
some of the veterans work

02:00:34.700 --> 02:00:40.880
might be giving 5-MeO
after the ibogaine.

02:00:40.880 --> 02:00:44.450
Phenomenologically, if there's
a difference between DMT

02:00:44.450 --> 02:00:48.170
and 5-MeO, people
might put it on 5-MeO

02:00:48.170 --> 02:00:52.160
being more of a reliable
ego dissolution experience.

02:00:52.160 --> 02:00:57.260
Less visual and more
all round immersion

02:00:57.260 --> 02:01:01.970
in the greater whole
loss of self-identity

02:01:01.970 --> 02:01:03.783
and just immersion
in everything.

02:01:03.783 --> 02:01:04.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:01:04.700 --> 02:01:07.290
Maybe we could just talk about
ego dissolution for a second,

02:01:07.290 --> 02:01:10.570
because it's such a
sticky, interesting idea.

02:01:10.570 --> 02:01:14.940
I can take a step back as a
neuroscientist and say, OK.

02:01:14.940 --> 02:01:15.810
Ego dissolution.

02:01:15.810 --> 02:01:18.870
This idea that-- from
a very early age,

02:01:18.870 --> 02:01:22.920
we have a concept of self and
that I wake up every morning

02:01:22.920 --> 02:01:24.810
and I know I'm me and
not somebody else.

02:01:24.810 --> 02:01:26.250
And presumably, you do the same.

02:01:26.250 --> 02:01:29.910
And most people do the
same, I would hope.

02:01:29.910 --> 02:01:31.560
And that there are
objects in the world

02:01:31.560 --> 02:01:33.180
and people in the
world beyond us.

02:01:33.180 --> 02:01:36.090
But every time I hear
about ego dissolution,

02:01:36.090 --> 02:01:41.190
it sounds like it's kind
of a temporary elimination

02:01:41.190 --> 02:01:45.840
of the idea that
things start and stop

02:01:45.840 --> 02:01:47.880
between us and everything else.

02:01:47.880 --> 02:01:50.670
Almost like in a kind
of a-- here I'm not

02:01:50.670 --> 02:01:53.340
trying to sound philosophical
or metaphysical,

02:01:53.340 --> 02:01:57.480
but there's the molecular
continuity of life, right?

02:01:57.480 --> 02:01:58.745
We're all just little bits--

02:01:58.745 --> 02:02:00.245
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Which is true.

02:02:00.245 --> 02:02:02.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--which is true, right?

02:02:02.070 --> 02:02:05.042
Not a functional way to
go through the day, right?

02:02:05.042 --> 02:02:06.750
Because you want to
make a cup of coffee,

02:02:06.750 --> 02:02:08.820
you don't really want
to get lost in that

02:02:08.820 --> 02:02:11.960
if your goal is to
make a cup of coffee.

02:02:11.960 --> 02:02:19.020
But what is the power
of ego dissolution?

02:02:19.020 --> 02:02:23.880
Is it the idea that we belong,
is it a sense of meaning?

02:02:23.880 --> 02:02:26.850
Is it the sense that we're
not as important as we think,

02:02:26.850 --> 02:02:30.480
which, of course, could be
a wonderfully useful way

02:02:30.480 --> 02:02:31.935
to go through life?

02:02:31.935 --> 02:02:34.080
To think that we're not as--

02:02:34.080 --> 02:02:36.880
like we are vitally important,
but we're not the only thing,

02:02:36.880 --> 02:02:37.380
right?

02:02:37.380 --> 02:02:41.620
Because I do believe connection
is vital as most people do.

02:02:41.620 --> 02:02:42.960
What is ego dissolution?

02:02:42.960 --> 02:02:48.330
And why would this serotonin
2A activation cause that?

02:02:48.330 --> 02:02:49.350
That's remarkable.

02:02:49.350 --> 02:02:51.030
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:02:51.030 --> 02:02:52.210
Great questions.

02:02:52.210 --> 02:02:55.850
I mean, what is it?

02:02:55.850 --> 02:02:58.610
You alluded to it with
the start/stop, I think.

02:02:58.610 --> 02:03:02.420
Because you could
define it by boundaries.

02:03:02.420 --> 02:03:07.930
In a sense what isn't
me is as valid here

02:03:07.930 --> 02:03:11.060
as the a developing
sense of what

02:03:11.060 --> 02:03:15.905
is me that a child
develops at whatever age.

02:03:15.905 --> 02:03:21.560
And so a major characteristic
of the ego dissolution

02:03:21.560 --> 02:03:25.520
experience rather than just a
negative, a thing going away.

02:03:25.520 --> 02:03:29.450
My sense of self going
away is the positive.

02:03:29.450 --> 02:03:29.990
Oh.

02:03:29.990 --> 02:03:33.530
Now, I feel interconnected
with other people and the world

02:03:33.530 --> 02:03:34.080
at large.

02:03:34.080 --> 02:03:38.030
And I realize that there is
that molecular continuity.

02:03:38.030 --> 02:03:40.310
And actually, that's
a ground truth.

02:03:40.310 --> 02:03:45.410
And oh, maybe the ego thing is
somewhat illusory or at least

02:03:45.410 --> 02:03:47.512
a construction of my mind.

02:03:47.512 --> 02:03:49.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
indeed it is, right?

02:03:49.220 --> 02:03:50.300
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
Well, it is.

02:03:50.300 --> 02:03:50.800
Yeah.

02:03:50.800 --> 02:03:54.470
I mean, there's no
transcendentalism about that.

02:03:54.470 --> 02:03:56.702
It's just not like logic.

02:03:56.702 --> 02:03:58.910
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think we
are about it a little bit

02:03:58.910 --> 02:03:59.540
like family.

02:03:59.540 --> 02:04:02.360
I mean, we all know what
immediate family is, but it's

02:04:02.360 --> 02:04:03.155
like--

02:04:03.155 --> 02:04:04.800
forgive me for
interrupting myself.

02:04:04.800 --> 02:04:07.050
I do it all the time, anyway.

02:04:07.050 --> 02:04:10.490
When I teach neuroanatomy,
some clever student

02:04:10.490 --> 02:04:11.533
always figures out, OK.

02:04:11.533 --> 02:04:13.700
Well, that's connected to
that and that's connected.

02:04:13.700 --> 02:04:15.290
But ultimately,
everything in the brain

02:04:15.290 --> 02:04:16.475
is connected to everything else.

02:04:16.475 --> 02:04:16.790
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:04:16.790 --> 02:04:18.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: There's
just no way around that.

02:04:18.470 --> 02:04:19.355
That's a true statement.

02:04:19.355 --> 02:04:19.790
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:04:19.790 --> 02:04:21.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
so you really just

02:04:21.710 --> 02:04:24.175
have to decide where you
draw the boundaries between--

02:04:24.175 --> 02:04:24.630
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:04:24.630 --> 02:04:25.790
Where do you draw the line?

02:04:25.790 --> 02:04:27.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Where are the modules?

02:04:27.300 --> 02:04:28.070
What are the modules?

02:04:28.070 --> 02:04:30.282
You could say, the brain is
just one big macro module.

02:04:30.282 --> 02:04:30.627
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:04:30.627 --> 02:04:32.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And then you
also want to include the body.

02:04:32.430 --> 02:04:34.250
And now fortunately,
people are starting

02:04:34.250 --> 02:04:37.930
to embrace this idea that
it's not mind-body, it's both.

02:04:37.930 --> 02:04:39.680
Because the nervous
system extends through

02:04:39.680 --> 02:04:41.690
both, of course.

02:04:41.690 --> 02:04:44.450
So the same could
be said of family.

02:04:44.450 --> 02:04:45.650
We're related, right?

02:04:45.650 --> 02:04:47.942
Not just by virtue of the
fact that we're human beings.

02:04:47.942 --> 02:04:49.790
If we did our
genealogical charts,

02:04:49.790 --> 02:04:51.905
we would find a
convergence at some point.

02:04:51.905 --> 02:04:53.030
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:04:53.030 --> 02:04:56.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And of course,
this becomes a bit of a game.

02:04:56.550 --> 02:05:01.570
But then one realizes that
where you draw the boundaries

02:05:01.570 --> 02:05:05.340
and if you draw them at
brother, sister, parents,

02:05:05.340 --> 02:05:07.980
biological parents, et
cetera, that's a game too.

02:05:07.980 --> 02:05:09.735
And so it is just a construct.

02:05:09.735 --> 02:05:10.860
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:05:10.860 --> 02:05:12.180
I mean, it is a fun game.

02:05:12.180 --> 02:05:15.210
Where do you draw the
line and when to pass

02:05:15.210 --> 02:05:16.860
and when to collapse.

02:05:16.860 --> 02:05:19.800
It's also a classic
consideration in science

02:05:19.800 --> 02:05:21.345
when to pass and when--

02:05:21.345 --> 02:05:22.485
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That
lumper versus the splitter.

02:05:22.485 --> 02:05:23.340
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
There you go.

02:05:23.340 --> 02:05:24.240
It's brilliant.

02:05:24.240 --> 02:05:25.213
Yeah.

02:05:25.213 --> 02:05:26.880
But you asked this
question, like, well,

02:05:26.880 --> 02:05:28.410
why does psychedelics do it?

02:05:28.410 --> 02:05:32.340
And there, we think
psychedelics do it,

02:05:32.340 --> 02:05:35.970
because the target
receptors, at least,

02:05:35.970 --> 02:05:37.690
classic psychedelics do it.

02:05:37.690 --> 02:05:39.300
And that's important to stress.

02:05:39.300 --> 02:05:42.780
So MDMA doesn't really
do it in the same way,

02:05:42.780 --> 02:05:44.610
might soften the ego a bit.

02:05:44.610 --> 02:05:45.900
But yeah.

02:05:45.900 --> 02:05:46.710
That's debatable.

02:05:46.710 --> 02:05:49.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: My
experience with MDMA

02:05:49.080 --> 02:05:51.075
is that it's such a
strong empathogen.

02:05:51.075 --> 02:05:52.671
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:05:52.671 --> 02:05:57.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And that it
can cause empathy for others.

02:05:57.360 --> 02:06:00.300
Certainly, you could
imagine situations

02:06:00.300 --> 02:06:03.030
where one in MDMA
journey and afterwards

02:06:03.030 --> 02:06:08.647
says, oh, my oppressors are
the people that harmed me.

02:06:08.647 --> 02:06:10.480
And here I'm not referring
to my experience.

02:06:10.480 --> 02:06:12.280
But they did the best
with what they have.

02:06:12.280 --> 02:06:13.810
Actually have empathy for them.

02:06:13.810 --> 02:06:14.590
Forgiveness.

02:06:14.590 --> 02:06:16.210
But also for oneself.

02:06:16.210 --> 02:06:18.700
That there's an
empathy for self,

02:06:18.700 --> 02:06:23.050
I know I said this earlier, that
is very hard for most people

02:06:23.050 --> 02:06:23.710
to access.

02:06:23.710 --> 02:06:26.005
Perhaps, not the narcissists
out there listening.

02:06:26.005 --> 02:06:27.880
They'll be like, of
course, empathy for self.

02:06:27.880 --> 02:06:34.360
But everyone else, I think,
all the other healthy people

02:06:34.360 --> 02:06:36.548
or the healthy people
other than narcissists

02:06:36.548 --> 02:06:37.840
and not picking on narcissists.

02:06:37.840 --> 02:06:39.410
I have to imagine
they suffer too.

02:06:39.410 --> 02:06:42.310
In fact, I think that's the
root of their narcissism.

02:06:42.310 --> 02:06:44.290
That empathy for
self is not something

02:06:44.290 --> 02:06:46.810
that comes reflexively
for most people.

02:06:46.810 --> 02:06:49.210
And here I'm not talking about
self love or self respect.

02:06:49.210 --> 02:06:54.040
But this notion of being able
to see the self as not just

02:06:54.040 --> 02:06:58.390
deserving of love and
care but actually holding

02:06:58.390 --> 02:07:01.240
that in place while
in confrontation

02:07:01.240 --> 02:07:04.340
with something challenging
in a way that allows a more,

02:07:04.340 --> 02:07:08.030
not less access to adaptive
responses to that challenge.

02:07:08.030 --> 02:07:09.780
I think that's the way
I conceptualize it.

02:07:09.780 --> 02:07:10.905
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:07:10.905 --> 02:07:11.910
Yeah.

02:07:11.910 --> 02:07:15.150
I mean, drugs offer a great--

02:07:15.150 --> 02:07:18.570
they offer great-- they
are great scientific tools

02:07:18.570 --> 02:07:20.010
for tackling this question.

02:07:20.010 --> 02:07:23.550
What is ego dissolution, and
why do drugs modulate it?

02:07:23.550 --> 02:07:25.395
And what does that tell
you about the brain?

02:07:25.395 --> 02:07:28.290
Because other drugs
like cocaine releasing

02:07:28.290 --> 02:07:31.200
more of a different
neurotransmitter,

02:07:31.200 --> 02:07:33.990
dopamine more than serotonin.

02:07:33.990 --> 02:07:37.200
The opposite is
the case with MDMA.

02:07:37.200 --> 02:07:38.837
Is more of an ego
inflator, right?

02:07:38.837 --> 02:07:40.170
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, absolutely.

02:07:40.170 --> 02:07:43.560
People will become
hyper-linear, hyper-linked

02:07:43.560 --> 02:07:47.280
to their own desires and wishes,
and future outcomes become

02:07:47.280 --> 02:07:48.310
an obsession.

02:07:48.310 --> 02:07:50.910
It's the stuff of
American Psycho

02:07:50.910 --> 02:07:57.885
and the cliches and stereotypes
of the '80s cocaine culture.

02:07:57.885 --> 02:07:59.010
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:07:59.010 --> 02:08:00.060
Yeah.

02:08:00.060 --> 02:08:01.620
We did a study once
actually looking

02:08:01.620 --> 02:08:04.380
at dose-dependent relationship
with ego inflation

02:08:04.380 --> 02:08:07.110
on one axis and ego
dissolution on the other

02:08:07.110 --> 02:08:11.560
and saw that it just massively
passed or differentiated

02:08:11.560 --> 02:08:14.100
between cocaine
and psychedelics.

02:08:14.100 --> 02:08:15.100
It's quite a neat study.

02:08:15.100 --> 02:08:16.475
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So cocaine makes

02:08:16.475 --> 02:08:17.840
people's egos super inflated?

02:08:17.840 --> 02:08:18.965
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:08:18.965 --> 02:08:21.430
And doesn't touch
dissolution and the opposite

02:08:21.430 --> 02:08:23.190
is the case with psychedelics.

02:08:23.190 --> 02:08:24.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is
there any neuroimaging

02:08:24.940 --> 02:08:27.507
to explain how
cocaine does that?

02:08:27.507 --> 02:08:29.590
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: That
would be a great study.

02:08:29.590 --> 02:08:30.175
Yeah.

02:08:30.175 --> 02:08:30.675
Great idea.

02:08:30.675 --> 02:08:31.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
We should do that.

02:08:31.690 --> 02:08:33.310
I have a sabbatical coming up.

02:08:33.310 --> 02:08:35.102
I've got 12 months of
sabbatical coming up.

02:08:35.102 --> 02:08:36.227
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:08:36.227 --> 02:08:36.940
That's--

02:08:36.940 --> 02:08:38.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm going
to show up in your lab.

02:08:38.500 --> 02:08:39.130
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:08:39.130 --> 02:08:40.240
That's a really good one.

02:08:40.240 --> 02:08:43.060
If it's all right to finish
the thread on why psychedelics

02:08:43.060 --> 02:08:45.640
and ego dissolution,
we do know some things

02:08:45.640 --> 02:08:47.330
or we have some hypotheses.

02:08:47.330 --> 02:08:49.930
And it's that the target
receptors, the serotonin 2A

02:08:49.930 --> 02:08:52.570
receptors that classic
psychedelics hit,

02:08:52.570 --> 02:08:54.640
are heavily expressed
in what these days I

02:08:54.640 --> 02:08:57.130
like to call recent brain.

02:08:57.130 --> 02:08:59.740
Because evolutionarily,
it's recent brain.

02:08:59.740 --> 02:09:03.770
It's cortex that humans have
more than any other species.

02:09:03.770 --> 02:09:07.450
If you look at a mapping of
cortical expansion from say,

02:09:07.450 --> 02:09:12.130
macaque or chimp to human,
it's the very same map

02:09:12.130 --> 02:09:15.190
that you'll find
the 2A receptors in.

02:09:15.190 --> 02:09:18.040
So that's the target.

02:09:18.040 --> 02:09:21.400
And it's just easy to
think that-- oh, well, that

02:09:21.400 --> 02:09:28.900
could be the egoic brain and the
classic psychedelics come in.

02:09:28.900 --> 02:09:31.180
They scramble up the activity.

02:09:31.180 --> 02:09:33.190
That's the entropic
brain action.

02:09:33.190 --> 02:09:38.680
And in terms of the
start/stop, the boundaries,

02:09:38.680 --> 02:09:42.730
that entropic action
spreads out the system.

02:09:42.730 --> 02:09:44.140
It doesn't shut it off.

02:09:44.140 --> 02:09:46.472
It spreads it out.

02:09:46.472 --> 02:09:47.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Dissolution?

02:09:47.680 --> 02:09:48.805
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:09:48.805 --> 02:09:52.820
And you were talking about
the headspace as well.

02:09:52.820 --> 02:09:54.660
So that fits.

02:09:54.660 --> 02:10:00.340
If it's more capacious, it fits.

02:10:00.340 --> 02:10:03.730
The big qualifier with
psychedelic therapy

02:10:03.730 --> 02:10:08.690
that people rightly bring
up is it doesn't last.

02:10:08.690 --> 02:10:10.030
That's the paradox of it.

02:10:10.030 --> 02:10:12.200
The paradox of ego dissolution.

02:10:12.200 --> 02:10:15.920
So the ego might go
away during the trip,

02:10:15.920 --> 02:10:18.020
and you have these
profound insights

02:10:18.020 --> 02:10:21.320
about the molecular continuity
and how we're all one

02:10:21.320 --> 02:10:22.610
and interconnected.

02:10:22.610 --> 02:10:25.490
And then you come
down, and however

02:10:25.490 --> 02:10:29.780
long later, the ego comes back,
but maybe with a vengeance.

02:10:29.780 --> 02:10:33.500
And sadly, things
can go awry when

02:10:33.500 --> 02:10:35.250
people haven't done the work.

02:10:35.250 --> 02:10:36.800
Perhaps haven't
done the integration

02:10:36.800 --> 02:10:42.062
work and maybe ego
defenses come back

02:10:42.062 --> 02:10:45.125
and it's not a pretty picture.

02:10:45.125 --> 02:10:48.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: How often
do you see that in the trials

02:10:48.230 --> 02:10:49.560
that you do?

02:10:49.560 --> 02:10:54.070
What percentage of
people coming through

02:10:54.070 --> 02:10:57.930
do you think end up
with worse than they

02:10:57.930 --> 02:10:59.327
were before the trial?

02:10:59.327 --> 02:11:01.410
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: It's
very rare in the trials

02:11:01.410 --> 02:11:04.450
that we've done.

02:11:04.450 --> 02:11:05.050
Yeah.

02:11:05.050 --> 02:11:07.120
But you see defenses come back.

02:11:07.120 --> 02:11:10.030
So you do see people relapse.

02:11:10.030 --> 02:11:13.330
That's more-- if
you're pushing out

02:11:13.330 --> 02:11:17.050
to like three months plus
in something like treatment

02:11:17.050 --> 02:11:18.850
resistant depression,
that's more the rule

02:11:18.850 --> 02:11:20.410
than the exception sadly.

02:11:20.410 --> 02:11:21.580
People relapse.

02:11:21.580 --> 02:11:26.260
If their histories are
histories of chronic depression,

02:11:26.260 --> 02:11:30.250
then while you might give
them a window of wellness,

02:11:30.250 --> 02:11:31.930
sadly, it doesn't last.

02:11:31.930 --> 02:11:34.360
That's not to say that
it doesn't ever last.

02:11:34.360 --> 02:11:35.140
It does.

02:11:35.140 --> 02:11:37.840
And we have people who
are in our first treatment

02:11:37.840 --> 02:11:39.310
resistant depression
trial who are

02:11:39.310 --> 02:11:42.310
well to my knowledge
today, back at work

02:11:42.310 --> 02:11:44.350
doing fantastically well.

02:11:44.350 --> 02:11:48.398
But sadly, the majority have
relapsed, to my knowledge.

02:11:48.398 --> 02:11:50.815
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And need to
do more psychedelic journeys.

02:11:50.815 --> 02:11:53.357
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Well, they
can't, because it's illegal.

02:11:53.357 --> 02:11:55.780
That's been the really
difficult situation

02:11:55.780 --> 02:11:56.920
that we've been up against.

02:11:56.920 --> 02:11:59.890
Is that we do a trial where
all of a sudden this schedule 1

02:11:59.890 --> 02:12:01.850
drug becomes a
medicine in the trial,

02:12:01.850 --> 02:12:03.820
or at least an
experimental medicine.

02:12:03.820 --> 02:12:06.760
We give the treatment, it
works fantastically well,

02:12:06.760 --> 02:12:10.340
gives people a remission
that they've never really

02:12:10.340 --> 02:12:15.770
had for however long,
and then the trial ends

02:12:15.770 --> 02:12:17.360
and they're denied
that treatment.

02:12:17.360 --> 02:12:20.000
And worse still, if they
were to have that treatment,

02:12:20.000 --> 02:12:21.830
they would be
committing a crime.

02:12:21.830 --> 02:12:23.390
It's a sick joke in a way.

02:12:23.390 --> 02:12:26.098
But that's the situation
that we've been in.

02:12:26.098 --> 02:12:27.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
that's a perfect segue

02:12:27.890 --> 02:12:31.220
for what I want to
talk about now, which

02:12:31.220 --> 02:12:35.630
is, what is the current
state of legality

02:12:35.630 --> 02:12:38.150
in terms of-- or the
progression towards legality.

02:12:38.150 --> 02:12:41.390
I'd also like to touch on
the role of, let's just say,

02:12:41.390 --> 02:12:42.800
incoming big pharma.

02:12:42.800 --> 02:12:45.440
There are a lot of start
up companies now trying

02:12:45.440 --> 02:12:47.060
to capitalize on
these discoveries

02:12:47.060 --> 02:12:50.165
that you and others have made.

02:12:50.165 --> 02:12:54.320
The landscape out there
is very unclear to me.

02:12:54.320 --> 02:12:59.330
Maybe I'll just call out
some silos as I see them

02:12:59.330 --> 02:13:02.690
and maybe we can draw
some bridges between them,

02:13:02.690 --> 02:13:04.580
if they exist.

02:13:04.580 --> 02:13:07.880
At the ground level,
not the grassroots,

02:13:07.880 --> 02:13:11.090
but at the ground level,
I look to laboratories

02:13:11.090 --> 02:13:14.840
like yours, Matthew
Johnson's, Roland Griffith's

02:13:14.840 --> 02:13:16.670
some laboratories at Stanford.

02:13:16.670 --> 02:13:18.260
Nolan Williams.

02:13:18.260 --> 02:13:21.860
Laboratories studying the
effects of psychedelics

02:13:21.860 --> 02:13:24.110
in human beings, so
not animal models,

02:13:24.110 --> 02:13:27.690
in terms of their clinical
application for the treatment

02:13:27.690 --> 02:13:29.910
of depression, anorexia.

02:13:29.910 --> 02:13:31.740
I now know fibromyalgia, trauma.

02:13:31.740 --> 02:13:34.200
Let's lump MDMA
in there as well,

02:13:34.200 --> 02:13:36.630
assuming that it all
works in equivalent way

02:13:36.630 --> 02:13:39.550
at the level of where the
legislature is taking things.

02:13:39.550 --> 02:13:40.050
OK.

02:13:40.050 --> 02:13:46.570
So labs using government
money, philanthropy, et cetera.

02:13:46.570 --> 02:13:51.540
Then there are the
therapists out there

02:13:51.540 --> 02:13:53.820
that are accessing
what we believe

02:13:53.820 --> 02:13:56.130
are clean sources of MDMA.

02:13:56.130 --> 02:13:58.060
Psilocybin, LSD to do this.

02:13:58.060 --> 02:13:59.880
They are doing it illegally.

02:13:59.880 --> 02:14:03.840
This is in the US or other
Western European countries,

02:14:03.840 --> 02:14:07.050
because, obviously, it's
going to differ by country.

02:14:07.050 --> 02:14:09.030
Who are administering
these things

02:14:09.030 --> 02:14:11.640
on the basis of what they're
reading in these studies

02:14:11.640 --> 02:14:13.590
that you all are publishing.

02:14:13.590 --> 02:14:15.900
But also expanding on
and experimenting hippie

02:14:15.900 --> 02:14:19.723
flips and combination drugs
and ketamine and et cetera.

02:14:19.723 --> 02:14:22.140
But let's leave ketamine out
right now because it's legal.

02:14:22.140 --> 02:14:23.910
But there's that.

02:14:23.910 --> 02:14:26.280
Then there's the--

02:14:26.280 --> 02:14:30.730
I don't want to say
recreational/open market,

02:14:30.730 --> 02:14:32.090
black market.

02:14:32.090 --> 02:14:34.750
And here I want to
raise a flag to the fact

02:14:34.750 --> 02:14:37.840
that Dr. Peter Attia did
a terrific podcast on this

02:14:37.840 --> 02:14:39.790
recently in his own
podcast, The Drive.

02:14:39.790 --> 02:14:42.970
The fact that fentanyl--
lacing with fentanyl

02:14:42.970 --> 02:14:45.883
is now showing up in MDMA
and psychedelics that

02:14:45.883 --> 02:14:47.050
are purchased on the street.

02:14:47.050 --> 02:14:49.300
So serious caution
to those getting it

02:14:49.300 --> 02:14:53.221
from uncertain sources.

02:14:53.221 --> 02:14:55.720
And then you've got pharma.

02:14:55.720 --> 02:14:58.240
And then as an umbrella
for all of this,

02:14:58.240 --> 02:15:02.650
you've got the FDA and law
enforcement agencies, which

02:15:02.650 --> 02:15:04.660
currently say, this
stuff is illegal

02:15:04.660 --> 02:15:06.700
unless it's being used
in a clinical trial.

02:15:06.700 --> 02:15:09.910
Selling it or possessing
it can get you charged

02:15:09.910 --> 02:15:11.410
with a crime ranging from--

02:15:11.410 --> 02:15:13.118
I don't want to say
because I don't know.

02:15:13.118 --> 02:15:14.230
But up to felonies.

02:15:14.230 --> 02:15:15.970
Right years in prison.

02:15:15.970 --> 02:15:19.950
So can't take it
through airports,

02:15:19.950 --> 02:15:22.830
don't get caught with it,
don't buy it, don't sell it

02:15:22.830 --> 02:15:23.590
kind of thing.

02:15:23.590 --> 02:15:28.780
So where are we going from
that picture of the silos?

02:15:28.780 --> 02:15:31.270
I know things are in
clinical trials now.

02:15:31.270 --> 02:15:33.490
Most people, including
myself, are not

02:15:33.490 --> 02:15:36.340
familiar with how the different
phases relate to the proximity

02:15:36.340 --> 02:15:37.540
to legality.

02:15:37.540 --> 02:15:41.620
Could you just give us the
landscape and touch on how long

02:15:41.620 --> 02:15:43.883
you think it will
be before the people

02:15:43.883 --> 02:15:45.550
that come through
your trials could then

02:15:45.550 --> 02:15:48.160
go get a prescription
for psilocybin

02:15:48.160 --> 02:15:51.460
or potentially buy
it without the risk

02:15:51.460 --> 02:15:53.170
from a reliable
source, one would hope,

02:15:53.170 --> 02:15:56.860
but without the risk of
getting thrown in jail.

02:15:56.860 --> 02:15:59.020
I used to live in
Oakland, California.

02:15:59.020 --> 02:16:01.780
My understanding-- and please
correct me if I'm wrong, folks.

02:16:01.780 --> 02:16:04.102
Don't trust this information
and get in trouble.

02:16:04.102 --> 02:16:05.560
My understanding
is that psilocybin

02:16:05.560 --> 02:16:08.710
is decriminalized in
Oakland, but that's

02:16:08.710 --> 02:16:10.360
not the same as being legal.

02:16:10.360 --> 02:16:11.767
So what is going on out there?

02:16:11.767 --> 02:16:12.850
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Mhm.

02:16:12.850 --> 02:16:13.390
Wow.

02:16:13.390 --> 02:16:13.973
Well, so much.

02:16:13.973 --> 02:16:14.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:16:14.890 --> 02:16:16.075
I just asked 55 questions.

02:16:16.075 --> 02:16:17.283
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: I know.

02:16:17.283 --> 02:16:20.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But feel free
to answer just a subset of them

02:16:20.110 --> 02:16:20.650
if you like.

02:16:20.650 --> 02:16:21.775
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:16:21.775 --> 02:16:23.730
Well, Oakland's a funny one.

02:16:23.730 --> 02:16:25.420
I live close to Oakland.

02:16:25.420 --> 02:16:30.400
There are head shops
in Oakland that

02:16:30.400 --> 02:16:36.820
might be selling cannabis and
cannabis-related paraphernalia

02:16:36.820 --> 02:16:38.726
that are selling
mushrooms as well.

02:16:38.726 --> 02:16:39.684
Psilocybin, mushrooms--

02:16:39.684 --> 02:16:40.345
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's a fact.

02:16:40.345 --> 02:16:41.230
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: --openly.

02:16:41.230 --> 02:16:41.440
Yeah.

02:16:41.440 --> 02:16:42.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's a fact.

02:16:42.670 --> 02:16:43.464
I can verify that.

02:16:43.464 --> 02:16:43.540
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: OK.

02:16:43.540 --> 02:16:44.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
haven't purchased them,

02:16:44.830 --> 02:16:47.575
but I've gone in and checked
it out, what's going on here.

02:16:47.575 --> 02:16:47.905
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:16:47.905 --> 02:16:48.235
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:16:48.235 --> 02:16:49.360
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:16:49.360 --> 02:16:55.389
So the police aren't
going to prioritize

02:16:55.389 --> 02:17:01.969
that activity, the purchasing
of those mushrooms as a crime

02:17:01.969 --> 02:17:05.049
now in Oakland because
of the decriminalization.

02:17:05.049 --> 02:17:08.290
So those head shops shouldn't
strictly be selling.

02:17:08.290 --> 02:17:10.330
Well, they shouldn't be selling.

02:17:10.330 --> 02:17:12.639
They won't have a
license to be selling.

02:17:12.639 --> 02:17:17.650
Licenses don't exist
yet for that here.

02:17:17.650 --> 02:17:21.969
But let's see whether
they get shut down.

02:17:21.969 --> 02:17:23.530
They probably
will, I don't know.

02:17:23.530 --> 02:17:28.780
But there's a church
in Oakland sort of

02:17:28.780 --> 02:17:33.920
say that they're selling and
it's part of religious rights.

02:17:33.920 --> 02:17:40.400
They're using that church
model as a loophole, the way

02:17:40.400 --> 02:17:42.620
that Native Americans
can use peyote

02:17:42.620 --> 02:17:46.940
and they have a more
genuine case, I think.

02:17:46.940 --> 02:17:49.440
Because there is
a history there.

02:17:49.440 --> 02:17:51.440
But they're trying to
kind of piggyback on that.

02:17:51.440 --> 02:17:55.219
Anyway, that's close to
where we are right now.

02:17:55.219 --> 02:18:00.170
But federally, which is really
the major inflection point,

02:18:00.170 --> 02:18:06.110
is the FDA and the licensing
of psychedelics as medicines

02:18:06.110 --> 02:18:10.250
to be legally prescribed across
the country, across the US

02:18:10.250 --> 02:18:17.540
and beyond, that is close
because the key phase--

02:18:17.540 --> 02:18:19.639
so there are different
phases of clinical trials,

02:18:19.639 --> 02:18:23.299
and the key one to know
about is phase III.

02:18:23.299 --> 02:18:25.790
Phase III trials are
licensing trials.

02:18:25.790 --> 02:18:27.620
If they're successful,
and typically you

02:18:27.620 --> 02:18:30.309
have to do at least
two successful ones,

02:18:30.309 --> 02:18:32.799
show the results to
the regulators who

02:18:32.799 --> 02:18:35.959
are the FDA, the medicine
regulators, and say,

02:18:35.959 --> 02:18:38.830
is this good enough now for
you to give me a license

02:18:38.830 --> 02:18:43.330
so that I can sell and provide
this medicine that we've

02:18:43.330 --> 02:18:45.370
demonstrated is a medicine.

02:18:45.370 --> 02:18:49.360
So that work has been
done with MDMA therapy

02:18:49.360 --> 02:18:51.610
for post-traumatic
stress disorder.

02:18:51.610 --> 02:18:55.900
MAPS have led that work and
done two phase III trials.

02:18:55.900 --> 02:18:57.910
I think they've already
publicly announced

02:18:57.910 --> 02:19:00.309
that the second
trial had results

02:19:00.309 --> 02:19:01.780
consistent with the first.

02:19:01.780 --> 02:19:03.430
We know the results
of the first,

02:19:03.430 --> 02:19:06.610
because they're published and
they were remarkably good.

02:19:06.610 --> 02:19:09.114
Something like 67%
remission rates.

02:19:09.114 --> 02:19:10.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And long term.

02:19:10.450 --> 02:19:12.242
My understanding is
some of those remission

02:19:12.242 --> 02:19:16.180
rates for trauma were years,
which is different than what

02:19:16.180 --> 02:19:17.620
you're describing
for psilocybin,

02:19:17.620 --> 02:19:19.245
where people might
need ongoing dosing.

02:19:19.245 --> 02:19:20.370
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:19:20.370 --> 02:19:20.920
That's true.

02:19:20.920 --> 02:19:21.580
Yeah.

02:19:21.580 --> 02:19:21.910
Yeah.

02:19:21.910 --> 02:19:23.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
But of course, just

02:19:23.410 --> 02:19:24.549
for trauma in those trials.

02:19:24.549 --> 02:19:26.947
My understanding is those
MDMA trials were not

02:19:26.947 --> 02:19:27.864
focused on depression.

02:19:27.864 --> 02:19:28.780
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yes.

02:19:28.780 --> 02:19:29.410
Yes.

02:19:29.410 --> 02:19:32.620
Focused on the trauma.

02:19:32.620 --> 02:19:36.250
So that's something,
because that data

02:19:36.250 --> 02:19:39.889
is being filed now, to my
knowledge, like as we speak.

02:19:39.889 --> 02:19:45.129
And they're anticipating
a decision maybe this year

02:19:45.129 --> 02:19:47.889
with rollout happening
as early as next year.

02:19:47.889 --> 02:19:49.573
I mean, that's
best case, I think.

02:19:49.573 --> 02:19:52.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Could I ask you
when you, when you say rollout,

02:19:52.240 --> 02:19:55.480
and it's the appropriate
term for MDMA, because

02:19:55.480 --> 02:19:57.940
of so-called rolling.

02:19:57.940 --> 02:19:59.950
About 20% of my
audience, maybe 50

02:19:59.950 --> 02:20:04.120
will understand that not
funny joke that I made.

02:20:04.120 --> 02:20:07.150
Who's going to roll it out?

02:20:07.150 --> 02:20:11.170
Where would one get the
clean source of MDMA,

02:20:11.170 --> 02:20:16.450
meaning not laced with fentanyl,
not laced with methamphetamine,

02:20:16.450 --> 02:20:18.670
not undergone any
chemical conversion

02:20:18.670 --> 02:20:21.190
to some other drug which can
happen with extended shelf

02:20:21.190 --> 02:20:22.510
life, et cetera.

02:20:22.510 --> 02:20:26.710
Are people going to go to
their psychiatrist to get MDMA?

02:20:26.710 --> 02:20:29.110
And who's going to
be providing it?

02:20:29.110 --> 02:20:32.770
Is it going to be
some big major pharma?

02:20:32.770 --> 02:20:34.537
This seems like a
serious set of issues.

02:20:34.537 --> 02:20:35.620
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Mhm.

02:20:35.620 --> 02:20:36.370
It is.

02:20:36.370 --> 02:20:37.970
And I don't have
all the answers.

02:20:37.970 --> 02:20:41.230
I do know that MAPS would be
providing, because they've

02:20:41.230 --> 02:20:45.400
done the work and they have
set themselves up in a sense

02:20:45.400 --> 02:20:50.890
to potentially
become the provider,

02:20:50.890 --> 02:20:53.920
whether as a pharma company,
which is the big question

02:20:53.920 --> 02:20:55.960
they're wrestling
with at the moment.

02:20:55.960 --> 02:21:00.190
It's very expensive to
become a pharma company.

02:21:00.190 --> 02:21:02.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And yet
they probably deserve

02:21:02.230 --> 02:21:04.240
to make the choice,
because they've

02:21:04.240 --> 02:21:07.750
put in so many years of hard
work when all of this stuff

02:21:07.750 --> 02:21:11.410
was considered like
raver culture party drug,

02:21:11.410 --> 02:21:15.662
they were the ones that spotted
the therapeutic potential.

02:21:15.662 --> 02:21:17.870
I mean, we knew there was
therapeutic potential based

02:21:17.870 --> 02:21:20.870
on work going back many
decades, but points to them,

02:21:20.870 --> 02:21:23.695
and I think in my opinion,
they should have the agency

02:21:23.695 --> 02:21:24.695
to make those decisions.

02:21:24.695 --> 02:21:25.820
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:21:25.820 --> 02:21:28.500
And it's such a remarkable
thing that's been achieved.

02:21:28.500 --> 02:21:33.630
And I think they've done it
all on philanthropic donations.

02:21:33.630 --> 02:21:35.420
I think so.

02:21:35.420 --> 02:21:36.140
Yeah.

02:21:36.140 --> 02:21:40.890
So there is this
big question mark.

02:21:40.890 --> 02:21:45.470
And the FDA are also
asking questions about,

02:21:45.470 --> 02:21:48.590
to your question,
who can provide this,

02:21:48.590 --> 02:21:52.950
because in the phase III
work and up until this point,

02:21:52.950 --> 02:21:56.120
there's been a MAPS training,
a MAPS therapist training.

02:21:56.120 --> 02:22:01.010
And you have to do this
formal training in order

02:22:01.010 --> 02:22:03.870
to be a practitioner
within the trials.

02:22:03.870 --> 02:22:05.780
But now, there's a
question from the FDA

02:22:05.780 --> 02:22:09.950
whether that MAPS training
can be the training

02:22:09.950 --> 02:22:14.600
that a clinician has to
have to now be a provider.

02:22:14.600 --> 02:22:19.550
And when I say rollout,
it's like offering this

02:22:19.550 --> 02:22:21.480
as a service, essentially.

02:22:21.480 --> 02:22:23.632
And so where would the
referral come from?

02:22:23.632 --> 02:22:24.590
That's a good question.

02:22:24.590 --> 02:22:26.750
That I'm not 100% on the answer.

02:22:26.750 --> 02:22:30.830
Whether it would have to come
from a psychiatrist or whether

02:22:30.830 --> 02:22:33.230
someone's--

02:22:33.230 --> 02:22:37.490
sort of general physician
could do that referral.

02:22:37.490 --> 02:22:40.340
But they will be going
to a provider who

02:22:40.340 --> 02:22:45.230
is licensed and certified and
will have done some training.

02:22:45.230 --> 02:22:47.840
And there will be
a consensus on what

02:22:47.840 --> 02:22:50.780
constitutes good enough
training to provide.

02:22:50.780 --> 02:22:53.120
There will also be
some stipulations

02:22:53.120 --> 02:22:56.120
on the basic underlying
professionalism

02:22:56.120 --> 02:22:59.120
of the clinician who provides.

02:22:59.120 --> 02:23:03.050
So I imagine, they'll have to
be a mental health professional.

02:23:03.050 --> 02:23:05.870
I don't think they would have to
necessarily be a psychiatrist,

02:23:05.870 --> 02:23:08.510
they could be, I think,
a clinical psychologist.

02:23:08.510 --> 02:23:11.780
For all the dosings, I
think without question,

02:23:11.780 --> 02:23:15.320
there would have to be a
physician present or at least

02:23:15.320 --> 02:23:18.233
within ready access in
case of an emergency.

02:23:18.233 --> 02:23:19.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, especially,

02:23:19.650 --> 02:23:22.565
with MDMA because of the
propensity for cardiac issues.

02:23:22.565 --> 02:23:23.690
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Yeah.

02:23:23.690 --> 02:23:25.982
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Because of
the amphetamine properties.

02:23:25.982 --> 02:23:30.570
And where is psilocybin in
terms of the phase trials?

02:23:30.570 --> 02:23:32.220
Is it in phase II, phase III?

02:23:32.220 --> 02:23:34.050
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
It's in phase III.

02:23:34.050 --> 02:23:36.270
There's psilocybin
therapy work being

02:23:36.270 --> 02:23:39.570
done for treatment
resistant depression

02:23:39.570 --> 02:23:41.880
by a company called Compass.

02:23:41.880 --> 02:23:45.900
Those trials which
are always multi-site.

02:23:45.900 --> 02:23:50.700
So there's always a bunch
of teams or labs in a sense.

02:23:50.700 --> 02:23:53.220
Geographically spread
out that are each

02:23:53.220 --> 02:23:56.460
contributing to data that
then gets matched together

02:23:56.460 --> 02:24:00.728
and is then submitted as part
of the phase III trial results.

02:24:00.728 --> 02:24:02.520
So that's happening
with Compass right now.

02:24:02.520 --> 02:24:06.030
It's psilocybin therapy for
treatment-resistant depression.

02:24:06.030 --> 02:24:09.450
Those trials have
just started and I

02:24:09.450 --> 02:24:11.130
think the earliest
estimate that I

02:24:11.130 --> 02:24:14.760
heard in a journalistic
article was

02:24:14.760 --> 02:24:16.620
because I don't think
Compass would say

02:24:16.620 --> 02:24:20.820
or they wouldn't say
publicly something like 2026.

02:24:20.820 --> 02:24:22.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: '26.

02:24:22.110 --> 02:24:22.680
Wow.

02:24:22.680 --> 02:24:24.508
So MDMA is ahead of psilocybin.

02:24:24.508 --> 02:24:25.800
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Oh, yeah.

02:24:25.800 --> 02:24:26.300
Yeah.

02:24:26.300 --> 02:24:28.230
It's quite a few years ahead.

02:24:28.230 --> 02:24:31.710
And it's more of,
not a certainty

02:24:31.710 --> 02:24:35.400
but it's very, very
strong position with MDMA.

02:24:35.400 --> 02:24:37.680
Whereas the work's
only just begun

02:24:37.680 --> 02:24:40.480
with psilocybin in terms
of the phase III trials.

02:24:40.480 --> 02:24:42.210
But then you have
this other situation

02:24:42.210 --> 02:24:45.300
of however many psychedelic
research centers

02:24:45.300 --> 02:24:52.650
there are now across the globe,
we had the first one in London

02:24:52.650 --> 02:24:54.240
in 2019.

02:24:54.240 --> 02:24:57.000
The first one in
2019, it's 2023 now,

02:24:57.000 --> 02:24:58.500
and I don't know
how many there are.

02:24:58.500 --> 02:25:04.080
But so much has happened in
such a small space of time.

02:25:04.080 --> 02:25:04.650
Yes.

02:25:04.650 --> 02:25:07.320
But all these
different indications

02:25:07.320 --> 02:25:09.330
I've been able to tell
you about-- anorexia

02:25:09.330 --> 02:25:11.280
and fibromyalgia syndrome.

02:25:11.280 --> 02:25:15.480
Trying to do a trial
with a colleague of mine

02:25:15.480 --> 02:25:19.150
at UCSF in methamphetamine
use disorder.

02:25:19.150 --> 02:25:22.800
He's got a trial going
on in Parkinson's disease

02:25:22.800 --> 02:25:26.640
and chronic lower back
pain and bipolar disorder.

02:25:26.640 --> 02:25:28.350
I mean, there's
so much going on.

02:25:28.350 --> 02:25:29.520
OCD.

02:25:29.520 --> 02:25:34.050
Almost the full gamut of
psychiatric disorders.

02:25:34.050 --> 02:25:38.320
Not schizophrenia, to my
knowledge, are being looked at.

02:25:38.320 --> 02:25:44.620
So there's so much
groundswell of activity.

02:25:44.620 --> 02:25:47.790
And I think these small
investigator-led studies,

02:25:47.790 --> 02:25:51.240
typically, they're small
because trials are expensive,

02:25:51.240 --> 02:25:53.680
are going to be reporting
positive results.

02:25:53.680 --> 02:25:54.820
I know what we're seeing.

02:25:54.820 --> 02:25:57.730
And it will be four--

02:25:57.730 --> 02:26:01.140
let's see now, at
least four trials all

02:26:01.140 --> 02:26:04.740
with really positive results
in very difficult-to-treat

02:26:04.740 --> 02:26:05.340
disorders.

02:26:05.340 --> 02:26:06.330
And that's just us.

02:26:06.330 --> 02:26:08.490
And I know there's
so much elsewhere.

02:26:08.490 --> 02:26:10.530
Addiction disorders as well.

02:26:10.530 --> 02:26:14.880
Mike Johnson's work,
obviously, Michael Bogenschutz.

02:26:14.880 --> 02:26:17.580
So all this compelling
groundswell,

02:26:17.580 --> 02:26:19.050
it's really something.

02:26:19.050 --> 02:26:22.100
And yet the system
to really make

02:26:22.100 --> 02:26:25.550
a big breakthrough in terms of
licensing is, of course, slow.

02:26:25.550 --> 02:26:34.073
And that can frustrate people,
but it has to be done properly.

02:26:34.073 --> 02:26:34.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:26:34.990 --> 02:26:39.400
Else we revert back to
what happened in the '70s

02:26:39.400 --> 02:26:43.300
where there was a lot of
interest in psychedelics.

02:26:43.300 --> 02:26:44.320
It's interesting to me.

02:26:44.320 --> 02:26:47.680
There was a close
juxtaposition of meditation

02:26:47.680 --> 02:26:52.810
and behavioral approaches to
self-directed state change

02:26:52.810 --> 02:26:54.040
and psychedelics.

02:26:54.040 --> 02:26:56.290
Meditation made it
through the hatch.

02:26:56.290 --> 02:26:57.790
I mean, there were
some years where

02:26:57.790 --> 02:27:02.530
it was considered counterculture
woo magic carpet weirdo

02:27:02.530 --> 02:27:05.350
stuff by Western science.

02:27:05.350 --> 02:27:07.330
But now, I mean, there are--

02:27:07.330 --> 02:27:09.100
probably tens of
thousands is not

02:27:09.100 --> 02:27:11.770
an overstatement of
quality studies exploring

02:27:11.770 --> 02:27:15.130
how meditation can provide
advantages for the mind

02:27:15.130 --> 02:27:17.170
and even for mental health.

02:27:17.170 --> 02:27:20.140
And psychedelics
are now catching up,

02:27:20.140 --> 02:27:22.180
but they used to
be close cousins

02:27:22.180 --> 02:27:25.150
in the cultural framework.

02:27:25.150 --> 02:27:29.200
But the problem was,
I think, psychedelics

02:27:29.200 --> 02:27:32.210
were viewed as
making people crazy.

02:27:32.210 --> 02:27:35.282
And university professors
lost their jobs

02:27:35.282 --> 02:27:36.740
for having discussions
like the one

02:27:36.740 --> 02:27:39.080
that you and I are
having right now.

02:27:39.080 --> 02:27:42.950
And some people went to
jail, but mostly people

02:27:42.950 --> 02:27:44.840
either left academic
institutions

02:27:44.840 --> 02:27:48.680
or lost their jobs, whereas
now these are some of--

02:27:48.680 --> 02:27:50.582
these studies of the
sort that you are doing

02:27:50.582 --> 02:27:52.790
and that are taking place
at Stanford and Hopkins and

02:27:52.790 --> 02:27:56.450
elsewhere are some of the
greatest magnetic pull

02:27:56.450 --> 02:27:58.460
for philanthropy
for universities.

02:27:58.460 --> 02:28:02.300
Donors are very
interested in supporting

02:28:02.300 --> 02:28:05.930
these sorts of studies, because
they and their family members

02:28:05.930 --> 02:28:09.500
and people they know suffer from
psychiatric illness for which

02:28:09.500 --> 02:28:14.270
the current big pharma
approaches simply have not

02:28:14.270 --> 02:28:14.900
worked.

02:28:14.900 --> 02:28:17.720
So it's interesting
to me that what once

02:28:17.720 --> 02:28:22.760
was seen as poison
is now being viewed

02:28:22.760 --> 02:28:26.120
as a potential therapeutic.

02:28:26.120 --> 02:28:30.110
It's not just
interesting, hopefully,

02:28:30.110 --> 02:28:33.440
it speaks to the evolution
of the human species.

02:28:33.440 --> 02:28:36.230
People seem to be coming more
open-minded about becoming

02:28:36.230 --> 02:28:37.163
more open-minded.

02:28:37.163 --> 02:28:38.330
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Right.

02:28:38.330 --> 02:28:38.830
Yeah.

02:28:38.830 --> 02:28:39.980
That's a good one.

02:28:39.980 --> 02:28:41.690
Yeah.

02:28:41.690 --> 02:28:46.430
And yet-- yeah.

02:28:46.430 --> 02:28:50.090
There's so much that's
happening so fast.

02:28:50.090 --> 02:28:56.060
And there are elements of--
it's complexifying the space.

02:28:56.060 --> 02:28:59.000
There is critique, there's
been some bad practice

02:28:59.000 --> 02:29:01.760
in psychedelic therapy,
boundary crossing issues

02:29:01.760 --> 02:29:03.637
that have caused some scandals.

02:29:03.637 --> 02:29:04.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's too bad.

02:29:04.970 --> 02:29:05.780
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS: Isn't it?

02:29:05.780 --> 02:29:06.697
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:29:06.697 --> 02:29:08.570
Well, I think too the
gene therapy, right?

02:29:08.570 --> 02:29:10.760
It just takes one bad incident.

02:29:10.760 --> 02:29:14.180
Gene therapy was on a fast
track three decades ago

02:29:14.180 --> 02:29:18.740
and then sadly, a child died
in a gene therapy trial.

02:29:18.740 --> 02:29:22.460
And it's like shut down
gene therapy practically

02:29:22.460 --> 02:29:24.620
for half a decade
and then it slowly

02:29:24.620 --> 02:29:26.758
started ratcheting up again.

02:29:26.758 --> 02:29:28.550
Gene therapy broadly
defined, and now we're

02:29:28.550 --> 02:29:31.268
in the age of potential
directed gene therapy using

02:29:31.268 --> 02:29:32.810
CRISPR and things
of that sort, which

02:29:32.810 --> 02:29:35.750
makes some people cringe and
other people very excited.

02:29:35.750 --> 02:29:37.550
If you have Huntington's
in your family,

02:29:37.550 --> 02:29:40.130
CRISPR is like the most
exciting technology ever,

02:29:40.130 --> 02:29:43.370
because you could potentially
eliminate it from your family

02:29:43.370 --> 02:29:45.710
line going forward, of course.

02:29:45.710 --> 02:29:47.930
ROBIN CARHART-HARRIS:
So I just really hope

02:29:47.930 --> 02:29:51.470
that we can be balanced
as this all plays out.

02:29:51.470 --> 02:29:54.650
Because it could go similar
way given the stigma, given

02:29:54.650 --> 02:29:59.720
the history that people would be
very twitchy with some isolated

02:29:59.720 --> 02:30:01.460
incidents.

02:30:01.460 --> 02:30:09.710
And overgeneralize them,
perhaps, in a sense,

02:30:09.710 --> 02:30:11.420
shining a light
on them, I think,

02:30:11.420 --> 02:30:14.270
is important that
has happened recently

02:30:14.270 --> 02:30:17.030
is important, because
it really drills home

02:30:17.030 --> 02:30:20.375
how important it is that
this work be done right

02:30:20.375 --> 02:30:25.640
and what the necessary
safeguards and standards should

02:30:25.640 --> 02:30:28.290
be.

02:30:28.290 --> 02:30:28.790
Yeah.

02:30:28.790 --> 02:30:33.970
And it won't be an
easy road forwards.

02:30:33.970 --> 02:30:38.170
But let's hope-- we've got
to hope that it succeeds,

02:30:38.170 --> 02:30:41.630
because current
treatments-- people talk

02:30:41.630 --> 02:30:44.870
about the mental health
crisis and to your point

02:30:44.870 --> 02:30:47.360
earlier about
anorexia rates, it's

02:30:47.360 --> 02:30:49.010
not always actually
the case when

02:30:49.010 --> 02:30:51.920
you look at the epidemiology.

02:30:51.920 --> 02:30:53.720
When you look at
the data that you

02:30:53.720 --> 02:30:58.070
see a big inflection
in diagnoses or cases

02:30:58.070 --> 02:30:59.930
of psychiatric illness.

02:30:59.930 --> 02:31:01.190
I would say, it's more--

02:31:01.190 --> 02:31:02.720
the treatments haven't moved.

02:31:02.720 --> 02:31:04.070
They haven't really progressed.

02:31:04.070 --> 02:31:08.015
They haven't got any better
since the 1950s more or less.

02:31:08.015 --> 02:31:11.880
And new drugs have
been more of the same.

02:31:11.880 --> 02:31:14.510
So there haven't been
any paradigm shifts.

02:31:14.510 --> 02:31:16.850
And that's why I get
a little impassioned

02:31:16.850 --> 02:31:20.120
when I talk about psychedelic
therapy and that point

02:31:20.120 --> 02:31:21.720
that this is
something different.

02:31:21.720 --> 02:31:24.590
It's not a drug every day.

02:31:24.590 --> 02:31:28.085
That system, it's
not cutting it.

02:31:28.085 --> 02:31:32.150
Do we really want to
keep on with that system?

02:31:32.150 --> 02:31:33.665
Sure.

02:31:33.665 --> 02:31:35.840
Not everyone will want to trip.

02:31:35.840 --> 02:31:37.940
And that will
terrify some people

02:31:37.940 --> 02:31:40.410
so much that they'll
just want to be

02:31:40.410 --> 02:31:44.070
on their Lexapro or a
non-psychedelic, psychedelic,

02:31:44.070 --> 02:31:44.610
or whatever.

02:31:44.610 --> 02:31:47.880
And of course, it should be
allowed to have those options.

02:31:47.880 --> 02:31:48.780
Of course.

02:31:48.780 --> 02:31:51.520
And the more
options, the better.

02:31:51.520 --> 02:31:57.450
But I think there is great
value in really understanding

02:31:57.450 --> 02:32:00.360
what psychedelic therapy is.

02:32:00.360 --> 02:32:02.610
And I think when
you do, you realize

02:32:02.610 --> 02:32:07.360
that it is a major paradigm
challenge on many levels.

02:32:07.360 --> 02:32:12.150
And the fact that it's
different might be its greatest

02:32:12.150 --> 02:32:13.860
appeal at the moment, I think.

02:32:13.860 --> 02:32:15.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, I am certainly

02:32:15.980 --> 02:32:19.850
grateful for your passion for
the potential for psychedelics

02:32:19.850 --> 02:32:22.280
to be added to the array
of potential treatments.

02:32:22.280 --> 02:32:26.510
And I really also appreciate
how much you put it in there

02:32:26.510 --> 02:32:28.340
alongside the other treatments.

02:32:28.340 --> 02:32:30.350
Maybe even in combination
with other treatments

02:32:30.350 --> 02:32:32.267
as opposed to saying,
this is the thing that's

02:32:32.267 --> 02:32:34.070
going to cure everything.

02:32:34.070 --> 02:32:36.830
And yet the passion
that you have

02:32:36.830 --> 02:32:40.340
for this potential paradigm
shift, the one that really

02:32:40.340 --> 02:32:44.150
appears to be happening at
the level of clinical data now

02:32:44.150 --> 02:32:46.500
is so important.

02:32:46.500 --> 02:32:49.910
So I want to extend
a voice of gratitude

02:32:49.910 --> 02:32:53.180
for that and for the
work that you're doing.

02:32:53.180 --> 02:32:56.332
I mean, I've been
outside of this field,

02:32:56.332 --> 02:32:58.790
but as a neuroscientist, I've
been paying careful attention

02:32:58.790 --> 02:33:03.470
to it really for the last
five, seven years or so.

02:33:03.470 --> 02:33:05.870
And it's abundantly
clear that it

02:33:05.870 --> 02:33:08.810
is a small group of individuals
who are really thinking

02:33:08.810 --> 02:33:11.060
in terms of how the
system works now

02:33:11.060 --> 02:33:13.250
and what needs to be
done in order to change

02:33:13.250 --> 02:33:15.930
the system for the
better like yourself

02:33:15.930 --> 02:33:20.850
that are really the driving
force behind this new movement

02:33:20.850 --> 02:33:21.990
or paradigm shift.

02:33:21.990 --> 02:33:25.110
That without question,
is going to lead

02:33:25.110 --> 02:33:28.390
to improvements in mental health
and physical health outcomes.

02:33:28.390 --> 02:33:31.000
So I just want to say
thank you for that.

02:33:31.000 --> 02:33:34.620
Also thank you so much for
joining us today to share

02:33:34.620 --> 02:33:38.700
this immense knowledge set about
the history of psychedelics.

02:33:38.700 --> 02:33:41.220
What they are, what they aren't.

02:33:41.220 --> 02:33:44.340
Their clinical applications
as seen in your laboratory

02:33:44.340 --> 02:33:46.260
and other laboratories.

02:33:46.260 --> 02:33:47.760
I'm sure people
already notice this,

02:33:47.760 --> 02:33:50.610
but you're incredibly generous
in terms of attribution

02:33:50.610 --> 02:33:52.357
and also in your
caution about explaining

02:33:52.357 --> 02:33:53.940
how some of the
results in particular,

02:33:53.940 --> 02:33:57.750
on anorexia, fibromyalgia, are
perhaps preliminary but very

02:33:57.750 --> 02:33:59.190
exciting.

02:33:59.190 --> 02:34:00.840
They're not published
yet, anyway.

02:34:00.840 --> 02:34:02.550
We wouldn't call
them preliminary.

02:34:02.550 --> 02:34:05.010
And also for touching
on mechanism.

02:34:05.010 --> 02:34:07.920
That is not just about people
feel better, but pointed

02:34:07.920 --> 02:34:10.170
to some potential
underlying mechanisms

02:34:10.170 --> 02:34:14.710
in terms of connectivity
changes and on and on.

02:34:14.710 --> 02:34:17.342
So thank you so much
for your time today.

02:34:17.342 --> 02:34:19.050
Thank you for the work
that you're doing.

02:34:19.050 --> 02:34:21.880
And thank you for the work
that is sure to continue.

02:34:21.880 --> 02:34:25.500
We will provide links to
studies in your laboratory,

02:34:25.500 --> 02:34:27.630
links to your laboratory
so people can learn more

02:34:27.630 --> 02:34:30.540
and support in the ways that
they deem appropriate for them.

02:34:30.540 --> 02:34:33.960
But just thank you,
thank you, thank you.

02:34:33.960 --> 02:34:35.627
Such important work
you're doing, Robin.

